//****************************************************************************
// $Module type: Class
// $Module name: cModAnalyzer
// $Author     : Nils Svedmyr, RDC Tools International, <mailto:support@rdctools.com>
// Web-site    : http://www.rdctools.com
//
// Description : Modernization Analyzer - the "brain" that maps a DataFlex compiler
//               MESSAGE (a warning OR an error: number + text) to a fix RECIPE: a
//               category (Auto-fix / Suggest / Explain / Unknown), a one-line hint,
//               and the refactor function that addresses it (if any).
//
//               The recipe set is DATA-DRIVEN - it lives in the "ModAnalyzer" table
//               (created by DUF_MultipleTables, filelist #12). BuildDefaultRecipes
//               holds the built-in defaults; RefreshRecipeTable writes them to the table on
//               first use (idempotent: a non-empty table keeps the user's edits), and
//               LoadRecipes caches the table in memory. ClassifyMessage then matches a
//               message against the cache - ClassifyFromRecipes is the pure matcher: a
//               row whose MatchText appears in the message text beats a generic (blank
//               MatchText) row for the same number.
//
//               Grounded in real DF19->DF21+ migration output, where the WARNING family
//               (4531 string commands, 4532 ZeroString, 4533 dbList/dbGrid, 4534
//               ToOEM/ToANSI, 4538 indicators, ...) dwarfs the error family and mostly
//               maps straight onto refactor functions we already ship.
//
// This code is distributed in the hope that it will be useful, but WITHOUT ANY WARRANTY;
// without even the implied warranty of MERCHANTABILITY or FITNESS for a PARTICULAR PURPOSE.
// This is free software; you can redistribute it and/or modify it under the terms of the
// GNU Lesser General Public License - see the "GNU Lesser General Public License.txt"
// in the help folder for more details.
//****************************************************************************
Use cApplication.pkg
Use cRefactorFuncLib.pkg
Use cRDCAutoCreateNewID.pkg
Use CompileErrorParser.pkg    // tCompileError + ReadCompilerMessages (parse the .prn)
Use RefactorFileIO.pkg        // rfOpenInput / rfOpenOutput / Read_Block (recipe JSON cache I/O)
Use cEncodingHelpers.pkg      // rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArray - the plan source-guards the warned line
Use cDeepScanner.pkg          // Deep Scan rules double as recipes (_AddDeepScanRecipes below)
Use cShadowedPackageFiles.pkg // WorkspaceSourceFiles - the sweep MissingIncludeHint looks a missing include up in
Use cLibraryInventory.pkg     // giLibraryInventoryClass - the inventory class that can read this program's workspace files
Use cJsonObject.pkg           // recipe JSON import/export (DataTypeToJson / JsonToDataType)
Use cPlanNoOps.pkg            // PlanNoOpFunctionsIn - the functions the last plans ran on a file without changing it
Use RefactorConstants.h.pkg

Open ModAnalyzer

// Version of the built-in recipe set. BUMP THIS whenever BuildDefaultRecipes changes, so
// RefreshRecipeTable refreshes already-seeded tables on the next run.
// v23: dbList 4537 recipe match-text corrected DBGRID -> DBLIST (the compiler emits the actual
//      class name - "Obsolete class: DBLIST" for a dbList - so the old "class: DBGRID" never matched
//      and every dbList object fell through to the generic 4537 Explain).
// v24: 4538 "Obsolete command: Indicator" ownership moved to the new ChangeIndicatorToBoolean (the
//      DECLARATION refactor); removed the mis-applied 4538 tag from ChangeLegacyIndicators /
//      ChangeSquareBracketsIndicators (they fix [Found]/Indicate USAGE, which emits no 4538).
// v25: new ChangeUppercaseCommandToFunction / ChangeLowercaseCommandToFunction (4531 | command:
//      Uppercase / Lowercase) - "Uppercase sX to sY" -> "Move (Uppercase(sX)) to sY".
// v26: 4538 is a SHARED message with THREE text variants - "Indicate. Use Move command", "Indicator.
//      Use Boolean", and "Obsolete technique...without parentheses" ([Found] square-bracket usage). v24
//      wrongly assumed usage emits no 4538 and stripped the tags, so [Found]/Indicate lines fell to the
//      generic ChangeIndicatorToBoolean and failed its source guard ("Explain - not fixable on this line").
//      Now match-text routes each: "Use Boolean" -> ChangeIndicatorToBoolean; "Use Move command" +
//      "without parentheses" -> ChangeLegacyIndicators; "without parentheses" -> ChangeSquareBracketsIndicators.
// v27: new CommentOutArgumentSizeCommands - comments out the obsolete Get_Argument_Size /
//      Set_Argument_Size legacy string-buffer-sizing commands (no-ops under modern dynamic strings).
//      4533 | Get_Argument_Size / Set_Argument_Size | Suggest (Review - the rare inline-If form drops
//      the guarding If when the whole line is commented, so it's worth an eyeball).
// v28: corrected that match-text. The compiler emits "SET_ARGUMENT_SIZE. It can be removed." /
//      "GET_ARGUMENT_SIZE. It can be removed." (NOT the "Obsolete command: X" phrasing the other 4533
//      recipes use), so the recipe keys on the bare command name (4533 | Set_Argument_Size /
//      Get_Argument_Size), not "command: ...", which never matched -> the rows fell to generic Explain.
// v29: 4345 is now a VARIANT-routed error (like 4538). The compiler emits "Type check error SELECT, MUST
//      BE DEFINED" when the predefined SELECT macro indicator is used bare without OldFMACCommands.pkg
//      (e.g. after ChangeLegacyIndicators / ChangeSquareBracketsIndicators bare-ify [SELECT], or in legacy
//      source that lost the Use line). A function-backed meta-tag recipe on DefineSelectMacro
//      (4345 | SELECT, MUST BE DEFINED) routes that variant to the fix; the FMAC reserved-name variant
//      (iEntry/iExit/iValidate) still falls to the function-less generic 4345 (Explain - manual rename).
// v30: DefineSelectMacro now DEFINES the SELECT macro (an IFNDEF-guarded #Replace SELECT shim) instead
//      of adding 'Use OldFMACCommands.pkg' - the Use line only made the REPORT macro available and did NOT
//      define bare SELECT on its own (Nils 2026-06-20). The recipe's hint (its {Description} first line)
//      changed, so the cached recipe table + ModAnalyzerRecipes.json must reseed. Recipe wiring unchanged.
// v32: new function-backed recipe RefactorListToCJGrid (4537 | class: LIST) - converts a legacy List
//      (single-column item list) to cCJGrid, joining the Grid / DbGrid / DbList -> CJ converters. A new
//      {ModRecipe} stays dormant until this bump reseeds the recipe table + ModAnalyzerRecipes.json.
// v33: removed the hardcoded "4537 | class: LIST" Explain-only recipe that SHADOWED the v32 meta-tag
//      auto-fix. ClassifyFromRecipes keeps the FIRST specific match; the hardcoded function-less Explain
//      (added in BuildDefaultRecipes, before the appended meta-tag recipes) always won, so the List
//      warning showed as Explain/no-fix. With it gone, RefactorListToCJGrid's meta-tag is the only
//      specific 4537|LIST recipe and the warning classifies as Review (eModSuggest - the function has
//      an {EnumList}, like the Grid/DbGrid/DbList converters). Needs this bump to reseed.
// v34: DefineSelectMacro - renamed from AddUseOldFMACCommands (the old name described the obsolete
//      'Use OldFMACCommands.pkg' behaviour; it now DEFINES the SELECT macro). The 4345 | SELECT meta-tag
//      recipe's function name changed, so the recipe table + ModAnalyzerRecipes.json must reseed.
// v35: 4345 hint accuracy. The empty-match generic hard-asserted the (rare, LegacyApp-ABSENT) reserved
//      FMAC-name cause for EVERY non-SELECT 4345, mislabelling the real population - "#$, ARGUMENT
//      REQUIRED" (33x), "|SI../|SV.. INVALID LITERAL", "NAME, MUST BE DEFINED" - which are cascades off an
//      undefined symbol / legacy Type member (Type->Struct) on the SAME line (e.g. GetBuff ... tRECT.left).
//      Generic broadened to an accurate catch-all; added text-routed "ARGUMENT REQUIRED" + "INVALID LITERAL"
//      cause-pointing variants; the 4328 hint now names the Type-member case. Needs this bump to reseed.
// v36: three new {ModRecipe} tags wired (LegacyApp grid walkthrough): RemoveLocalKeyWord (4533 | command: Local -
//      it DOES warn "Obsolete command: Local", this note's backlog wrongly filed it "no-warning"), plus two new
//      obsolete-command modernizers ChangeSystemToAbort (4533 | command: System) and
//      ChangeExternalFunction32ToExternalFunction (4533 | command: External_Function32). The meta-tag recipes
//      stay dormant until this bump reseeds the recipe table + ModAnalyzerRecipes.json.
// v37: ChangeDefineAutoIncrement (4533 | DEFINE_AUTO_INCREMENT - the warning reads "Obsolete command
//      DEFINE_AUTO_INCREMENT" with NO colon, so the match-text is the bare command name). Converts the DD
//      command Define_Auto_Increment <src> To <dest> to Set Field_Auto_Increment Field <dest> File_Field <src>.
// v38: "indicators in If statements" variant wired to ChangeLegacyIndicators + ChangeSquareBracketsIndicators
//      under BOTH 4535 and 4538. The warning "Do not use indicators in If statements, use expression if (var)"
//      (fired by e.g. `if [gibDebugMode]` / `If Not [Found] Begin`) had no matching recipe, so it fell through
//      to Unknown even though the bracket converters fix `if [X]`. Note: `if not [X]` maps to Review but the
//      converters skip the not-before-bracket form (mangle guard), so those recur as Review until handled.
// v39: "indicators in If statements" (4535/4538) promoted from Review to AUTO-FIX. ChangeLegacyIndicators now
//      ALSO handles `if not [X]` (normalizes `not [X]` -> `[~X]`), so `If [X]` AND `If Not [X]` both auto-apply.
//      A VerifyAutoFix dry-run demotes the cases the converter can't touch (SELECT / non-refactorable, which it
//      skips) to Explain, so no phantom auto-fix. Grid picks ChangeLegacyIndicators (first specific match).
// v40: raw compiler errors 54 "existing class name" (undefined class) and 52 "operator" (malformed expression,
//      e.g. '()' on a Get) now carry plain-language Explain hints so they read as "fix in source" instead of
//      "No Refactoring Function yet - review manually" (they are real errors, not unhandled refactors).
// v41: two new Auto-fix functions wired: ChangeObsoleteEditControls (4537 | class: dbEdit -> cDbTextEdit,
//      class: Edit -> cTextEdit, a pure 'is a' class swap) and ChangeAsciiCommandToFunction (4531 | command:
//      Ascii -> 'Move (Ascii(<arg>)) to <dest>'). Plus the multi-line ';'-continuation walk-back
//      (GuardLineForStatement) - no recipe change, but reseeding keeps the table fresh.
// v42: CommentOutSetDateAttribute wired (4533 | command: Set_Date_Attribute | Suggest) - comments out the
//      obsolete Y2K/epoch Set_Date_Attribute command (sysdate4_State / Date4_State / epoch_value are no-ops
//      in modern DataFlex). Reversible comment-out (Review), not a mechanical Set_Attribute swap.
// v43: 4533 Begin_Row rows are now ACTIONABLE (were function-less Explain parroting the compiler text, so the
//      user could never discover the grid converters - Nils' dbList0 catch): "4533 | Begin_Row | Suggest" wired
//      to BOTH RefactorDbGridTocDbCJGrid and RefactorDbListTocDbCJGridPromptList; VerifyBeginRow (grid + plan)
//      resolves the ENCLOSING "Object ... is a <class>", keeps the right-family converter, and for a subclass
//      the pattern misses (dbList0) the hint gives the exact parameter to set. REVERSES the 2026-06-18
//      "Begin_Row -> Explain, the 4537 class anchor carries the action" decision: the anchor misses SUBCLASS
//      objects (their class is defined in a library, so no workspace-anchored 4537 fires) - Begin_Row is those
//      objects' only signal. Row noise stays solved: the per-(file,function) merge folds 4537 + 4533 into one row.
// v44: dbEdit/Edit conversion REBUILT as whole-file converters on the grid-family standard (Nils' catch: NOT a
//      pure class swap - the lineages differ, Edit/DfBaseEdit vs cTextEdit/DFBaseTextEdit). The v41
//      ChangeObsoleteEditControls (line-by-line class swap) is REPLACED by RefactorDbEditTocDbTextEdit +
//      RefactorEditTocTextEdit (eOtherFunction): swap Object AND Class declarations (a subclass base like
//      LegacyApp's dbEdit0 is modernised at its definition), Wrap_State -> pbWrap inside the block, TODO-flag
//      properties with no modern equivalent. Match-texts .prn-verified: "class: DBEDIT" / "class: EDIT".
// v45: guidance recipes for the remaining backlog families (Explain-only, LIST template; .prn-verified
//      2026-07-04): ONE recipe covers the whole 18-command REGISTRY family via the shared text stem
//      "Obsolete Registry command" (guided cRegistry/cIniFile migration - stateful, never a line swap);
//      "command: Gosub" + its paired "command: Return" (extract-to-Procedure guidance; a guided REPORT is
//      the planned follow-up); 4537 per-class hints for ERRORSYSTEM / HELPSYSTEM / DBFLOATINGPOPUPMENU /
//      CMAPIHANDLER (no converter - honest hand-migrate guidance instead of the bare generic). Hardcoded
//      recipes seed BEFORE the install scan's parroted full-message rows, so these win the first-specific
//      precedence.
// v46: Explain recipe for error 4332 "Symbol already defined" (was Unknown/"No Refactoring Function yet"):
//      against a newer target the classic cause is a hand-declared Windows API struct/constant the
//      runtime packages now define themselves (LegacyApp: a local Struct tWinMemoryStatusEx colliding
//      with the target's own, 2026-07-05). Hand-fix guidance, no converter.
// v49: Auto-fix recipe for 4537 "Obsolete class: WinReport" -> RefactorWinReportTocWinReport2 (WinPrint 1
//      WinReport -> WinPrint 2 cWinReport2; also retires the obsolete Use Dfwinrpt.pkg). 2026-07-08.
// v50: Suggest recipes for 4533 "command: Gosub" + "command: On" (the ON..GOSUB dispatch) ->
//      GosubGotoReport, the Gosub-subroutine + Goto-rewrite-hint worksheet (report only; Goto itself
//      emits NO compiler message, so the report is its only surface). 2026-07-09.
// v51: Suggest recipes for the legacy Workspace-object shell -> RefactorWorkspaceTocApplication.
//      FIRST ERROR-shaped recipes in the library: Workspc.pkg is REMOVED from modern DataFlex, so
//      the construct fails hard instead of warning - 4313 (include WORKSPC not found) + 4345/4328
//      (U_WORKSPACE undefined). Match-texts key on the locale-independent WORKSPC / U_WORKSPACE
//      tokens (the surrounding message text is localized on non-English compilers). 2026-07-10.
// v52: Error routes for WinPrint 1 -> RefactorWinReportTocWinReport2 (Suggest): 4345/4328
//      U_WINREPORT, 4328 WINPRINTID, and the 4298 undefined-command families (DFWRITE*/DFFONT*/
//      DFHEADER*/DFBEGINHEADER/DFENDHEADER/DFLEFT-/DFRIGHTMARGIN). Modern Dfallrpt.pkg no longer
//      includes WinPrint 1, so a legacy report view fails HARD - the converter's class+Use swap
//      (cWinReport2.pkg -> Winprint_Commands.pkg) re-provides every one of them. 2026-07-10.
// v53: Suggest recipe 4328 GHOWORKSPACE -> ChangeLegacyWorkspaceAccessors: the retired Workspc.pkg
//      global read via 'Get Current<X>Path of ghoWorkspace to ...' (~270 sites in the LegacyApp3 study
//      workspace) rewrites to 'Get ps<X>Path of (phoWorkspace(ghoApplication)) to ...'. 2026-07-10.
// v54: (a) Error routes for the GRID family (same warning-vs-error gap as WinPrint v52): 4345/4328
//      U_DBGRID / U_DBLIST + 4298 BEGIN_ROW / END_ROW -> RefactorDbGridTocDbCJGrid resp.
//      RefactorDbListTocDbCJGridPromptList (Suggest). (b) Two guidance Explain hints: 52
//      "(operator) @" = an UNDECLARED @-suffixed global split at the '@' -> run
//      RefactorBareGlobalsToGlobalVariable on the PROGRAM (seeded BEFORE the generic 52 hint -
//      first-specific-wins); 4313 ".fd" = missing table definition, regenerate - the
//      <TABLE>.<column> cascade below is one fix. 2026-07-10.
// v55: Suggest recipe 4392 "Missing keyword returns on function declaration" ->
//      AddFunctionReturnsKeyword: appends 'Returns <type>' with the type inferred from the body's
//      Function_Return statements (literals + the body's own local declarations). The error line
//      IS the declaration line, so no match-text is needed. 2026-07-10.
// v56: Deep Scan integration - every ENABLED cDeepScanner catalog rule doubles as a recipe keyed
//      on its stable DS pseudo message number (9001+), so the source-driven Deep Scan's synthetic
//      messages classify and plan exactly like compiler messages (one matcher for grid + plan).
//      2026-07-10.
// v57: Deep Scan recipes ALPHA-GATED OFF (CB_DeepScanEnabled=False - Deep Scan ships in Alpha 4,
//      not Alpha 3): this reseed DROPS the DS recipes from any table that seeded at v56 with them
//      in (only dev machines). Bump again when the gate flips so they reseed back. 2026-07-10.
// v58: gate back ON for continued dev testing (Nils same evening) - the DS recipes reseed in.
// v59: PROMOTIONS after live verification (Nils 2026-07-11): the ten WinPrint v52 error routes
//      (RefactorWinReportTocWinReport2 - "all winprint errors are gone") and 4392
//      AddFunctionReturnsKeyword ("4392 OK") graduate Suggest -> AutoFix.
// v60: DS rule 'Cmdline command' re-routed from Explain to the NEW ChangeCmdlineCommandToFunction
//      (Suggest) - the DS rules double as recipes, so the route change needs a reseed. 2026-07-11.
// v61: + DS rule "Use without file extension" -> AddUseFileExtension (warning 4535 found source-side;
//      Nils's paytype.DD Use lines slipped an exhausted scan cycle). Rules seed v2. 2026-07-11.
// v62: + the eight DS obsolete-class rules (eDSClassDecl - the 4537 family source-side, so an
//      exhausted scan also means "no obsolete classes"). Rules seed v3. 2026-07-11.
// v63: class rules split Object-form vs SUBCLASS-definition form ('Class cMy is a dbList') - the
//      converters refuse class definitions, so the subclass rows are their own Explain rules
//      (Nils's 12-file LegacyApp3 residue). Rules seed v4. 2026-07-11.
//      *** WHEN NILS CALLS THE ALPHA 3 FREEZE: flip the Deep Scan gate OFF + bump to the NEXT
//      version so the shipped build reseeds the DS recipes OUT - see the CB_DeepScanEnabled list. ***
// v64: ALPHA 3 FREEZE (2026-07-12) - Deep Scan gate flipped OFF: CB_DeepScanEnabled -> False +
//      DeepScanObsoleteSymbols { Published = False }. This bump reseeds the DS recipes OUT of the
//      persisted ModAnalyzerRecipes JSON (the DS recipes ride BuildDefaultRecipes behind the gate).
// v65: 4536 hint fix (2026-07-12) - the generic "obsolete package" hint no longer asserts "replace with a
//      class" for EVERY 4536; many packages (e.g. Windows.pkg inside a WebApp) are ignored and should be
//      removed. A specific "will be ignored" recipe gives those a correct "remove the Use line" hint.
// v66: Deep Scan RE-ENABLED after Alpha 3 shipped (2026-07-12) - CB_DeepScanEnabled -> True again; this bump
//      reseeds the DS recipes back INTO the persisted table (they ride BuildDefaultRecipes behind the gate).
//      DeepScanObsoleteSymbols stays { Published = False } (permanent by design, not part of the gate).
// v68: ChangeSysdate4 -> ChangeSysdate, now owns BOTH Sysdate + Sysdate4 (-> CurrentDateTime()); + a
//      { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: Sysdate } tag, and the Sysdate Deep Scan rule flips to AutoFix.
// v69: 9 dev-only/char-mode relics (Debug/Memory/Winput/Get+Set_cursor_visible/…) route to
//      CommentOutObsoleteDevCommands - their Deep Scan rules flip Explain->Suggest (recipe reseed).
// v70: 9 Database-API commands (File_Size/Size_of_Field/Ifchange/Get_date_format/…) route to the
//      LegacyDbApiReport worksheet - their Deep Scan rules flip Explain->Suggest (recipe reseed).
// v71: 16 Registry/profile/INI commands (Reg_*/Get+Set_Profile_String/Read_dfini/…) route to the
//      LegacyRegistryReport worksheet - their Deep Scan rules flip Explain->Suggest (recipe reseed).
// v72: 25 Embedded SQL commands (SQLConnect/SQLOpen/SQLPrepare/SQLExecute/SQLFetch/CLI_*/…) route to
//      the LegacySqlReport worksheet - their Deep Scan rules flip Explain->Suggest (recipe reseed).
// v73: two Explain rules gain converters: Chain -> ChangeChainToRunprogram (+ a { ModRecipe = 4533 |
//      command: Chain } tag, dormant until this bump), DF_SYSDATE_4_STATE -> CommentOutSysdate4State.
//      (FindErr -> (Not(Found)) was tried + reverted: FindErr is a PRESERVED runtime indicator here.)
// v77: Deep Scan rules v14 (+ Get_File_Exists -> ChangeGetFileExistsToFileExist) - the DS rules
//      double as recipes, so the table reseeds to pick the new rule up (Nils 2026-07-16).
// v79: + GuardDuplicateExternalFunctions ("4390 | as an external_function", Suggest) - the
//      duplicate-External_Function flavor gets its version-guard converter (Nils 2026-07-23).
// v80: + "4345 | should not be defined" Explain - the duplicate-name flavor (a class the newer
//      DataFlex now owns from its RUNTIME, e.g. DF 25's cRegEx); the row was a dead end with no
//      hint at all (Nils 2026-07-23, regexp.pkg:206 on LegacyApp).
// v81: + 4546 Explain - a MESSAGE (sql.pkg's SQLExecute Procedure, classically) called
//      function-style in an expression; DF 19.1 compiled it, DF 25 refuses. Probe-proven on the
//      LegacyApp OrderFunctions sites (Nils 2026-07-23); was "Explain with NO text" - gap closed.
// v82: + 14 Explain - runtime error 14 at COMPILE time = constant folding of a string-vs-numeric
//      coercion (quoted 'TABLE.COLUMN' literal >= numeric var, avtalsfakt.vw). The LAST recipe-less
//      error family on the LegacyApp dashboard (Nils 2026-07-23).
// v95: Deep Scan rules v20 (+ "Workspace program shell" -> RefactorWorkspaceTocApplication, and DS
//      9059 no longer claims '#REPLACE CURRENT$WORKSPACE'). The DS rules double as recipes, so the
//      table reseeds to pick the new rule up. This gives the legacy shell a SCAN-lane surface for the
//      first time: the v51 recipes above are ERROR-shaped (4313/4345/4328), so they only appear once
//      the file has been compiled by a modern compiler - a folder scan saw nothing (Nils 2026-07-30,
//      201 shells in one DF18 workspace).
// v96: Deep Scan rules v21 (+ ToANSI / ToOEM). The DS rules double as recipes, so the table
//      reseeds to pick them up. These exist because the COMPILER cannot locate this construct: it
//      reports 4534 at every CALL SITE of a workspace command whose body calls ToANSI, and never on
//      the package holding the call - so VerifyAutoFix rightly demotes all of those, and the one
//      file worth editing is invisible. The scan lane reads the source where it sits (Nils
//      2026-07-31).
// v116: the generic 4536 hint gains its third shape - a package can be obsolete yet still the only
//       declarer of supported functions (MapiDllCalls.pkg carries ShellExecute; Nils 2026-08-30,
//       correcting the earlier remove-the-Use framing) - and warns against blind Use removal.
// v117: ChangeCodeJockReportToGrid gains a 4306 recipe keyed on XTPREPORT, and Deep Scan rules v37
//       add the constants rule beside it. Both its lanes keyed on the PACKAGE name, so a workspace
//       already on the modern base class whose only residue was 'Set peVisualTheme to
//       xtpReportThemeVisualStudio2012Light' was invisible to the analyzer and broke the build on a
//       line the converter has always rewritten (Nils 2026-08-30: "it is not being triggered").
// v119: ChangeReplaceCommandToFunction was declared TWICE - the superseded copy is gone and its
//       {ModRecipe 4531} moved onto the survivor.
// v120: MATCHER ONLY, and it REMOVES findings. DS 9221 now asks GuardAnsiExternalFunctionWithWide's
//       own _AnsiWideRefusalReason before raising a row, so a declaration the converter would refuse
//       no longer reaches the board with a Run button (Nils 2026-08-31). The scan rules double as
//       analyzer recipes, so the table has to reseed with the rules bump (CI_DeepScanRulesVersion v38).
// v128: DS 9226 removed (CI_DeepScanRulesVersion v46) - the recipe table has to reseed or it
//       keeps offering a rule that no longer exists.
// v127: DS 9226 (CI_DeepScanRulesVersion v45) - the scalar-in-a-byte-buffer Explain rule.
// v126: DS 9225's matcher narrowed (CI_DeepScanRulesVersion v44) - a nested member with no
//       shipped tWin twin now refuses the Type, so the recipe table has to reseed with it.
// v125: DS 9225 (CI_DeepScanRulesVersion v43) - the Win32 Type buffer routed to
//       ChangeExternalFunctionCallers, which until now no rule offered at all.
// v124: DS 9225/9226 WITHDRAWN (CI_DeepScanRulesVersion v42) - the Tier-3 pair never shipped.
// v122/v123: DS 9225, then the 9225/9226 split. Never released.
// v121: DS 9224 joins the seed (CI_DeepScanRulesVersion v39) - the scan rules double as
//       analyzer recipes, so the table has to reseed for the new row to be offered at all.
Define CI_ModAnalyzerRecipeVersion for 190   // v190: DS 9221 refuses raw-memory A/W pairs (see CI_DeepScanRulesVersion v107), so its rows reseed (2026-11-21). v189: wording only - every function's { Description } reworded in plain, complete sentences, and the hint is a Description's first line, so the table reseeds to carry the new text (Nils 2026-11-21). v188: 4345 "error u_" + 4328 "argument u_" -> MakeFileAutonomous; the grid converters' U_DBGRID / U_DBLIST rows get the Class-line guard (2026-11-13). v187: 4298 "not found word/byte/long" -> ChangeStructMemberLegacyTypes (2026-11-12). v186: 4539 "64-bit pointer to integer" -> Win64IntegerToLongPtr (2026-11-12). v185: paired with DeepScan rules v106 (DS 9253 and 9254, reads of ghoWorkspace). v184: paired with DeepScan rules v105 (DS 9204 carries its converter). v183: paired with DeepScan rules v104 (DS 9252, an Error number with its own text). v182: paired with DeepScan rules v103 (a file is no reader of a method it defines). v181: paired with DeepScan rules v102 (the struct catalogue holds every named struct, so message and notification structs take Windows' layout). v180: paired with DeepScan rules v101 (a buffer followed across methods). v179: paired with DeepScan rules v100 (flattened Win32 Types convert). v178: paired with DeepScan rules v99 (a call proves a Win32 Type's struct). v177: paired with DeepScan rules v98 (DS 9246, (Self) in an object reference, Auto-fix and on). v176: paired with DeepScan rules v97 (an @-string's text is text). v175: paired with DeepScan rules v96 (a multi-line string's body is text). v174: paired with DeepScan rules v95 (the old-class-name pre-check). v173: paired with DeepScan rules v94 (the obsolete 'for' targets, the old class names, Field_Main_Index). v172: paired with DeepScan rules v93 (DFTrue / DFFalse, the two Error number rules, the (Self) object-reference rule). v171: paired with DeepScan rules v92 (the pointer/string Move rules of 4539). v170: paired with DeepScan rules v91 (the Incr, Decr and Sub command rules). v169: paired with DeepScan rules v90 (an Else in front of an Indicate, an Ind or a bracket no longer hides the line). No recipe text changed; the rules double as recipes, so this is the reseed that lets the grid and the plan see the new rows. v168: paired with DeepScan rules v89 (a bracket-prefixed Ind is command position, as v74 made a bracket-prefixed Indicate). No recipe text changed; the rules double as recipes, so this is the reseed that lets the grid and the plan see the new rows. v167: paired with DeepScan rules v88 - wording only: no hint calls a method, a declaration, a Type or the source "your" any more, the workspace is named instead (Nils 2026-10-05: "I wonder if there might be more places where 'your ' or 'yours' are used? Might as well change them as well"). The 4379, 4332 and 4328 hints, the library-line hint and the skip-marker note with them. v166: paired with DeepScan rules v87 - DS 9229 (FloatingPopupMenu subclass) carries ConvertPopupMenuSubclassToCodeJock, Review. v165: paired with DeepScan rules v86 - wording only: a class the workspace declares is a "workspace subclass" / "workspace class" in every hint, never "your own" (Nils 2026-10-05: "it is often consultants working with somebody else's codebase"). The E 4345 hint with them. v164: paired with DeepScan rules v85 - hint text only: the six grid subclass rules name the object converter ConvertGridSubclassToCodeJock runs itself (Nils 2026-10-05). The rules double as recipes, so the table reseeds to carry the new text. v163: paired with DeepScan rules v84 - DS 9234-9236 (an object of a grid subclass ConvertGridSubclassToCodeJock converts in place, Auto-fix) appended, and the three subclass rules DS 9198-9200 carry that converter (Nils 2026-10-04: every grid of a workspace sat in the hand band). The rules double as recipes, so this is the reseed that lets the grid and the plan see them. VerifyBeginRow hands a Begin_Row row inside such an object to the same converter. v162: paired with DeepScan rules v83 - the dbGrid / Grid / dbList subclass rules' hints name ConvertGridSubclassToCodeJock, which ports such a class in place and converts every object of it (Nils 2026-10-04). The rules double as recipes, so the table reseeds to carry the new text. v161: paired with DeepScan rules v81 - the grid conversions are Auto-fix (DS 9190 dbGrid, 9191 Grid and 9193 List promoted; 9192 dbList already was). The four 4537 class tags of the grid converters say AutoFix explicitly: they carried no category, so they took the { EnumList } default, Review. The Begin_Row / BEGIN_ROW / U_DBGRID / U_DBLIST recipes stay Review - they land in the same (file, function) row as the object's scan hit, and a merged row takes the higher category (Nils 2026-10-03). v160: paired with DeepScan rules v80 - the four grid/list subclass rule hints rewritten (their objects stay as they are until the subclass is ported). The rules double as recipes, so this is the reseed that lets the grid and the plan show the new text. v159: paired with DeepScan rules v79 - DS 9233 ('Resident' on a legacy report image's name line, Auto-fix via RemoveResidentImageOption) appended. The rules double as recipes, so this is the reseed that lets the grid and the plan see the new row. v158: paired with DeepScan rules v75 - DS 9231/9232 (DF_DATE_4_STATE / DF_EPOCH_VALUE) appended and the DF_SYSDATE_4_STATE rule re-pointed at the renamed CommentOutLegacyDateAttributes; the rules double as recipes, so this is the reseed. v157: paired with DeepScan rules v74 (a bracket-prefixed Indicate is command position; an 'Indicate x as' line is a condition line). No recipe text changed; the rules double as recipes, so this is the reseed that lets the grid and the plan see the new rows. v156: paired with DeepScan rules v73 (DS 9221 declines an A/W pair whose arms take different structures). No recipe text changed; the rules double as recipes, so without this the grid and the plan keep answering Auto-fix for the 15 rows from the older table. v155: paired with DeepScan rules v72 - the DS 9229/9230 subclass hints no longer promise that objects of a FloatingPopupMenu subclass are listed, because that family is not in _SubclassLearningToken and they never were. Hint text only: no category, converter or match changed. The rules double as recipes, so this is the cache flush that lets the corrected hint reach the grid and the plan. v154: paired with DeepScan rules v71, and the new { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: IfChange | Suggest } on ChangeIfchangeCommandToAttribute - the command still compiles on DF26 under the obsolete-command warning, so the compiler lane offers the same fix the scan does. Tagged Suggest explicitly: the recipe would otherwise take the AutoFix default, and this one is deliberately Review. Note that such a line also raises two 4538 indicator warnings (the macro answers through indicator 122); those match the 'without parentheses' recipe and route to the indicator converters, which cannot change the line, so VerifyAutoFix hands them back as Explain and the 4533 row is the one that carries the fix. v153: paired with DeepScan rules v70, and the two { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: Get_FileList } / { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: Set_FileList } tags on the new ChangeFileListCommandToAttribute - both commands still compile on DF26 under the obsolete-command warning, so the compiler lane offers the same fix the scan does. The other new converter, ChangeGetCurrentLockcountToAttribute, deliberately gets NO tag: FMAC gives Get_Current_Lockcount no WarningEx, so no compiler message exists to key a recipe on and one would only sit there matching nothing (Nils 2026-09-15, asking why obsolete Database-API symbols come with no auto-fixable suggestion). v152: the Deep Scan rows LEAVE the recipes JSON. Until now BuildDefaultRecipes appended the catalog rules as 9001+ rows and WriteRecipesToJson persisted them, so the tracked JSON carried a SNAPSHOT of the rules - and whenever that JSON's stamp matched the code (a hand-kept 'in step' bump does exactly that), the snapshot won over the live catalog: the scan said Auto-fix / ChangeGetDateFormatToAttribute for DS 9086 while the grid and the plan still answered Review / LegacyDbApiReport from the frozen row (Nils 2026-09-15, 'left without a refactoring suggestion'; the same shape pinned a superseded hint on 2026-09-01). Now RefreshRecipeTable drops every DS row from whichever set won and re-appends the live rules (_WithLiveDeepScanRecipes), and the writer strips them (_WithoutDeepScanRecipes) - the rules catalog is the ONLY source of a DS row's category and converter, and the recipes JSON can no longer disagree with it. v151: paired with DeepScan rules v69 (DS 9086 Get_date_format -> ChangeGetDateFormatToAttribute, Auto-fix), and the new { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: Get_Date_Format } tag on that converter - the command still compiles on DF26 under the obsolete-command warning, so the compiler lane offers the same fix. v150: the nine { ModRecipe = 4328 | symbol: <type> } tags on ChangeLegacyTypeCast - a match text written 'symbol: X' hits as a WHOLE symbol only (MatchTextHits), so DATETIME no longer fires on DWHIGHDATETIME; a plain match text stays a substring, which the stem families (4298 | DFWRITE, 4306 | XTPREPORT) rely on. v149: paired with DeepScan rules v68, and the two { ModRecipe = 4537 | class: FLOATINGPOPUPMENU / DBFLOATINGPOPUPMENU } tags now say AutoFix. v148: paired with DeepScan rules v67 (the popup converter's own refusal marker is re-asked, in both lanes). v147: paired with DeepScan rules v66 (DS 9227-9230, the legacy right-click menu), and the two { ModRecipe = 4537 | class: FLOATINGPOPUPMENU / DBFLOATINGPOPUPMENU } tags on ChangeFloatingPopupMenuToCodeJock, which replace the hardcoded DBFLOATINGPOPUPMENU Explain. v146: paired with DeepScan rules v65 (DS 9225 reaches Put_String / GetBuff_String on a Type's fixed text field). v145: paired with DeepScan rules v64 (DS 9225 reaches a buffer bound to a one-field Type - tDWORD / tHANDLE become the scalar). v144: paired with DeepScan rules v63 (DS 9225 reaches a buffer handed straight to a declared API's Pointer parameter and a Size_of_Field on the Type; a declined Type raises one Explain row with the run's reason). v143: paired with DeepScan rules v61 (six rules promoted to Auto-fix), and the 4538 'without parentheses' recipe on ChangeLegacyIndicators / ChangeSquareBracketsIndicators now says AutoFix. It carried no category, so it took the { EnumList } default - Review - though the [Found] usage it matches is exactly what both converters rewrite, and their other 4535 / 4538 tags already said AutoFix (Nils 2026-09-11). v142: paired with DeepScan rules v60 (DS 9226, the unpaired 'CompilerWarnings Off'). The rules double as recipes, so the row stays dormant in the grid and the plan until the recipe table reseeds. v141: paired with DeepScan rules v58, and the new { ModRecipe = 4533 | command: Return } on ChangeReturnToGosubReturn. Guard D handles the rest of that family for free: a 4533 'command: Return' reported against a MACRO invocation line has no 'return' on it, so it demotes to Explain with the macro-expansion hint instead of promising an auto-fix that would no-op there. v140: paired with DeepScan rules v57 (the '#REPLACE directive' hint is cut to one line). v139: paired with DeepScan rules v56 (the '#REPLACE directive' row carries ChangeReplaceDirectiveToDefine and admits negative values). v138: paired with DeepScan rules v55 (the strmark / strlen rows carry their converters). v137: paired with DeepScan rules v54 (the edit subclass rows carry their converter). v136: paired with DeepScan rules v53 (an EMPTY grid-converter parameter reaches the learned subclasses). v135: paired with DeepScan rules v52 (Grid / List: an empty parameter converts the plain class only; dbGrid / dbList keep the wide EMPTY). v134: paired with rules v51. Previously v133: error 52 "(operator)" is owned by SplitCommandOutOfCondition (Auto-fix by shape, VerifyAutoFix hands the rest back to Explain). v132: paired with DeepScan rules v50 (subclass instances of the legacy classes; subclass hints). v131: paired with DeepScan rules v49 (_TypeDeclaringMember counts only bare Field declarations). The rules double as recipes, so bump both or the grid and the plan keep answering from the older table - the v130 lesson (2026-09-02).
// v129:   // v129: DS 9225's suggested-fix text no longer promises an SDK catalogue entry - the converter falls back to the author's own member types - and says out loud that the fallback path preserves the layout WITHOUT correcting a member already wrong for 64-bit. The rules double as recipes, so the text stays stale in the grid and the plan until this reseeds (Nils 2026-09-01).

// FT: generic UI-yield hook so a long plan build keeps the message loop pumping (animates the FT marquee).
// The FT caller sets phoUIYieldTarget (in Construct_Object) to an object that pumps; a console run leaves it
// 0 so this no-ops - keeps Desktop/PumpMsgQueue out of this shared engine file.
Register_Procedure DoYieldToUI

Class cModAnalyzer is a cObject

    Procedure Construct_Object
        Forward Send Construct_Object
        /* Library roots for the current workspace, memoised for VerifyLibraryOwned - lowercased and
           separator-terminated, index-parallel with pasLibNames. Keyed on the workspace's .sws so a
           workspace switch recomputes. Deliberately settable: the fixtures seed them directly,
           because ghoApplication under UnitTests is a bare cApplication and has no
           psSWSFile to compute them from. */
        Property String[] pasLibRoots
        Property String[] pasLibNames
        Property String   psLibRootsKey ""
        /* THE SELECTED FOLDERS, lowercased + separator-terminated (2026-08-16). A library file that
           sits under a folder the user SELECTED is theirs to change - "All folders that are selected
           should be able to be changed ... It doesn't matter if the files are part of a library or
           not - it never has" (Nils, twice on 2026-08-16). VerifyLibraryOwned strips the recipe only
           for a library the user did NOT select (the git-cached vwin32fh / VDFQuery: replaced, not
           edited). Refreshed with the roots when they are computed; seedable like them (a '*' key
           pins both), so the fixtures stay independent of ghoApplication. */
        Property String[] pasSelectedRoots
        /* THE MISSING-INCLUDE INDEX (2026-10-05) - see MissingIncludeHint. Built at the first "Include file
           not found" this object is asked about and kept for its life, which is one analyze: the disk may
           differ by the next. The roots, the folders of the workspace files already referenced and the
           target version come from the loaded workspace - unless pbMissIncSeeded says a fixture set them,
           because ghoApplication under the test runner has no workspace to compute them from. */
        Property Boolean   pbMissIncBuilt False
        Property Boolean   pbMissIncSeeded False
        Property String[]  pasMissIncRoots          // where to look: the workspace home and its libraries' roots
        Property String[]  pasMissIncRefFolders     // lowercased folders of the .sws files the workspace has
        Property String    psMissIncTarget ""       // the target DataFlex version, "26.0"
        Property String[]  pasMissIncFiles          // every source file under the roots, full path
        Property String[]  pasMissIncLeafLc         // its lowercased leaf name, index-parallel
        Property Integer[] paiMissIncRoot           // the root it was found under, index-parallel
        // In-memory recipe cache, lazily loaded from the ModAnalyzer table.
        Property tModAnalyzer[] paRecipes
        Property Boolean pbRecipesLoaded False
        // The target DataFlex version this cache was last loaded for. Compared against the app's
        // current psTargetDfVersion to detect a target switch and force a re-scan / re-seed.
        Property String psLoadedDfVersion ""
        // FT marquee pump target - set by the FT dock StartAutoSelect; 0 = no UI yield (e.g. console runner).
        Property Handle phoUIYieldTarget 0
        /* WHY ReadSavedPlan SAID NO (2026-08-17), when it was the plan's PROVENANCE and not its absence:
           the file was there and parsed, but its compile output is gone, unstamped, or has been written
           since. "" after a True, or after a False that was "no file / bad layout". A caller that would
           otherwise say "no saved Check for this workspace yet" reads this first - the readout has to
           distinguish "never checked" from "checked, but not this workspace as it stands now". */
        Property String psSavedPlanStaleWhy ""
        // The whole-file dry-run probe memo (VerifyAutoFixWholeFile) moved to the funclib
        // (pasWholeFileSessKey / paiWholeFileSessVal on cBaseFuncLib) so it persists across analyzes -
        // see the note there. It used to be an instance property here (per-analyze only).
    End_Procedure

    // Human-readable label for a category value (shown in the analysis grid).
    // Pulls the ambiguous MESSAGE name out of an error-4544 text. The compiler writes
    //     "Ambiguous function in expression Function GET_VALUE with ambiguous return type used in expression"
    // so the name sits between "GET_" and the next space, with the GET_ prefix dropped: GET_VALUE -> VALUE.
    // THE COMPILER IS THE ORACLE here - it names the ambiguous messages outright, so ChangeAmbiguousCallToGet
    // can be driven straight from the diagnostics with no workspace-wide declaration harvest and no cached
    // state (Nils 2026-07-21). Casing is the compiler's (upper); every consumer matches case-insensitively,
    // and the converter emits the name as SPELLED IN THE SOURCE, so upper-case here is harmless.
    // "" when the text is not that shape. PURE -> unit-testable.
    Function AmbiguousFnNameFromText String sText Returns String
        String sRest sUp
        Integer iPos

        Move (Uppercase(sText)) to sUp
        /* ANCHOR ON THE COMPILER'S PHRASE, "FUNCTION GET_", not the first "GET_" (2026-08-16). The
           display text carries the FILE PATH before the message - "Error 4544:   E:\WinServ\AppSrc\Machines_get_data.dg   (ln 765) Ambiguous function ... Function GET_PBSILENT ..." -
           and a path with "_get_" in it won the search: the board listed "PBSILENT, customer_data.dg"
           as two ambiguous names and offered to rewrite calls of a file. Fall back to a bare "GET_"
           only when the phrase is absent (a text with no path in it). */
        Move (Pos("FUNCTION GET_", sUp)) to iPos
        If (iPos > 0) ;
            Move (iPos + 9) to iPos
        Else ;
            Move (Pos("GET_", sUp)) to iPos
        If (iPos = 0) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move (Mid(sText, (Length(sText) - iPos - 3), (iPos + 4))) to sRest
        Move (Pos(" ", sRest)) to iPos
        If (iPos > 0) ;
            Move (Left(sRest, (iPos - 1))) to sRest
        Function_Return (Trim(sRest))
    End_Function

    // Pulls the clashing NAME out of an error-4390 text. Two shapes, both seen live:
    //     "Illegal method name definition Global method GET SPLIT already defined"
    //     "Illegal method name definition WINAPI_INTERNETQUERYOPTION is already defined as an external_function"
    // The first carries "GET <NAME>" (a SPACE, unlike 4544's "GET_<NAME>"); in the second the name is the
    // word before "is already defined". Naming the clashing symbol is what lets the findings list show the
    // CAUSE - six declarations - instead of an error count nobody can act on. PURE -> unit-testable.
    Function BackportNameFromText String sText Returns String
        String sUp sRest
        Integer iPos

        Move (Uppercase(sText)) to sUp
        Move (Pos("GET ", sUp)) to iPos
        If (iPos > 0) Begin
            Move (Mid(sText, (Length(sText) - iPos - 3), (iPos + 4))) to sRest
            Move (Pos(" ", sRest)) to iPos
            If (iPos > 0) ;
                Move (Left(sRest, (iPos - 1))) to sRest
            Function_Return (Trim(sRest))
        End
        Move (Pos(" IS ALREADY DEFINED", sUp)) to iPos
        If (iPos = 0) ;
            Function_Return ""
        // Walk back over the name that precedes it.
        Move (Trim(Left(sText, (iPos - 1)))) to sRest
        Move (RightPos(" ", sRest)) to iPos
        If (iPos > 0) ;
            Move (Right(sRest, (Length(sRest) - iPos))) to sRest
        Function_Return (Trim(sRest))
    End_Function

    Function CategoryText Integer eCategory Returns String
        If (eCategory = eModAutoFix) ;
            Function_Return "Auto-fix"
        If (eCategory = eModSuggest) ;
            Function_Return "Review"
        If (eCategory = eModExplain) ;
            Function_Return "Explain"
        Function_Return "Unknown"
    End_Function

    // Append one recipe to a recipe array and return the grown array.
    Function AddRecipe tModAnalyzer[] aRecipes Integer iMsgNo Boolean bWarn String sMatch String sFn Integer eCat String sHint Returns tModAnalyzer[]
        Integer i
        Move (SizeOfArray(aRecipes)) to i
        Move iMsgNo to aRecipes[i].iMsgNumber
        Move bWarn  to aRecipes[i].bIsWarning
        Move sMatch to aRecipes[i].sMatchText
        Move eCat   to aRecipes[i].eCategory
        Move sHint  to aRecipes[i].sHint
        Move sFn    to aRecipes[i].sRefactorFn
        Get CategoryText eCat to aRecipes[i].sCategoryText
        Function_Return aRecipes
    End_Function

    // NOTE: the per-file obsolete-warning parser (_ObsoleteRecipesFromLines) and the install-Pkg scan
    // it drove (ScanInstallPackages) were RELOCATED to cRefactorFuncLib (ghoFuncLib). ONE shared
    // install-package walk there (WalkInstallPackages / InstallScanRecipes) now feeds BOTH this recipe
    // scan and the TypeToStructReport Type catalogue, reading each package once. RefreshRecipeTable
    // (below) calls InstallScanRecipes of ghoFuncLib; AddRecipe / CategoryText (above) stay here.

    // The built-in DEFAULT recipe set. This is both the SEED written to the ModAnalyzer
    // table on first run, and the data the unit tests classify against (no DB needed).
    // Tune the live mapping by editing the ModAnalyzer TABLE - this only bootstraps it.
    Function BuildDefaultRecipes Returns tModAnalyzer[]
        tModAnalyzer[] aR
        // --- ERRORS (bIsWarning = False) ---
        Get AddRecipe aR 4539 False "" "" eModSuggest "Pointer/String conversion (Unicode compiler) - check the direction; see the Unicode refactor family." to aR
        // 4544 (ambiguous return type used in expression) is now FUNCTION-BACKED via ChangeObjectFunctionCallToGet's
        // { ModRecipe = 4544 } meta-tag (Auto-fix, whole-file source-guarded): the DF26 object-method-in-expression
        // call it rewrites IS the dominant 4544 cause, and the Get form assigns the return to a typed local (exactly
        // the old hint's advice). A bare Get_ accessor / COM property 4544 (which it can't touch) is demoted back to
        // Explain by VerifyAutoFixWholeFile's whole-file dry-run. The old function-less generic was removed here so it
        // no longer shadows the meta-tag recipe (ClassifyFromRecipes keeps the FIRST generic). (Nils 2026-07-07.)
        Get AddRecipe aR 4390 False "" "" eModSuggest "Duplicate global definition - remove or rename the later one." to aR
        // 4345 is variant-routed by message text. "SELECT, MUST BE DEFINED" (bare predefined SELECT macro
        // with no OldFMACCommands.pkg) is owned by DefineSelectMacro via a { ModRecipe } meta-tag (auto-fix).
        // "ARGUMENT REQUIRED" (the #$ placeholder) and "INVALID LITERAL" (|SI../|SV.. compiler tokens) are NOT
        // root problems - they cascade off an undefined symbol / legacy Type member (Type->Struct) on the SAME
        // line, so each points at the real cause. The empty-match generic is the accurate catch-all for every
        // other 4345 (undefined/removed global, or the rare reserved FMAC name). All are function-less, so
        // ClassifyFromRecipes shows them as Explain. NB the OLD generic hint hard-asserted the reserved-FMAC
        // cause for ALL of them - wrong for the real population (LegacyApp: 33x ARGUMENT REQUIRED, |SI/|SV
        // literals, MUST BE DEFINED; ZERO reserved-name 4345). Specifics must NOT use bare "MUST BE DEFINED":
        // it would shadow the more-specific "SELECT, MUST BE DEFINED" auto-fix (first specific match wins).
        Get AddRecipe aR 4345 False "ARGUMENT REQUIRED" "" eModExplain "An argument could not be resolved on this line - almost always a knock-on from an undefined symbol, e.g. a legacy Type member (like tRect.left) accessed as a Struct. Fix that symbol (see the 4328 on the same line) and this clears with it." to aR
        Get AddRecipe aR 4345 False "INVALID LITERAL" "" eModExplain "A compiler-internal literal token is invalid - fall-out from a broken expression on this line, usually an undefined symbol or a legacy Type member accessed as a Struct. Fix the root symbol (see the 4328 here), not the literal." to aR
        Get AddRecipe aR 4345 False "" "" eModSuggest "Type-check error - review manually. Usually an undefined or removed global, or a legacy Type member accessed as a Struct (Type->Struct); occasionally a reserved FMAC name (iEntry/iExit/iValidate) used as a local. Check the flagged symbol and any 4328 on the same line." to aR
        /* 4379 names NO symbol, so the row cannot carry a converter by recipe - the findings row reads
           the LINE instead (ObjExprCallNameInLine) and offers "Rewrite as Get..." when it finds the
           object-expression shape.

           THE MECHANISM, probe-compiled against DF26 on 2026-08-19 rather than assumed (the first
           wording was read as blaming the call SYNTAX, which is wrong): an own method called
           Name(Self, a, b) compiles fine when DataFlex does not use the name, so the expression form
           is still legal. What breaks is a NAME COLLISION - "Move (Split("T", "a T b")) to sa"
           compiles in a file that declares nothing, so DF26's runtime owns Split; it appears in no
           .pkg and not in Lib\GlobalFunctions.lst, and StringSplit does not exist at all (E 4367).
           Three arguments then overrun the runtime function's signature, which is the 4379. Note the
           quiet variant this implies: a call whose argument count HAPPENS to fit binds to DataFlex's
           function and compiles - wrong behaviour, no error. */
        Get AddRecipe aR 4379 False "" "" eModExplain "Wrong number of arguments - check the number of arguments the call passes against the function's declared parameter list. Two classic causes. (1) An OLD MISMATCH surfacing: DataFlex 19.1 and earlier let an expression-form call pass extra arguments unchecked, so a call that has been quietly wrong for years first errors here - and often the arguments fit a near-miss SIBLING (SelectFolder vs SelectFolder2), which is the function the call was meant for. Rewrite against the sibling, preferably in the Get form: Get Name of hoObject args to dest. (2) A NAME COLLISION with the newer DataFlex: a workspace method called as Name(Self, args) is still legal syntax, but when the new release owns the same name the call resolves to ITS function and the count no longer fits. The colliding owner can be invisible to a workspace search: DF26's runtime owns Split (declared in no package), and install packages declare more - Pkg\cSelectFolderDialog.pkg declares a 2-parameter SelectFolder - while the Studio's global search does NOT search the install's Pkg folder; check there before concluding the name is unowned. For a collision on a workspace method the Get form binds back to it: Get Name args to dest. Either way: if the argument count happens to FIT the wrong function, the call compiles and quietly misbehaves - so check sibling calls of the same name too." to aR
        Get AddRecipe aR 4546 False "" "" eModExplain "Function not legal at runtime / has moved - check the owning package." to aR
        // The generic. NOTE the LAST cause: DF26 refuses a TYPE NAME used as a conversion function and
        // reports the type's INTERNAL name, which for two of them is a word the source never contains
        // (Handle -> LONGPTR, Pointer -> ADDRESS). Four of the five route to ChangeLegacyTypeCast by
        // meta-tag; ADDRESS deliberately does not (it is an ordinary column name - see that function).
        Get AddRecipe aR 4328 False "" "" eModExplain CS_Mod4328Hint to aR
        // 4332 fires once per duplicate definition. Against a NEWER target the dominant modernization case
        // is a hand-declared Windows API item the runtime packages now define themselves (v46; LegacyApp:
        // a local Struct tWinMemoryStatusEx vs the target's own copy). No converter - deleting a local
        // declaration the app may still reference elsewhere is not a safe blind rewrite.
        Get AddRecipe aR 4332 False "" "" eModExplain "Symbol already defined - the flagged name is declared twice. Against a newer DataFlex target the classic cause is a hand-declared Windows API struct/constant that the runtime packages now define themselves: delete the local copy (or keep it for old targets behind a version #IF). A duplicate External_Function belongs in an #IFNDEF get_FunctionName guard; if both definitions are in the workspace's code, remove or rename one." to aR
        // Raw compiler errors that are NOT modernization targets - give them a plain-language cause so they read
        // as "fix in source", NOT as an unhandled refactor ("No Refactoring Function yet - review manually").
        // Match-text scoped so only the exact variant gets the hint (other 52/54 stay Unknown). (Nils 2026-07-02:
        // LegacyApp rows that looked like phantom refactors were REAL errors - undefined class cCJReportSelectedRows,
        // and a '()' on a Get form.)
        Get AddRecipe aR 54 False "existing class name" "" eModExplain "Undefined class - the class name in RefClass/Create resolves to nothing (a missing Use, a definition-order issue, or a removed/renamed class). Fix in source (define or Use it, or remove the reference); not an automated refactor." to aR
        // The '(operator) @' variant MUST be seeded BEFORE the generic 'operator' one: the classifier
        // takes the FIRST matching specific, and every @-message also contains 'operator'. The shape
        // means an UNDECLARED @-suffixed global was split at the '@' (the companion 4328 names the
        // stem): typically the program still declares the OLD bare name while a hand-modernized
        // shared package reads the g-prefixed one (Nils 2026-07-10, the LegacyApp3 login package).
        Get AddRecipe aR 52 False "(operator) @" "" eModExplain "An @-suffixed global the compiler cannot resolve is SPLIT at the '@' (the companion 4328 error names the stem). Usually the PROGRAM still declares the old bare name while a modernized shared package reads the g-prefixed one - run RefactorBareGlobalsToGlobalVariable on the program source (.src), not on the file this error points at." to aR
        /* 52 "(operator)" is OWNED by SplitCommandOutOfCondition since 2026-09-05 ({ ModRecipe = 52 |
           operator | AutoFix } on the function - tag recipes are appended after these seeds, and a seed
           with the same match-text here would take the row first). The general advice that used to be
           this seed's hint now comes from VerifyAutoFix, which dry-runs the repair against the warned
           line and hands every 52 that is NOT the wrapped-command shape back to Explain with it
           (CS_ModExpr52Hint). The '(operator) @' seed above stays first, as before. */
        // Missing table-definition file: one setup problem, dozens of cascade errors - name the cause
        // so the <TABLE>.<column> avalanche below it reads as ONE fix (Nils 2026-07-10, the LegacyApp3
        // error wall: 33 of 94 remaining errors were this single missing .fd).
        Get AddRecipe aR 4313 False ".fd" "" eModExplain "A table definition file (.fd) was not found - the table is missing from the filelist, or its .fd was never generated for this workspace. Regenerate the .fd files in the Studio (Database Explorer / Synchronize Data Dictionaries); every <TABLE>.<column> undefined-symbol error that follows is cascade from this one missing file." to aR
        // Runtime error 14 surfacing at COMPILE time = constant folding. Diagnosed on LegacyApp
        // 2026-07-23 (avtalsfakt.vw): ("MACHINES.SVC_CACHE" >= nfrom_status) - a quoted column-name
        // STRING literal compared against a NUMERIC variable; DF 25 folds the constant coercion while
        // compiling and hits the same error 14 the runtime would have thrown. The four sibling lines
        // against STRING variables slip through (string-vs-string needs no coercion).
        Get AddRecipe aR 14 False "" "" eModExplain "Runtime error 14 at COMPILE time: the compiler evaluated a CONSTANT expression and could not turn a quoted string into a number (DF 19.1 deferred that coercion to runtime; DF 25 folds it while compiling). Typical shape: a quoted 'TABLE.COLUMN' string literal compared against a NUMERIC variable - sibling lines against STRING variables slip through silently. Decide what was MEANT: a column REFERENCE (drop the quotes) or a text comparison (make both sides strings) - and check the surrounding same-shaped lines while you are there." to aR
        // 4546: a MESSAGE called function-style in an expression. Diagnosed on LegacyApp 2026-07-23
        // (OrderFunctions.pkg): "Move (SQLExecute(...)) to s" bound to sql.pkg's SQLExecute
        // PROCEDURE - DF 19.1 compiled it, DF 25 refuses (probe-proven: Use sql.pkg + that Move = this
        // exact error). The workspace's real wrapper was SQLExecuteX, and a PARAMETER shadowing that
        // name is how the wrong name got written - hence the shadow warning in the hint.
        Get AddRecipe aR 4546 False "" "" eModExplain "A DataFlex MESSAGE - typically a Procedure; sql.pkg's SQLExecute is the classic - is called function-style inside an expression. DF 19.1 compiled that and resolved it at runtime; DF 25 rejects it, because a Procedure has no return value an expression can use. The call was usually MEANT for a similarly-named wrapper FUNCTION: check for a near-miss name (SQLExecute vs SQLExecuteX), and for a local or parameter SHADOWING the wrapper's name - often the reason the wrong name got written. Point the call at the intended function; if a local shadows its name, rename the local too." to aR
        // The duplicate-name flavor of 4345: the Class command's should-not-be-defined check. The
        // message prints only the EXISTING symbol's internal token (e.g. |CI134), never its name, so
        // without this hint the row is a dead end. Live case (LegacyApp 2026-07-23): the workspace's
        // own cRegEx COM wrapper vs DF 25's native cRegEx - which lives in the RUNTIME, so no Pkg
        // source declares it and no catalogue can see it; a 3-line probe compile proved the owner.
        Get AddRecipe aR 4345 False "should not be defined" "" eModExplain "The name this line defines already exists - the compiler's duplicate-name check (the |CI token in the message is the existing symbol's internal id; its name is not printed). When a class that compiled clean on the old release trips this on the new one, the newer DataFlex usually provides the name natively from its RUNTIME - no Pkg source declares it, so searching the install finds nothing (DF 25 owns cRegEx this way). Rename the workspace's class and its use sites, or migrate them to the native feature. A version guard around the block is only safe when nothing still uses the workspace's version on the new release - the native API usually differs, so surviving call sites would silently change behaviour." to aR
        // --- WARNINGS (bIsWarning = True) ---
        Get AddRecipe aR 4533 True "" "" eModExplain "Obsolete command - see the message for the specific command and its modern replacement." to aR
        // Three shapes, not two (Nils 2026-08-30): an obsolete package can still be the ONLY
        // declarer of fully-supported functions - MapiDllCalls.pkg is flagged obsolete yet carries
        // External_Function ShellExecute - so the hint must never read as "remove the Use".
        Get AddRecipe aR 4536 True "" "" eModExplain "Obsolete package - see the message for the specific action: many are replaced with a modern class (e.g. dftable.pkg -> cCJGrid/cCJDbGrid), some are inert here and can simply be removed (e.g. a desktop-only package inside a WebApp), and some still declare fully-supported functions (MapiDllCalls.pkg is obsolete yet carries ShellExecute) - never remove a Use without checking what the file uses from it." to aR
        // A 4536 package the compiler IGNORES here (a desktop-only package like Windows.pkg pulled into a
        // WebApp) is NOT a class-replacement - it is inert; the fix is to REMOVE its Use line. Keyed on the
        // "will be ignored" phrase so it beats the generic replace-with-a-class hint. Explain-only: removal
        // is context-dependent (Windows.pkg is legitimate in a desktop app), so no blind auto-fix. (Nils 2026-07-12.)
        Get AddRecipe aR 4536 True "will be ignored" "" eModExplain "This package is obsolete AND ignored by the compiler in this program (e.g. a desktop-only package like Windows.pkg inside a WebApp) - it has no effect here. Remove its Use line; there is no modern-class replacement (a deletion, not a swap)." to aR
        Get AddRecipe aR 4537 True "" "" eModExplain "Obsolete class - replace with the modern equivalent." to aR
        // The REGISTRY command family - 18 commands, all reading "Obsolete Registry command (not unicode
        // safe): <X>. Use cRegistry class" (REG_OPEN_KEY, REG_SET_DWORD, SET/GET_PROFILE_STRING, ...):
        // ONE recipe via the family's shared text stem. Stateful open/access/close as a group - a guided
        // migration, never a line swap (.prn-verified 2026-07-04; the backlog's "registry" item).
        Get AddRecipe aR 4533 True "Obsolete Registry command" "" eModExplain "Legacy registry/profile command - migrate the WHOLE block (root + open + read/write + close) to the cRegistry class (Use cRegistry.pkg): OpenKey/CreateKey, ReadString/WriteString, ReadDWord/WriteDWord, CloseKey when done. If the code targets an .ini FILE (a foreign profile) rather than the registry, use the cIniFile class instead. The commands are stateful as a group - hand-migrate the block, not line by line." to aR
        // Gosub and its paired Return (two 4533 texts): label subroutines SHARE the enclosing routine's
        // locals, so this is a semantic extraction, never a blind swap (Nils agreed 2026-06-12). A guided
        // Gosub REPORT is the planned follow-up; these hints make the rows self-explanatory meanwhile.
        Get AddRecipe aR 4533 True "command: Gosub" "" eModExplain "Gosub jumps to a label INSIDE this routine, sharing all its locals. Modernize by extracting the label block into a Procedure/Function and replacing the Gosub with Send/Get (shared locals become parameters/ByRef). Mind fall-through into the label block and any Goto entanglement - semantic surgery, review manually." to aR
        Get AddRecipe aR 4533 True "command: Return" "" eModExplain "This Return pairs with a Gosub (a subroutine return). Quick fix: rename it to Gosub_Return. Better: extract the subroutine into a Procedure and use Procedure_Return - see the 'Obsolete command: Gosub' rows in this file." to aR
        // 4537 classes with NO converter (the LIST-template Explain-only decision, 2026-06-10): honest
        // per-class guidance instead of the bare generic. Observed firing in real workspaces (backlog).
        Get AddRecipe aR 4537 True "class: ERRORSYSTEM" "" eModExplain "Legacy ErrorSystem class - the modern runtime installs its own error handling; custom logic belongs in an Error_Report override (or the cApplication error hooks). The old object can usually be removed. Hand-migrate." to aR
        Get AddRecipe aR 4537 True "class: HELPSYSTEM" "" eModExplain "Legacy HelpSystem class - replace with cHtmlHelp (Use cHtmlHelp.pkg) and CHM-based help, then re-wire the help-context calls. Hand-migrate." to aR
        // 4537 class DBFLOATINGPOPUPMENU / FLOATINGPOPUPMENU are function-backed since 2026-09-13:
        // ChangeFloatingPopupMenuToCodeJock carries both { ModRecipe } tags (Suggest). The hardcoded
        // Explain-only recipe that stood here for the db form was removed for the LIST reason below.
        Get AddRecipe aR 4537 True "class: CMAPIHANDLER" "" eModExplain "Legacy MAPI mail class - no direct modern equivalent. Simple notification mail: a mailto: shell launch; full mail handling: an SMTP or web-mail API integration. Hand-migrate." to aR
        // 4537 class LIST is now function-backed: RefactorListToCJGrid carries a { ModRecipe = 4537 |
        // class: LIST } meta-tag (Review - it has an {EnumList}), loaded by _AddMetaTagRecipes below. The old hardcoded
        // Explain-only recipe was removed (it shadowed the function: ClassifyFromRecipes keeps the FIRST
        // specific match, and a hardcoded function-less specific added here would always beat the
        // appended meta-tag one). Nils approved the function 2026-06-27.
        /* THE GENERIC 4534 MUST NOT DESCRIBE ONE FAMILY (Nils 2026-07-29, on
           "Get Parse_Path sDirectory to sPath" being told "Not Unicode-safe (OEM/ANSI) - see the
           message. ToOEM/ToANSI auto-rewrite..."). 4534 is "obsolete global function", and the
           OEM/ANSI calls are only its biggest family - on his workspace 6151 of 6177. Everything
           else inherited an explanation about a problem it does not have, which is how a hint stops
           being read at all. The OEM/ANSI advice lives on its own specifics below, where it is
           true; the generic now says only what is true of every 4534. "see the message" is load-
           bearing - _AppendCompilerText keys on it to append the compiler's own sentence. */
        Get AddRecipe aR 4534 True "" "" eModExplain "This global is obsolete - see the message for which one, and what the runtime offers instead." to aR
        /* 4534 on WINDOWS_MESSAGE specifically: nothing to do, and the generic hint above actively
           misleads on it. Nils, 2026-07-29, on 'Get Windows_Message CB_LIMITTEXT iMargin 0 to
           iRetval': he went looking for the OEM/ANSI problem the hint described, found none, and
           checked the system packages himself - "it actually calls the newer WindowsMessage function
           internally. So I think it is safe to ignore the warning." Compiler text is
           "Obsolete Global Function: WINDOWS_MESSAGE" (1 hit on WinServ, alongside 49 ToANSI 4534s -
           which is exactly why one number needed two answers).

           Explain, not a suppression: the compiler DID emit it, and a modernization tool that
           silently drops warnings teaches you not to trust its counts. State why it is safe and let
           the user decide; "Ignore all like this" is one click away if they want it off the board. */
        Get AddRecipe aR 4534 True "WINDOWS_MESSAGE" "" eModExplain "Safe to leave. Windows_Message is flagged as an obsolete global, but in current DataFlex it forwards to the modern WindowsMessage function internally, so the call behaves correctly and there is no Unicode issue to fix here (verified in the system packages, Nils 2026-07-29). Rewrite it only if you are tidying the name; nothing breaks if you do not." to aR
        // Function-backed recipes are co-located on the functions as { ModRecipe } meta-tags.
        Get _AddMetaTagRecipes aR to aR
        // Deep Scan rules double as recipes keyed on their stable DS pseudo numbers (9001+), so the
        // synthetic messages a Deep Scan feeds the grid classify + plan EXACTLY like compiler rows.
        // ALPHA-GATED (CB_DeepScanEnabled): Deep Scan ships in Alpha 4 - until then the recipes stay
        // out of the seeded table/JSON entirely. Flip list: see the constant in RefactorConstants.h.pkg.
        If (CB_DeepScanEnabled = True) ;
            Get _AddDeepScanRecipes aR to aR
        Function_Return aR
    End_Function

    // EVERY Deep Scan catalog rule becomes a recipe row - including the shipped-DISABLED ones:
    // a disabled rule produces no hits (its recipe is inert), but the moment the user ENABLES it
    // in the rules UI its findings must classify without waiting for a recipe-table reseed.
    // iMsgNo = the rule's stable iDSNumber (9001+, APPEND-ONLY seed - see cDeepScanner.AddDSRule),
    // no match-text (one number = one rule), category/function/hint straight from the rule. One
    // source of truth: the catalog (via its editable JSON) drives BOTH the scanner and the
    // classification, so the grid and the plan can never disagree with the scan.
    Function _AddDeepScanRecipes tModAnalyzer[] aRecipes Returns tModAnalyzer[]
        Handle ho
        tDeepScanRule[] aRules
        Integer i iLast
        Get Create (RefClass(cDeepScanner)) to ho
        Get LoadDeepScanRules of ho (DeepScanRulesJsonPath(ho)) to aRules
        Send Destroy of ho
        Move (SizeOfArray(aRules) - 1) to iLast
        For i from 0 to iLast
            Get AddRecipe aRecipes aRules[i].iDSNumber True "" aRules[i].sRefactorFn aRules[i].eCategory aRules[i].sHint to aRecipes
        Loop
        Function_Return aRecipes
    End_Function

    /* THE DEEP SCAN ROWS ARE DERIVED, NEVER STORED (v152, Nils 2026-09-15).

       A DS recipe (9001+) is a COPY of a catalog rule: its category, converter and hint all live in
       the rules JSON, which has its own version gate and its own editing surface (the rules dialog).
       Persisting that copy into the recipes JSON gave the same fact two homes with two stamps, and
       whenever the recipes stamp matched the code the copy won: DS 9086 had gone Auto-fix /
       ChangeGetDateFormatToAttribute in the catalog, the scan cache said so for the line, and the
       grid and the plan still filed it Review / LegacyDbApiReport from the row frozen in the JSON.
       A hand-kept 'in step' JSON makes that certain, not merely possible - the stamp is bumped by
       hand, the DS rows are not.

       So: the writer strips the DS rows (the tracked JSON carries compiler + install rows only),
       and RefreshRecipeTable drops whatever DS rows the winning set carries (an older on-disk JSON
       still has them) and re-appends the LIVE catalog. BuildDefaultRecipes keeps appending them so
       the pure in-memory classify the fixtures use (MakeRecipe) still sees a DS number. */
    Function _WithoutDeepScanRecipes tModAnalyzer[] aRecipes Returns tModAnalyzer[]
        tModAnalyzer[] aKept
        Integer i iLast
        Move (SizeOfArray(aRecipes) - 1) to iLast
        For i from 0 to iLast
            If (aRecipes[i].iMsgNumber < CI_DeepScanFirstDSNumber) ;
                Move aRecipes[i] to aKept[SizeOfArray(aKept)]
        Loop
        Function_Return aKept
    End_Function

    // The canonical set with its DS rows replaced by the live catalog's (gate-aware, like
    // BuildDefaultRecipes).
    Function _WithLiveDeepScanRecipes tModAnalyzer[] aRecipes Returns tModAnalyzer[]
        tModAnalyzer[] aOut
        Get _WithoutDeepScanRecipes aRecipes to aOut
        If (CB_DeepScanEnabled = True) ;
            Get _AddDeepScanRecipes aOut to aOut
        Function_Return aOut
    End_Function

    // Build the function-backed recipes from each refactor function's { ModRecipe } meta-tag(s),
    // read off ghoFuncLib's parsed metadata (the tag is co-located with the function in
    // oRefactorFuncLib / UserDefinedRefactorFunctions). Tag format: "msg# | match-text | [category]"
    // - category defaults from bFuncEnumList (an { EnumList } -> Review, none -> Auto-fix), overridable by
    //   an explicit category in the tag; the hint is the function's { Description } first line; and
    // bIsWarning is display-only here (ClassifyFromRecipes matches on number + text, not W/E).
    Function _AddMetaTagRecipes tModAnalyzer[] aRecipes Returns tModAnalyzer[]
        tFuncLib[] FuncLibArray
        Integer iFunc jTag iFuncCount iTagCount iMsgNo eCat iPos k iParts
        String sPart sMatch sHint
        String[] asParts

        Get pFuncLibArray of ghoFuncLib to FuncLibArray
        Move (SizeOfArray(FuncLibArray)) to iFuncCount
        For iFunc from 0 to (iFuncCount - 1)
            Move (SizeOfArray(FuncLibArray[iFunc].asModRecipe)) to iTagCount
            For jTag from 0 to (iTagCount - 1)
                Move (StrSplitToArray(FuncLibArray[iFunc].asModRecipe[jTag], "|")) to asParts
                Move (SizeOfArray(asParts)) to iParts
                If (iParts > 0) Begin
                    Move 0 to iMsgNo
                    Move (Trim(asParts[0])) to iMsgNo
                    Move "" to sMatch
                    // Default the category from whether the function exposes a user-choosable parameter
                    // (an { EnumList }, flagged by bFuncEnumList): a choice to make -> Review; none -> a
                    // deterministic transform -> Auto-fix. An explicit category in the tag (handled in
                    // the loop below) still overrides this default.
                    If (FuncLibArray[iFunc].bFuncEnumList) ;
                        Move eModSuggest to eCat
                    Else ;
                        Move eModAutoFix to eCat
                    For k from 1 to (iParts - 1)
                        Move (Trim(asParts[k])) to sPart
                        Case Begin
                            Case (Lowercase(sPart) = "autofix")
                                Move eModAutoFix to eCat
                                Case Break
                            Case (Lowercase(sPart) = "suggest")
                                Move eModSuggest to eCat
                                Case Break
                            Case (Lowercase(sPart) = "explain")
                                Move eModExplain to eCat
                                Case Break
                            Case Else
                                Move sPart to sMatch
                        Case End
                    Loop
                    // Hint = first line of the function's { Description } overview (sHelp).
                    Move FuncLibArray[iFunc].sHelp to sHint
                    Move (Pos(Character(10), sHint)) to iPos
                    If (iPos > 0) ;
                        Move (Left(sHint, (iPos - 1))) to sHint
                    Move (Trim(sHint)) to sHint
                    If (iMsgNo > 0) ;
                        Get AddRecipe aRecipes iMsgNo True sMatch FuncLibArray[iFunc].sName eCat sHint to aRecipes
                End
            Loop
        Loop
        Function_Return aRecipes
    End_Function

    // Pure matching: find the best recipe in aRecipes for (message number, message text).
    // A row whose MatchText appears in the text (specific) beats a blank-MatchText row
    // (generic) for the same number. Returns an eModUnknown recipe if nothing matches.
    /* A hint that says "see the message" must actually SHOW the message.

       Nils, 2026-07-29, on 'Get Windows_Message CB_LIMITTEXT iMargin 0 to iRetval' flagged W 4534:
       "triggers this false positive". It is not a false positive - the COMPILER emitted the 4534 -
       but our hint read "Not Unicode-safe (OEM/ANSI) - see the message. ToOEM/ToANSI auto-rewrite;
       other _Oem/_Ansi calls are manual", so he looked for a ToOEM on the line, found none, and
       reasonably concluded the tool was wrong. The hint explicitly deferred to the compiler and then
       hid what the compiler said.

       Only for hints that defer this way, and only when the compiler gave us something: elsewhere
       our own guidance is the more useful text, and the analyzer's demotion hints (the macro
       family) replace sHint outright and are unaffected. Same principle as showing the compiler's
       own words for a message we have no recipe for at all. */
    Function _AppendCompilerText tModAnalyzer Recipe String sMsgTxt Returns tModAnalyzer
        Move (Trim(sMsgTxt)) to sMsgTxt
        If (sMsgTxt = "") ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        If (Pos("see the message", Lowercase(Recipe.sHint)) = 0) ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        Move (Recipe.sHint + "  Compiler: " + sMsgTxt) to Recipe.sHint
        Function_Return Recipe
    End_Function

    Function ClassifyFromRecipes Integer iMsgNo String sMsgTxt tModAnalyzer[] aRecipes Returns tModAnalyzer
        tModAnalyzer Recipe rSpecific rGeneric
        Integer i iSize
        Boolean bSpecific bGeneric bHit
        String sTxt sMatch

        Move (Lowercase(sMsgTxt)) to sTxt
        Move False to bSpecific
        Move False to bGeneric
        Move (SizeOfArray(aRecipes) -1) to iSize
        For i from 0 to iSize
            If (aRecipes[i].iMsgNumber = iMsgNo) Begin
                If (aRecipes[i].sMatchText = "") Begin
                    If (bGeneric = False) Begin
                        Move aRecipes[i] to rGeneric
                        Move True to bGeneric
                    End
                End
                Else Begin
                    Move (Lowercase(aRecipes[i].sMatchText)) to sMatch
                    // A substring, or a whole symbol for a 'symbol: X' text - see MatchTextHits (2026-09-14).
                    Get MatchTextHits sTxt sMatch to bHit
                    If (bSpecific = False and bHit = True) Begin
                        Move aRecipes[i] to rSpecific
                        Move True to bSpecific
                    End
                End
            End
        Loop

        // A function-less SPECIFIC must not SHADOW a function-bearing GENERIC - mirror
        // MatchingRecipesFromList, so the triage GRID classifies a message the SAME way
        // Auto Select will act on it. The install-catalog scan seeds a function-less
        // Explain specific for every obsolete command (e.g. "...: Type. Use Struct/
        // End_Struct"), which would otherwise show the Type-family 4532 as Explain/no-fix
        // in the grid while Auto Select runs TypeToStructReport (generic 4532 | Suggest) -
        // a confusing split (Nils 2026-06-11). Prefer the function-bearing generic.
        If (bSpecific = True) Begin
            If ((Trim(rSpecific.sRefactorFn) = "") and bGeneric and (Trim(rGeneric.sRefactorFn) <> "")) ;
                Function_Return rGeneric
            // A matched recipe with NO refactor function can't be "applied" - it is
            // informational, so present it as Explain, never Review/Auto-fix (keeps
            // function-less compiler errors out of the actionable "review" list).
            If (Trim(rSpecific.sRefactorFn) = "") Begin
                Move eModExplain to rSpecific.eCategory
                Get CategoryText eModExplain to rSpecific.sCategoryText
            End
            Get _AppendCompilerText rSpecific sMsgTxt to rSpecific
            Function_Return rSpecific
        End
        If (bGeneric = True) Begin
            If (Trim(rGeneric.sRefactorFn) = "") Begin
                Move eModExplain to rGeneric.eCategory
                Get CategoryText eModExplain to rGeneric.sCategoryText
            End
            Get _AppendCompilerText rGeneric sMsgTxt to rGeneric
            Function_Return rGeneric
        End

        /* Nothing matched this number - Unknown. SHOW WHAT THE COMPILER SAID (Nils 2026-07-28).

           The hint used to be the fixed line "No Refactoring Function yet - review manually", which
           tells the reader only what the Unknown CATEGORY already says, and throws away the one
           thing that would help: the compiler's own sentence about that line. A row reading
           "W 4545 cWSFMAudit.pkg (183) No Refactoring Function yet" against a source line of
           "Integer Mono" inside a Struct is unreadable - there is no recipe for 4545 anywhere in
           this product, so OUR text can add nothing, while DataFlex's text is the whole answer.

           sMsgTxt is tCompileError.sErrorTxt: the message with the "ON LINE: n" tail already
           stripped by the parser, so it is clean prose and carries no number/file/line to duplicate
           the badge beside it. Falls back to the old wording when the compiler gave us nothing. */
        Move iMsgNo to Recipe.iMsgNumber
        Move eModUnknown to Recipe.eCategory
        If (Trim(sMsgTxt) <> "") ;
            Move (Trim(sMsgTxt)) to Recipe.sHint
        Else ;
            Move "No Refactoring Function yet - review manually." to Recipe.sHint
        Move "" to Recipe.sRefactorFn
        Get CategoryText eModUnknown to Recipe.sCategoryText
        Function_Return Recipe
    End_Function

    // Source-guard a recipe against the actual source line: a line-based refactor only applies if its
    // obsolete construct is really ON that line. Two guards:
    //   A) obsolete-function auto-fix ("use of X" - the MARK$AS$OBSOLETEUNICODE phrasing): the line
    //      must contain X (e.g. ToOEM for UnicodeOemAnsiFunctions).
    //   B) legacy-indicator review (ChangeLegacyIndicators): the line must carry a bracket indicator
    //      or an Indicate command - mirrors the function's own gate (HasBracket / bHasIndicateCommand).
    // If the construct isn't on the line, it sits inside a macro/command expansion - the warning fires
    // at the call site (File_Exist_OEM's body calls ToOEM; Deferred_View's body has [Found ~Found]),
    // where the line-based fix would no-op. Demote those to Explain (fix at the definition). Pure (no
    // file I/O): the caller passes the source line, keeping this unit-testable.
    Function VerifyAutoFix tModAnalyzer Recipe String sSourceLine Returns tModAnalyzer
        String sMatch sGuard sFn sLC sPinName sPinWhy sPinFile sPinAt sMasked sLiftKind sLiftName
        String[] asPinnedSet asWhy asLifted asLiftIncs
        Boolean bGuarded bOnLine bApplies bMore bSkip
        Integer iLeft iRight iAt iFrom iNext
        tTokenizer TD

        Move (Trim(Recipe.sMatchText)) to sMatch
        Move (Lowercase(Trim(Recipe.sRefactorFn))) to sFn
        Move (Lowercase(sSourceLine)) to sLC
        Move False to bGuarded
        Move False to bOnLine
        // PINNED indicator (Nils 2026-07-09): the three indicator converters now leave a GROUP/ALL
        // participant - or a name read by a 'use'd include - ENTIRELY legacy, so an Auto-fix / Review row
        // for it would promise a fix that never runs. Demote to Explain with the reason. The caller
        // (ModAnalyzerFT LoadData + PlanFromMessages) computes + sets pasPinnedIndicators when it reads
        // the guard file, so the GRID and the PLAN agree. No-op when nothing is pinned (empty set).
        // (This is the ONE spot that makes VerifyAutoFix stateful - it tokenizes the guard line + reads the
        // pinned set; a unit test with no pin set is unaffected.)
        // PERF (Nils 2026-07-17, the 41k-message WinServ plan: "source guards 103s"): the check ran a
        // FULL tokenizer pass on EVERY guarded indicator message before asking whether anything is even
        // pinned - and the pinned set is EMPTY for most files. An empty set can never pin, so gate the
        // tokenize on a non-empty set; behaviour is identical, one lexer pass per message saved.
        If (sFn = "changelegacyindicators" or sFn = "changesquarebracketsindicators" or sFn = "changeindicatortoboolean") Begin
            Get pasPinnedIndicators of ghoFuncLib to asPinnedSet
            If (SizeOfArray(asPinnedSet) > 0) Begin
                Send ClearFuncLibProperties of ghoFuncLib
                Get Tokenizer of ghoFuncLib sSourceLine to TD
                Get _PinnedIndicatorOnLine of ghoFuncLib TD to sPinName
                If (sPinName <> "") Begin
                    Move eModExplain to Recipe.eCategory
                    Get CategoryText eModExplain to Recipe.sCategoryText
                    /* NAME THE REASON AND THE FILE (Nils 2026-07-30). This hint used to state BOTH
                       reasons as an either/or and lead with the GROUP/ALL one - which is the rare
                       case ("I think I have ever seen it a handful of times"). So on nearly every
                       real row it opened with the wrong explanation, and a correct verdict read as a
                       misfire: he reported 'Indicate not_hist_sch true' as wrongly identified, in a
                       file containing no group Indicate at all - it was pinned because a shared .MAC
                       that 19 programs include reads [NOT_HIST_SCH].

                       The reason has been known since the pin was computed; it just was not carried.
                       Empty reason (the pinned set was Set directly, or by an older path) falls back
                       to the old either/or wording rather than guessing which one applies. */
                    Get _PinnedIndicatorReason of ghoFuncLib sPinName to sPinWhy
                    If (Left(sPinWhy, 4) = "USE|") Begin
                        /* "USE|<file>[|<why>...]" (2026-09-14): since the include lift, an include that CAN
                           convert with the run pins nothing, so a pin that remains carries WHY that include
                           cannot - MACRO, NESTED, PINS|<name>, USER|<file>, HOLDS|<file>|<name>, VIA|<file>
                           (the contract is the block above _LiftIncludeVerdict). The hint says what would
                           let it. The old "convert both in the same run", which no row could act on, is
                           kept only for a pin computed with no run context. */
                        Move (StrSplitToArray(sPinWhy, "|")) to asWhy
                        Move "" to sPinFile
                        Move "" to sLiftKind
                        If (SizeOfArray(asWhy) > 1) ;
                            Move asWhy[1] to sPinFile
                        If (SizeOfArray(asWhy) > 2) ;
                            Move asWhy[2] to sLiftKind
                        Move ("Left legacy on purpose: " + sPinName + " is also read by '" + sPinFile + "', a local include this" ;
                            + " run does not convert - renaming it here would leave that read pointing at a name that no longer" ;
                            + " exists.") to Recipe.sHint
                        If (sLiftKind = "MACRO") Begin
                            Move (Recipe.sHint + " " + sPinFile + " carries macro code (a #COMMAND body, a #DATA block or a '!' directive" ;
                                + " line), which is never refactored, so it cannot convert with the run - port that include by hand," ;
                                + " or leave both legacy.") to Recipe.sHint
                        End
                        Else If (sLiftKind = "NESTED") Begin
                            Move (Recipe.sHint + " " + sPinFile + " has local includes of its own, and the run follows one level only -" ;
                                + " convert that chain by hand, or leave both legacy.") to Recipe.sHint
                        End
                        Else If (sLiftKind = "PINS" and SizeOfArray(asWhy) > 3) Begin
                            Move (Recipe.sHint + " " + sPinFile + " keeps " + asWhy[3] + " legacy for a reason of its own (a group line" ;
                                + " the converter does not write, a directive, an odd name), so it cannot convert with the run.") to Recipe.sHint
                        End
                        Else If (sLiftKind = "USER" and SizeOfArray(asWhy) > 3) Begin
                            Move (Recipe.sHint + " '" + asWhy[3] + "' uses " + sPinFile + " too and is not in this run - run both" ;
                                + " programs together and the include converts with them.") to Recipe.sHint
                        End
                        Else If (sLiftKind = "HOLDS" and SizeOfArray(asWhy) > 4) Begin
                            Move (Recipe.sHint + " '" + asWhy[3] + "' uses " + sPinFile + " too and keeps " + asWhy[4] + " legacy for a" ;
                                + " reason of its own, so the include cannot change its read - resolve that first, or leave both" ;
                                + " legacy.") to Recipe.sHint
                        End
                        Else If (sLiftKind = "VIA" and SizeOfArray(asWhy) > 3) Begin
                            Move (Recipe.sHint + " " + sPinFile + " shares its names with '" + asWhy[3] + "', another include of the" ;
                                + " same program, which cannot convert with the run - the two go together.") to Recipe.sHint
                        End
                        Else If (sLiftKind = "EXPR" and SizeOfArray(asWhy) > 3) Begin
                            Move (Recipe.sHint + " " + sPinFile + " reads " + asWhy[3] + " as a plain Boolean expression ('If (X)'), a" ;
                                + " form no line converter rewrites and an include's own pass has no declaration to rename by - so it" ;
                                + " cannot convert with the run. Write that read as '[X]' first and run again, or leave both legacy.") to Recipe.sHint
                        End
                        Else Begin
                            Move (Recipe.sHint + " Convert " + sPinFile + " and every program that uses it in the same run, or leave" ;
                                + " both legacy.") to Recipe.sHint
                        End
                    End
                    /* CITE THE GROUP LINE (Nils 2026-07-30). He read this verdict as flatly false on
                       'Indicate franvaro_stamp False' and asked for the GROUP/ALL case to be removed.
                       It was correct: the same report file carried 'Indicate felaktig_stamp GROUP ANY
                       [franvaro_stamp ...]' 199 lines further down, indented 40 columns in. One group
                       line pins 4 names and each is used on many lines, so one easily-missed line
                       produces a spray of rows that all look unfounded.

                       Naming the reason without saying where to check it just moves the guesswork -
                       the same gap the include case had before it started naming its file. Older
                       reasons carry no number ("GROUP|"); those still read correctly, just without
                       the citation. */
                    Else If (Left(sPinWhy, 6) = "GROUP|") Begin
                        Move (Mid(sPinWhy, (Length(sPinWhy) - 6), 7)) to sPinAt
                        If (sPinAt <> "") ;
                            Move (" at line " + sPinAt) to sPinAt
                        Move ("Left legacy on purpose: " + sPinName + " belongs to the GROUP/ALL Indicate" + sPinAt + " in this" ;
                            + " file, a group line the converter does not write (a bracket or a legacy condition in front of it," ;
                            + " or a ';' continuation). Converting it here would leave that line referring to a name that no" ;
                            + " longer exists. Simplify that line so the converter takes it, modernise the whole group by" ;
                            + " hand, or leave all of its indicators legacy.") to Recipe.sHint
                    End
                    /* The three other pin reasons (2026-09-07's macro-body, directive and odd-name pins) had
                       no wording of their own and fell into the GROUP/include sentence below - Nils read
                       "used in GROUP/ALL Indicate" on a plain 'Indicate is$done false' (2026-09-13).
                       Each names its own cause and cites the line the pin came from. */
                    Else If (Left(sPinWhy, 8) = "ODDNAME|") Begin
                        Move (Mid(sPinWhy, (Length(sPinWhy) - 8), 9)) to sPinAt
                        If (sPinAt <> "") ;
                            Move (" (line " + sPinAt + ")") to sPinAt
                        Move ("Left legacy on purpose: " + sPinName + "'s declaration" + sPinAt + " spells a name the converters do" ;
                            + " not carry into a Boolean ('$' and '#' they do; anything else they leave). Rename the indicator by" ;
                            + " hand at its declaration and every use, then run again - or leave it legacy.") to Recipe.sHint
                    End
                    Else If (Left(sPinWhy, 6) = "MACRO|") Begin
                        Move (Mid(sPinWhy, (Length(sPinWhy) - 6), 7)) to sPinAt
                        If (sPinAt <> "") ;
                            Move (" at line " + sPinAt) to sPinAt
                        Move ("Left legacy on purpose: " + sPinName + " is read or set inside a #COMMAND body" + sPinAt + ", which no" ;
                            + " converter rewrites - a Boolean here would leave the macro's expansions referring to an indicator" ;
                            + " that no longer exists. Port the macro by hand first, or leave all of its indicators legacy.") to Recipe.sHint
                    End
                    Else If (Left(sPinWhy, 10) = "DIRECTIVE|") Begin
                        Move (Mid(sPinWhy, (Length(sPinWhy) - 10), 11)) to sPinAt
                        If (sPinAt <> "") ;
                            Move (" at line " + sPinAt) to sPinAt
                        Move ("Left legacy on purpose: " + sPinName + " is read on a raw compiler directive" + sPinAt + " (the" ;
                            + " '!' form), which takes an indicator and nothing else. Rewrite that directive by hand, or leave the" ;
                            + " indicator legacy.") to Recipe.sHint
                    End
                    Else Begin
                        Move ("Left legacy on purpose: " + sPinName + " is used in a GROUP/ALL Indicate the converter does not" ;
                            + " write, or is read by a 'use'd include that is not converted - converting it here would break that" ;
                            + " reference. Modernise the group / include by hand.") to Recipe.sHint
                    End
                    Move "" to Recipe.sRefactorFn
                    Function_Return Recipe
                End
            End
            /* THE ROW THAT CONVERTS says which include converts with it (2026-09-14). A name whose only
               outside reader is an include the run lifts is NOT pinned, so the row stays as the recipe
               says - and a reader who remembers the old "read by X.mac" demotion deserves to hear where
               that read went. Gated on the lifted list being non-empty at all, which is rare. */
            If (sPinName = "") Begin
                Get pasLiftedNames of ghoFuncLib to asLifted
                If (SizeOfArray(asLifted) > 0) Begin
                    Send ClearFuncLibProperties of ghoFuncLib
                    Get Tokenizer of ghoFuncLib sSourceLine to TD
                    Get _IndicatorOnLineInList of ghoFuncLib TD asLifted to sLiftName
                    If (sLiftName <> "") Begin
                        Get pasLiftedNameIncs of ghoFuncLib to asLiftIncs
                        Move (SearchArray(sLiftName, asLifted)) to iAt
                        If (iAt >= 0 and iAt < SizeOfArray(asLiftIncs)) ;
                            Move (Trim(Recipe.sHint) + " The local include '" + asLiftIncs[iAt] + "' reads " + sLiftName ;
                                + " and converts with the run.") to Recipe.sHint
                    End
                End
            End
        End
        // Deep Scan AUTO-FIX row (pseudo msg number 9001+): a DS rule's category is the catalog's
        // STATIC default for the symbol, not a per-line guarantee - the converter can decline the
        // hit line's exact shape (a no-'to' Pos = the [Found] side-effect form, an If Not whose
        // operand is followed by a legacy operator, a malformed paren split). A DS recipe carries
        // no match-text, so none of the keyword guards below apply - the honesty check IS the
        // dry-run: run the mapped converter against the real line; unchanged = demote to Explain
        // instead of promising an auto-fix the plan run would no-op on (the "same rows survive the
        // run" complaint). The hint names the two honest outcomes: other selected refactors may
        // reshape the line so a RE-SCAN converts it next pass (the convergent scan->fix loop), or
        // it is a hand fix. Line-by-line functions only - AutoFixChangesLine returns True (keep,
        // never demote on doubt) for a whole-file converter, whose Object declaration the scanner
        // already shape-verified (eDSClassDecl matches the converters' exact form). DS Review/
        // Explain rows fall through unchanged. (Nils 2026-07-16, Deep Scan auto-fix honesty pass.)
        If (Recipe.iMsgNumber >= CI_DeepScanFirstDSNumber and Recipe.eCategory = eModAutoFix and sFn <> "") Begin
            Get AutoFixChangesLine of ghoFuncLib sFn sSourceLine to bApplies
            If (bApplies = True) ;
                Function_Return Recipe
            Move eModExplain to Recipe.eCategory
            Get CategoryText eModExplain to Recipe.sCategoryText
            /* Say WHY when the line itself tells. Parentheses that do not balance - a dropped or doubled one
               the old compiler let through - are the common case, and "fix it by hand" alone left the user
               with an Ignore-all button and no idea what to fix (Nils 2026-09-05). ONE stray ')' on a
               keyword-led line no longer arrives here (ChangeLegacyOperators repairs that shape itself);
               what does is genuinely hand work, so name the count. */
            Get _MaskedCodeLine of ghoFuncLib sSourceLine to sMasked
            Move 0 to iLeft
            Get _NumberOfParanthesis of ghoFuncLib sMasked (&iLeft) to iRight
            If (iLeft <> iRight) ;
                Move ("Not auto-fixable as written: its parentheses do not balance - " + String(iLeft) + " opening against " + String(iRight) + " closing, a dropped or doubled parenthesis the old compiler let through. Balance them by hand (click the file to open the line); the next Check then offers the operators for conversion.") to Recipe.sHint
            Else ;
                Move ("Not auto-fixable in this exact shape - a dry-run of " + Trim(Recipe.sRefactorFn) + " leaves the line unchanged. Other selected refactors may reshape it: re-scan after the plan run; if it persists, fix it by hand.") to Recipe.sHint
            Move "" to Recipe.sRefactorFn
            Function_Return Recipe
        End
        // Guard A - obsolete-function row. ACTIONABLE (Auto-fix OR Review), not auto-fix only: see
        // Guard D and RecipeNeedsSourceGuard - a Review row attributed to a macro invocation line
        // points the user at code that does not contain the construct.
        If ((Recipe.eCategory = eModAutoFix or Recipe.eCategory = eModSuggest) and Lowercase(Left(sMatch, 7)) = "use of ") Begin
            Move (Trim(Right(sMatch, Length(sMatch) - 7))) to sGuard
            If (sGuard <> "") Begin
                Move True to bGuarded
                Move (Pos(Lowercase(sGuard), sLC) > 0) to bOnLine
            End
        End
        // Guard B - legacy-indicator USAGE review. BOTH usage functions fix the same [bracket] /
        // Indicate constructs, so both guard the SAME way: the warned line must carry a "[" or "Indicate".
        If (sFn = "changelegacyindicators" or sFn = "changesquarebracketsindicators") Begin
            Move True to bGuarded
            Move ((Pos("[", sLC) > 0) or (Pos("indicate", sLC) > 0)) to bOnLine
            // ...or the line IS an 'Ind' command - Indicate's obsolete spelling (dafmac.pkg expands it
            // to Indicate), which ChangeLegacyIndicators converts since 2026-09-20. Without this the
            // line was "inside a macro" and the board said "not yours to change" of the user's own
            // 'ind gbFlag True' (Nils). Command position only: 'ind' is a common word otherwise.
            If (bOnLine = False) ;
                Move (Left(LTrim(Replaces(Character(9), sLC, " ")), 4) = "ind ") to bOnLine
        End
        // Guard C - legacy Indicator DECLARATION (ChangeIndicatorToBoolean owns 4538). The warned line
        // must carry the "Indicator" keyword; a 4538 that fires inside a macro expansion (its call-site
        // line has no literal "Indicator") demotes to Explain. ("indicate" is NOT a substring of
        // "indicator", so Guard B and Guard C never cross-match.)
        If (sFn = "changeindicatortoboolean") Begin
            Move True to bGuarded
            Move (Pos("indicator", sLC) > 0) to bOnLine
        End
        // Guard D - obsolete-COMMAND auto-fix (the "command: X" recipe family: ChangeMidCommandToFunction,
        // ChangeCalcToMoveStatement, ChangeLeft/Insert/ReplaceCommandToFunction, and the rest). The warned
        // line must contain the command keyword X. When a 4531/4533 fires inside a macro / #COMMAND expansion
        // the compiler attributes EVERY expanded warning to the INVOCATION line (e.g.
        // "SETT_UDDA_AAR trans_datum to udda_aar", usually with an (xN) count) - which has no literal X - so
        // the line-based refactor would no-op there. Demote to Explain (fix it at the macro definition).
        // Mirrors Guard A's "use of X".
        // ACTIONABLE (Auto-fix OR Review), not auto-fix only (Nils 2026-07-26): a REVIEW row is just
        // as misattributed. LegacyApp's Deferred_View macro body holds 'Gosub Create_!1', so the
        // 4533 "command: Gosub" fired at every deferred-view INVOCATION and produced a Review row
        // against 'Object <name> is a cTrDbView' - a line with no Gosub on it, and nothing the
        // user can act on there. The dry-run below stays auto-fix-only: a Review row promises no
        // automatic fix, so there is nothing to dry-run.
        If ((Recipe.eCategory = eModAutoFix or Recipe.eCategory = eModSuggest) and Lowercase(Left(sMatch, 9)) = "command: ") Begin
            Move (Trim(Right(sMatch, Length(sMatch) - 9))) to sGuard
            If (sGuard <> "") Begin
                Move True to bGuarded
                Move (Pos(Lowercase(sGuard), sLC) > 0) to bOnLine
            End
        End
        // For the obsolete-COMMAND auto-fix family the keyword being ON the line is necessary but NOT
        // sufficient: the line-by-line converter can still decline it (a no-"to" Pos = [Found] side-effect;
        // an already-fixed line). DRY-RUN the real function - if it leaves the line unchanged the fix can't
        // apply here, so drop bOnLine to fall through to the Explain demote below. The function is its own
        // detector, so this tracks new forms automatically (e.g. the in-place Uppercase/Lowercase support -
        // "uppercase sVar" now CHANGES, so it stays Auto-fix). Only fires when a keyword-bearing command
        // recipe would otherwise be trusted; ghoFuncLib isolates the probe (error state + props). (Nils
        // 2026-07-01: LegacyApp Run Plan left no-"to" Pos "Auto-fix" but never cleared it.)
        If (Recipe.eCategory = eModAutoFix and Lowercase(Left(sMatch, 9)) = "command: " and bOnLine = True) Begin
            Get AutoFixChangesLine of ghoFuncLib sFn sSourceLine to bApplies
            Move bApplies to bOnLine
        End
        // Indicator USAGE auto-fix (if [X] / if not [X], msgs 4535/4538 now Auto-fix): Guard B's [ / Indicate on
        // the line is necessary but NOT sufficient - a SELECT / non-refactorable indicator makes the converter
        // no-op (it skips those), and only ChangeLegacyIndicators handles 'not [X]'. DRY-RUN the real function; if
        // it leaves the line unchanged the fix can't apply here, so demote to Explain (no phantom auto-fix).
        // Mirrors the command family above. (Nils 2026-07-02: 'indicators in If statements' promoted to Auto-fix.)
        If (Recipe.eCategory = eModAutoFix and (sFn = "changelegacyindicators" or sFn = "changesquarebracketsindicators") and bOnLine = True) Begin
            Get AutoFixChangesLine of ghoFuncLib sFn sSourceLine to bApplies
            Move bApplies to bOnLine
        End
        // Guard E - the error-52 repair (SplitCommandOutOfCondition, 2026-09-05). Its recipe says
        // Auto-fix because the shape it repairs is exactly what the compiler reports; a 52 of another
        // shape (a stray operator, 'Get Func() to var') goes back to Explain with the general advice
        // this message used to carry - not the macro wording below, which would be false here.
        If (sFn = "splitcommandoutofcondition") Begin
            Get AutoFixChangesLine of ghoFuncLib sFn sSourceLine to bApplies
            If (bApplies = True) ;
                Function_Return Recipe
            Move eModExplain to Recipe.eCategory
            Get CategoryText eModExplain to Recipe.sCategoryText
            Move CS_ModExpr52Hint to Recipe.sHint
            Move "" to Recipe.sRefactorFn
            Function_Return Recipe
        End
        /* Guard F - the legacy type-name CAST (ChangeLegacyTypeCast, 2026-09-14). Its 4328 recipes are
           keyed on the type's INTERNAL name (LONGPTR, DATETIME, ...) and, as plain substrings,
           'DATETIME' fired on "Undefined symbol in argument DWHIGHDATETIME" - a bare Type member on an
           'Add 10 to dwHighDateTime' line - which the plan offered as a cast rewrite and the run found
           nothing to rewrite (Nils, a real workspace). The tags say 'symbol: <type>' now, which the
           matcher takes whole; this is the second half: the line must carry the type's SOURCE name
           (Handle for LONGPTR) as
           a whole word followed, after any spaces, by '('. Otherwise the row is a plain undefined
           symbol and takes the generic 4328 wording - not the macro sentence below, false here. */
        If ((Recipe.eCategory = eModAutoFix or Recipe.eCategory = eModSuggest) and sFn = "changelegacytypecast") Begin
            Get CastSourceName sMatch to sGuard
            Move True to bGuarded
            Move False to bOnLine
            Move 1 to iFrom
            Move (sGuard <> "") to bMore
            While (bMore = True)
                Get _FindKeywordPos of ghoFuncLib sLC sGuard True iFrom to iAt
                If (iAt = 0) Begin
                    Move False to bMore
                End
                Else Begin
                    Move (iAt + Length(sGuard)) to iNext
                    Move True to bSkip
                    While (bSkip = True)
                        If (iNext > Length(sLC)) Begin
                            Move False to bSkip
                        End
                        Else If (Mid(sLC, 1, iNext) <> " ") Begin
                            Move False to bSkip
                        End
                        Else Begin
                            Increment iNext
                        End
                    Loop
                    If (iNext <= Length(sLC)) Begin
                        If (Mid(sLC, 1, iNext) = "(") Begin
                            Move True to bOnLine
                            Move False to bMore
                        End
                    End
                    If (bMore = True) ;
                        Move (iAt + 1) to iFrom
                End
            Loop
            If (bOnLine = False) Begin
                Move eModExplain to Recipe.eCategory
                Get CategoryText eModExplain to Recipe.sCategoryText
                Move CS_Mod4328Hint to Recipe.sHint
                Move "" to Recipe.sRefactorFn
                Function_Return Recipe
            End
        End
        If (bGuarded = False) ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        If (bOnLine = True) ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        // The construct isn't on this line -> it's inside a macro/command -> demote to Explain.
        Move eModExplain to Recipe.eCategory
        Get CategoryText eModExplain to Recipe.sCategoryText
        Move CS_ModMacroExpansionHint to Recipe.sHint
        Move "" to Recipe.sRefactorFn
        Function_Return Recipe
    End_Function

    /* A DFRefactor SKIP-MARKER in the source is a converter's own recorded verdict: it already saw
       this construct, refused it, and wrote the reason above or beside it. Re-offering that line as
       an Auto-fix promises a fix that can never land - the converter declines again, nothing
       changes, and the row returns on the next Check for ever (Nils 2026-07-26: "keeps nagging
       about ChangeDfTimerTocTimer, although a comment has already been placed above the timer
       object").

       The COMPILER cannot know this. 'Object oCounter is a DFTimer' still raises 4537 no matter
       what comment sits above it, so { ModRecipe = 4537 | class: DFTIMER } kept matching. Deep Scan
       has honoured these markers since 2026-07-11/12 ("the marker in the source IS the outcome") -
       this is that same rule on the compiler-message path, which never got it.

       DEMOTED to Explain rather than dropped: the warning is real - the class IS still obsolete -
       so it belongs in Review carrying the converter's own words. It just must never enter a plan
       (PlanFromSavedRows admits AutoFix and Suggest, so Explain is what keeps it out).

       Two placements, read exactly as cDeepScanner reads them:
         TRAILING (same line): '... // TODO (DFRefactor): NOT converted - <reason>'
         ABOVE (own comment):  '//TODO dfTimer->cTimer (skipped): <reason>'
       asLines is 0-based and iLine is 1-based (see GuardLineForStatement), so the warned line is
       asLines[iLine - 1] and the line above it asLines[iLine - 2]. */
    /* Fills pasLibRoots / pasLibNames for the loaded workspace, once. Cheap after the first call;
       a workspace switch re-keys and recomputes. */
    Procedure RefreshLibraryRoots
        tLibraryInfo[] aLibs
        Handle ho
        String sWS sSeen sRoot
        String[] asRoots asNames asEmpty asSelected asSelRoots
        Integer i iMax

        Get psLibRootsKey to sSeen
        /* A key beginning with '*' means the roots were SUPPLIED directly rather than computed -
           never recompute over them. A real key is a lowercased .sws path and can never start with
           '*' (illegal in a Windows filename), so this cannot collide.

           It exists because the fixture seeds pasLibRoots/pasLibNames, and matching the derived key
           by hand is not reliable: whether ghoApplication answers psSWSFile at all depends on which
           program hosts the test, so the seed could be silently recomputed away and the test then
           failed for a reason unrelated to what it checks. */
        If (Left(sSeen, 1) = "*") ;
            Procedure_Return
        Get psSWSFile of ghoApplication to sWS
        If (Lowercase(sWS) = sSeen) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Set pasLibRoots   to asEmpty
        Set pasLibNames   to asEmpty
        Set psLibRootsKey to (Lowercase(sWS))
        // The selected folders ride with the roots (same key, same lifetime).
        Get pasSelectedFolders of ghoApplication to asSelected   // the LIVE full-path cache; "SelectedFolders" is a Register_Function stub with no body (err 98 on the first live run)
        Move (SizeOfArray(asSelected) - 1) to iMax
        Move asEmpty to asSelRoots
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (Trim(asSelected[i]) <> "") ;
                Move (Lowercase(vFolderFormat(Trim(asSelected[i])))) to asSelRoots[SizeOfArray(asSelRoots)]
        Loop
        Set pasSelectedRoots to asSelRoots
        If (sWS = "") ;
            Procedure_Return

        /* giLibraryInventoryClass, not cLibraryInventory by name (2026-10-05): the plain class reads no
           libraries from a JSON workspace file, so on a DataFlex 26 workspace these roots stayed empty -
           no row was ever a library's (VerifyLibraryOwned), and the Libraries dialog counted no compiler
           message against any library (cFTLibraryReport asks LibraryOwningFile). */
        Get Create giLibraryInventoryClass to ho
        Get WorkspaceLibraryInventory of ho sWS "" to aLibs
        Move (SizeOfArray(aLibs) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            // The library's ROOT, not the folder holding its .sws - vwin32fh keeps its .sws in a
            // StudioLibrary subfolder while its packages sit one level up.
            Get LibraryRootFolder of ho aLibs[i].sFolder to sRoot
            Move (Lowercase(vFolderFormat(sRoot))) to asRoots[i]
            Move aLibs[i].sName to asNames[i]
        Loop
        Send Destroy of ho
        Set pasLibRoots to asRoots
        Set pasLibNames to asNames
    End_Procedure

    // The library a source file belongs to, or "" for the workspace's own code.
    Function LibraryOwningFile String sFilePath Returns String
        String[] asRoots asNames
        String sLow
        Integer i iMax

        If (Trim(sFilePath) = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        Send RefreshLibraryRoots
        Get pasLibRoots to asRoots
        Get pasLibNames to asNames
        Move (Lowercase(sFilePath)) to sLow
        Move (SizeOfArray(asRoots) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (Left(sLow, Length(asRoots[i])) = asRoots[i]) ;
                Function_Return asNames[i]
        Loop
        Function_Return ""
    End_Function

    /* A message from a LIBRARY file may never carry a refactor function.

       The compiler follows the selected program's include chain straight into vwin32fh, DUF and the
       rest, so their files produce messages like any other - and some of those messages match a
       recipe. Offering the fix would edit code that is maintained upstream: the next update to the
       library overwrites or conflicts with it, and nothing records that it happened. That is the one
       thing the library work has been consistent about, so the recipe is stripped and the row drops
       to Explain - the same move the pinned indicators make when a converter cannot act.

       NOT suppression. If a library's old build will not compile under the target, those messages
       are the single most important fact about the migration; hiding them would let the board read
       "clean" while the program does not build. The row stays, with its count, and says whose code
       it is and what to do about it - which is replace the library, not edit it.

       Applied by BOTH the grid (ModAnalyzerFT) and the plan (PlanFromMessages) immediately after the
       recipe is matched - before the source-guard machinery, so it covers every message rather than
       only the guarded ones, and so a stripped recipe skips that expensive guard entirely.

       Only touches rows that carry a function: a library message with no fix on offer is already
       harmless, and rewriting its hint would lose whatever it says. */
    // Is sFile under a folder the user SELECTED? (pasSelectedRoots - see the property.)
    Function IsUnderSelectedFolder String sFile Returns Boolean
        String[] asSel
        String sLow
        Integer i iMax

        If (Trim(sFile) = "") ;
            Function_Return False
        Send RefreshLibraryRoots
        Get pasSelectedRoots to asSel
        Move (Lowercase(sFile)) to sLow
        Move (SizeOfArray(asSel) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (asSel[i] <> "" and Left(sLow, Length(asSel[i])) = asSel[i]) ;
                Function_Return True
        Loop
        Function_Return False
    End_Function

    Function VerifyLibraryOwned tModAnalyzer Recipe String sFilePath Returns tModAnalyzer
        String sLib

        If (Trim(Recipe.sRefactorFn) = "") ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        Get LibraryOwningFile sFilePath to sLib
        If (sLib = "") ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        // A library the user SELECTED is theirs to change (Nils's rule, 2026-08-16) - only an
        // unselected library's row loses its function.
        If (IsUnderSelectedFolder(Self, sFilePath)) ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        Move eModExplain to Recipe.eCategory
        Get CategoryText eModExplain to Recipe.sCategoryText
        Move "" to Recipe.sRefactorFn
        Move ("This line belongs to the " + sLib + " library, which is maintained elsewhere - it is REPLACED, not refactored. " ;
            + "Refactoring it here would be overwritten by the next update to " + sLib + ". Use Libraries on the Target DataFlex " ;
            + "version tile to see whether a build for the target version exists.") to Recipe.sHint
        Function_Return Recipe
    End_Function

    /* ---- A MISSING INCLUDE FILE, AND THE LIBRARY IT IS IN (Nils 2026-10-05). ----

       A workspace's .sws listed one library. That library holds five more, each with a workspace file of
       its own, and none of the five was referenced: eight "Include file not found" errors, a struct that
       was never declared, and a compiler that crashed in what followed. The files were on the disk all
       along, two folders from where the compiler looked - and the row said what the compiler said.

       So the row says where the file is. The workspace's home and the roots of the libraries it references
       are swept once per analyze for source files (the shadowed-files report's sweep), the missing name is
       looked up in that, and the nearest workspace file above the hit names the library to add. A hit
       under a .sws the workspace already has means the FOLDER is off the source path instead, and the hint
       says that. Nothing found: the row keeps the hint it had.

       What it never looks at: anything outside those roots. A file of the same name in an unrelated
       workspace on the same drive is no answer, and a drive is not something to sweep. */

    // PURE. Does a lowercased path run through a folder a retire has moved aside - a name ending in
    // ".old", ".old2" ... (cShadowedPackageFiles.RetireTargetName's spelling)? Such a tree is no place to
    // point at.
    Function _PathHasRetiredFolder String sPathLc Returns Boolean
        String sRest sCh
        Integer iPos iAt

        Move sPathLc to sRest
        Move (Pos(".old", sRest)) to iPos
        While (iPos > 0)
            Move (iPos + 4) to iAt
            Move (Mid(sRest, 1, iAt)) to sCh
            While (sCh >= "0" and sCh <= "9" and iAt <= Length(sRest))
                Increment iAt
                Move (Mid(sRest, 1, iAt)) to sCh
            Loop
            If (sCh = "\") ;
                Function_Return True
            Move (Right(sRest, (Length(sRest) - iPos - 3))) to sRest
            Move (Pos(".old", sRest)) to iPos
        Loop
        Function_Return False
    End_Function

    /* PURE. The file an "Include file not found" message names: the last word of the text, without a
       folder in front of it. The compiler writes the name in capitals and the text in its own language
       ("Hittar ej fil att inkludera WORKSPC.PKG"), so the position is all there is to go by. "" when the
       last word is no file name - and for a table definition (.fd), which is generated, not found in a
       library, and has a recipe of its own. */
    Function MissingIncludeName String sMsgTxt Returns String
        String sName
        Integer iPos

        Move (Trim(sMsgTxt)) to sName
        Move (RightPos(" ", sName)) to iPos
        If (iPos > 0) ;
            Move (Right(sName, (Length(sName) - iPos))) to sName
        Move (RightPos("\", sName)) to iPos
        If (iPos = 0) ;
            Move (RightPos("/", sName)) to iPos
        If (iPos > 0) ;
            Move (Right(sName, (Length(sName) - iPos))) to sName
        If (Pos(".", sName) < 2 or Right(sName, 1) = ".") ;
            Function_Return ""
        If (Lowercase(Right(sName, 3)) = ".fd") ;
            Function_Return ""
        Function_Return sName
    End_Function

    /* PURE. Of the workspace files in one folder, the one for the target DataFlex version - a library
       usually keeps one per version, "cRegex-Library-DF191.sws" beside "cRegex-Library-DF260.sws". The
       name that holds the version, dotted ("26.0") or compact ("260"), wins; with none, the last in
       name order. "" for an empty list. */
    Function PickSwsForVersion String[] asSwsLeafs String sTargetVersion Returns String
        String sCompact sLow sPick
        Integer i iMax

        Move (SizeOfArray(asSwsLeafs) - 1) to iMax
        If (iMax < 0) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move (SortArray(asSwsLeafs)) to asSwsLeafs
        Move asSwsLeafs[iMax] to sPick
        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sTargetVersion))) to sTargetVersion
        If (sTargetVersion = "") ;
            Function_Return sPick
        Move (Replaces(".", sTargetVersion, "")) to sCompact
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (Lowercase(asSwsLeafs[i])) to sLow
            If ((sLow contains sTargetVersion) or (sLow contains sCompact)) ;
                Function_Return asSwsLeafs[i]
        Loop
        Function_Return sPick
    End_Function

    /* The workspace file of the library a source file belongs to, looked for from the file's own folder
       upwards: in the folder, then in its StudioLibrary subfolder (where vwin32fh and many others keep
       theirs), then the same one folder up, and so on up to and including sStopRoot. "" when there is
       none. Reads folder listings only. */
    Function LibrarySwsNear String sFile String sStopRoot String sTargetVersion Returns String
        tRFSearchResult[] aFound
        String[] asLeafs asNone
        String sFolder sStop sPick sTry sLeaf
        Integer iHop iPos i iLast iTry

        Move (Trim(sFile)) to sFolder
        Move (RightPos("\", sFolder)) to iPos
        If (iPos < 3) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move (Left(sFolder, (iPos - 1))) to sFolder
        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sStopRoot))) to sStop
        If (Right(sStop, 1) = "\") ;
            Move (Left(sStop, (Length(sStop) - 1))) to sStop
        For iHop from 0 to 8
            For iTry from 0 to 1
                Move (sFolder + "\") to sTry
                If (iTry = 1) ;
                    Move (sFolder + "\StudioLibrary\") to sTry
                Move (rfFileSearch((sTry + "*.sws"), DIRMODE_FILES_ONLY, False)) to aFound
                Move asNone to asLeafs
                Move (SizeOfArray(aFound) - 1) to iLast
                For i from 0 to iLast
                    Move (Trim(aFound[i].sFilename)) to sLeaf
                    // "*.sws" can answer "x.sws.lock" through its short name - a workspace file ends in .sws
                    If (Lowercase(Right(sLeaf, 4)) = ".sws") ;
                        Move sLeaf to asLeafs[SizeOfArray(asLeafs)]
                Loop
                Get PickSwsForVersion asLeafs sTargetVersion to sPick
                If (sPick <> "") ;
                    Function_Return (sTry + sPick)
            Loop
            // one folder up - unless this was the root the sweep started from
            If (Length(sFolder) <= Length(sStop)) ;
                Function_Return ""
            Move (RightPos("\", sFolder)) to iPos
            If (iPos < 3) ;
                Function_Return ""
            Move (Left(sFolder, (iPos - 1))) to sFolder
        Loop
        Function_Return ""
    End_Function

    /* One library entry of the workspace file - a path to the library's own .sws, as written - into the
       two lists the index is built from: the library's ROOT (its packages can sit a folder above a
       StudioLibrary that holds the .sws) and the folder of that .sws, lowercased, as "referenced". An
       entry that is no .sws path - a registry package name such as "Owner/Name#1.0.2" - adds nothing:
       its checkout is under the home's DfPkg folder, which is swept with the home. hoInv is any
       cLibraryInventory; only its path arithmetic is used. */
    Procedure _AddLibraryRootFor Handle hoInv String sWsFolder String sEntry String[] ByRef asRoots String[] ByRef asRefFolders
        String sLibSws sRoot
        Integer iAt

        Move (Replaces("/", Trim(sEntry), "\")) to sEntry
        If (Lowercase(Right(sEntry, 4)) <> ".sws") ;
            Procedure_Return
        Get _ResolvePath of hoInv sWsFolder sEntry to sLibSws
        Move (RightPos("\", sLibSws)) to iAt
        If (iAt < 3) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Get LibraryRootFolder of hoInv (Left(sLibSws, iAt)) to sRoot
        Move (vFolderFormat(sRoot)) to asRoots[SizeOfArray(asRoots)]
        Move (Lowercase(Left(sLibSws, iAt))) to asRefFolders[SizeOfArray(asRefFolders)]
    End_Procedure

    // Builds the missing-include index, once per object (see the property block and the note above).
    Procedure _EnsureMissingIncludeIndex
        tLibraryInfo[] aLibs
        tSwsFile TheSws          // not "Sws": that is sWS, the String below, to a compiler that ignores case
        Handle hoInv hoSweep
        String[] asRoots asRefFolders asFiles asLeafLc asFound
        Integer[] aiRoot
        String sWS sHome sRoot sTarget sLow sRootLc sWsFolder
        Integer i iMax j jMax iAt k iFormat
        Boolean bNested

        If (pbMissIncBuilt(Self) = True) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Set pbMissIncBuilt to True
        If (pbMissIncSeeded(Self) = True) Begin
            Get pasMissIncRoots to asRoots
        End
        Else Begin
            Get psHomeTarget of ghoApplication to sHome
            If (Trim(sHome) <> "") Begin
                Move (vFolderFormat(Trim(sHome))) to asRoots[0]
                Move (Lowercase(vFolderFormat(Trim(sHome)))) to asRefFolders[0]
            End
            Get psSWSFile of ghoApplication to sWS
            If (Trim(sWS) <> "") Begin
                // the workspace's own .sws is not a library to add
                Move "" to sWsFolder
                Move (RightPos("\", sWS)) to iAt
                If (iAt > 0) Begin
                    Move (Left(sWS, iAt)) to sWsFolder
                    Move (Lowercase(sWsFolder)) to asRefFolders[SizeOfArray(asRefFolders)]
                End
                Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventory)) to hoInv
                /* A JSON WORKSPACE FILE IS READ THROUGH THE APPLICATION (the same evening - the hint did not
                   show on the very workspace it was written for). cLibraryInventory answers "no libraries"
                   for a JSON .sws: its JSON reader lives in cLibraryInventoryApp, an application-side
                   subclass this package cannot name, because the test runner compiles this package too. So
                   for the DataFlex 26 format the only root was the home, and the missing files were under
                   the library next door. The path dependencies are taken from the application's own reader
                   here; an INI workspace file goes through the inventory as before. */
                Get SwsFormatOf of hoInv sWS to iFormat
                If (iFormat = eSwsJson) Begin
                    If (vFilePathExists(sWS) <> 0) Begin
                        Get ReadSwsJsonFile of ghoApplication sWS to TheSws
                        Move (SizeOfArray(TheSws.aDependencies) - 1) to iMax
                        For i from 0 to iMax
                            If (TheSws.aDependencies[i].bIsObject = False) ;
                                Send _AddLibraryRootFor hoInv sWsFolder TheSws.aDependencies[i].sPath (&asRoots) (&asRefFolders)
                        Loop
                    End
                End
                Else Begin
                    Get WorkspaceLibraryInventory of hoInv sWS "" to aLibs
                    Move (SizeOfArray(aLibs) - 1) to iMax
                    For i from 0 to iMax
                        If (Trim(aLibs[i].sFolder) <> "") Begin
                            Get LibraryRootFolder of hoInv aLibs[i].sFolder to sRoot
                            Move (vFolderFormat(sRoot)) to asRoots[SizeOfArray(asRoots)]
                            Move (Lowercase(vFolderFormat(Trim(aLibs[i].sFolder)))) to asRefFolders[SizeOfArray(asRefFolders)]
                        End
                    Loop
                End
                Send Destroy of hoInv
            End
            Get psTargetDfVersion of ghoApplication to sTarget
            Set pasMissIncRoots      to asRoots
            Set pasMissIncRefFolders to asRefFolders
            Set psMissIncTarget      to sTarget
        End

        Get Create (RefClass(cShadowedPackageFiles)) to hoSweep
        Move (SizeOfArray(asRoots) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (Lowercase(Trim(asRoots[i]))) to sRootLc
            // a root inside an earlier root was swept with it
            Move False to bNested
            For k from 0 to (i - 1)
                If (Trim(asRoots[k]) <> "" and Left(sRootLc, Length(Trim(asRoots[k]))) = Lowercase(Trim(asRoots[k]))) ;
                    Move True to bNested
            Loop
            If (sRootLc <> "" and bNested = False) Begin
                Get WorkspaceSourceFiles of hoSweep asRoots[i] to asFound
                Move (SizeOfArray(asFound) - 1) to jMax
                For j from 0 to jMax
                    Move (Lowercase(asFound[j])) to sLow
                    If (_PathHasRetiredFolder(Self, sLow) = False) Begin
                        Move (RightPos("\", sLow)) to iAt
                        Move asFound[j] to asFiles[SizeOfArray(asFiles)]
                        Move (Right(sLow, (Length(sLow) - iAt))) to asLeafLc[SizeOfArray(asLeafLc)]
                        Move i to aiRoot[SizeOfArray(aiRoot)]
                    End
                Loop
            End
        Loop
        Send Destroy of hoSweep
        Set pasMissIncFiles  to asFiles
        Set pasMissIncLeafLc to asLeafLc
        Set paiMissIncRoot   to aiRoot
    End_Procedure

    /* Where a missing include file is and what to do about it, as the row's hint - or "" when no file of
       that name is under the workspace or its libraries. Of several, the one in a library the workspace
       does not reference is taken first: that is the case with a one-step answer. */
    Function MissingIncludeHint String sLeaf Returns String
        String[] asFiles asLeafLc asRoots asRefFolders
        Integer[] aiRoot
        String sLeafLc sTarget sSws sFolder sBestFile sBestSws sHint
        Integer i iMax iAt iRoot iRank iBestRank iHits

        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sLeaf))) to sLeafLc
        If (sLeafLc = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        Send _EnsureMissingIncludeIndex
        Get pasMissIncFiles      to asFiles
        Get pasMissIncLeafLc     to asLeafLc
        Get paiMissIncRoot       to aiRoot
        Get pasMissIncRoots      to asRoots
        Get pasMissIncRefFolders to asRefFolders
        Get psMissIncTarget      to sTarget
        Move 0 to iHits
        Move 0 to iBestRank
        Move "" to sBestFile
        Move "" to sBestSws
        Move (SizeOfArray(asFiles) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (asLeafLc[i] = sLeafLc) Begin
                Increment iHits
                Move aiRoot[i] to iRoot
                Get LibrarySwsNear asFiles[i] asRoots[iRoot] sTarget to sSws
                // 3 = in a library the workspace does not reference; 2 = no workspace file near it;
                // 1 = under a workspace file it already has - the folder is off the source path
                Move 2 to iRank
                If (sSws <> "") Begin
                    Move (RightPos("\", sSws)) to iAt
                    If (SearchArray(Lowercase(Left(sSws, iAt)), asRefFolders) <> -1) ;
                        Move 1 to iRank
                    Else ;
                        Move 3 to iRank
                End
                If (iRank > iBestRank) Begin
                    Move iRank      to iBestRank
                    Move asFiles[i] to sBestFile
                    Move sSws       to sBestSws
                End
            End
        Loop
        If (iHits = 0) ;
            Function_Return ""

        Move (RightPos("\", sBestFile)) to iAt
        Move (Left(sBestFile, (iAt - 1))) to sFolder
        Move (Right(sBestFile, (Length(sBestFile) - iAt))) to sLeaf            // as the disk spells it
        If (iBestRank = 3) Begin
            Move (RightPos("\", sBestSws)) to iAt
            Move (sLeaf + " was found in " + sFolder + ", which belongs to the library " + Right(sBestSws, (Length(sBestSws) - iAt)) ;
                + " (in " + Left(sBestSws, (iAt - 1)) + "). This workspace does not reference that library, so the compiler cannot find the file. " ;
                + "Add the library to the workspace - Libraries, on the Target DataFlex version tile - and press Check again.") to sHint
        End
        Else Begin
            Move (sLeaf + " was found in " + sFolder + ", but that folder is not on the source path the compiler searches. " ;
                + "Add the folder to the source path in the Studio, or move the file to a folder that is on it, and press Check again.") to sHint
        End
        If (iHits > 1) ;
            Move (sHint + " (" + String(iHits) + " files of that name were found; this is the likeliest.)") to sHint
        Function_Return sHint
    End_Function

    /* The hook for the row builder: an "Include file not found" (4313) row that no converter owns gets
       the hint that says where the file is. A row with a function is left alone - WORKSPC.PKG and
       cCJReportControl.pkg are missing on purpose and have their own converters - and so is a row whose
       file is nowhere to be found. */
    Function VerifyMissingInclude tModAnalyzer Recipe Integer iMsgNo String sMsgTxt Returns tModAnalyzer
        String sLeaf sHint

        If (iMsgNo <> 4313) ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        If (Trim(Recipe.sRefactorFn) <> "") ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        Get MissingIncludeName sMsgTxt to sLeaf
        If (sLeaf = "") ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        Get MissingIncludeHint sLeaf to sHint
        If (sHint = "") ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        Move sHint to Recipe.sHint
        Function_Return Recipe
    End_Function

    /* THE SCAN'S VERDICT OUTRANKS ITS RULE'S DEFAULTS (Nils 2026-09-11: a plan run of "2 changes"
       changed 0 lines, and every Check offered it again).

       The Deep Scan decides some hits per LINE, not per rule: an object of a learned subclass that
       no converter acts on keeps its rule's DS number but loses the function (cDeepScanner.
       _SubclassMatchNote - ChangeDfTimerTocTimer takes only the literal dfTimer), and a hit inside a
       #COMMAND body is capped at Review. The recipe table is keyed by the DS number ALONE, so the
       analyzer re-classified such a hit from its rule and put the function back - an Auto-fix row
       for a run that can change nothing, on every Check.

       The verdict now travels with the message (tCompileError.eScanCategory / sScanFn / sScanHint,
       filled by HitsToMessages) and is applied here, at the same point in the grid and the plan,
       right after VerifyLibraryOwned. It only ever LOWERS: the function goes when the scan withheld
       it, the category can only drop, and the hint becomes the scan's - the rule's plus what the scan
       found on that line. A compiler message, and a scan message carrying no verdict, come back
       exactly as they went in. */
    Function VerifyScanVerdict tModAnalyzer Recipe tCompileError Msg Returns tModAnalyzer
        If (Msg.bDeepScan = False or Msg.eScanCategory < eModExplain) ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        If (Trim(Msg.sScanFn) = "" and Trim(Recipe.sRefactorFn) <> "") Begin
            Move "" to Recipe.sRefactorFn
            // A row with no function is neither Auto-fix nor Review, whatever category travelled.
            If (Recipe.eCategory > eModExplain) Begin
                Move eModExplain to Recipe.eCategory
                Get CategoryText eModExplain to Recipe.sCategoryText
            End
        End
        If (Msg.eScanCategory < Recipe.eCategory) Begin
            Move Msg.eScanCategory to Recipe.eCategory
            Get CategoryText Msg.eScanCategory to Recipe.sCategoryText
        End
        If (Trim(Msg.sScanHint) <> "") ;
            Move Msg.sScanHint to Recipe.sHint
        Function_Return Recipe
    End_Function

    /* A FUNCTION THE LAST PLAN RAN ON THIS FILE WITHOUT CHANGING IT IS NOT OFFERED AGAIN (Nils 2026-11-12:
       "After a new check a new refactoring plan is suggested with exactly the same three functions
       suggested again. So there is something wrong here.").

       asNoOpFns are the functions cPlanNoOps recorded for the message's file while it keeps the stamp it
       had (PlanNoOpFunctionsIn), lowercased. Such a row loses its function and drops to Explain, with the
       reason - in any category, because PlanFromMessages adds a function on sRefactorFn alone, and a
       Review row reaches the plan untried. Applied by BOTH lanes right after VerifyScanVerdict; keep the
       two in lock-step. PURE. */
    Function VerifyPlanNoOp tModAnalyzer Recipe String[] ByRef asNoOpFns Returns tModAnalyzer
        String sFn

        If (SizeOfArray(asNoOpFns) = 0) ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        Move (Trim(Recipe.sRefactorFn)) to sFn
        If (sFn = "") ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        If (SearchArray(Lowercase(sFn), asNoOpFns) = -1) ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        Move "" to Recipe.sRefactorFn
        If (Recipe.eCategory > eModExplain) Begin
            Move eModExplain to Recipe.eCategory
            Get CategoryText eModExplain to Recipe.sCategoryText
        End
        Move ("The last refactoring plan ran " + sFn + " on this file and the file did not change: " + sFn + " leaves this line as it is. Its run notes may say why; otherwise this is a hand fix. It is offered again once the file changes.") to Recipe.sHint
        Function_Return Recipe
    End_Function

    /* A ROW ON MACRO CODE IS NEVER OFFERED AS A FIX (Nils 2026-09-07: "no macro code should ever be
       refactored").

       Macro code is what the engine leaves as written whatever function runs (the library's macro-code guard): a
       '#COMMAND .. #ENDCOMMAND' body, a '#DATA .. #ENDDATA' block, and a compiler-directive line - one
       whose code starts with the exclamation mark. A row on such a line that names a converter promises a
       change the run will not make, so the FUNCTION is cleared, in every category:

       PlanFromMessages adds a function to the plan on sRefactorFn <> "" ALONE - there is no category test -
       so a Review row that still names its converter joins the plan exactly like an Auto-fix one (Nils
       2026-07-31: "the Run the refactoring plan button is still there and active"). Until 2026-10-04 this
       cleared Auto-fix rows only, from the days when the run did rewrite a macro body and a Review row was
       a person deciding to; after the engine stopped, a Review row on macro code ran a function that
       changed nothing ("0 lines Renamed", Nils 2026-10-04). The converter is named in the hint instead,
       and the category follows to Explain - a recipe with no function never reads Auto-fix or Review.

       Here and not only in the scanner, because a COMPILER message can land on macro code too
       (File_Exist_Oem's ToOem, reported inside the command body). Each kind of line is tested the
       engine's way (IsMacroDirectiveLine, and the block directives MacroCodeFlags reads), so the
       analyzer and the run agree on which lines are left alone. Applied by BOTH lanes right beside
       VerifySkipMarker, which has the same shape and the same reason to exist - keep the two in
       lock-step. */
    Function VerifyMacroBodyLine tModAnalyzer Recipe String[] ByRef asLines Integer iLine Returns tModAnalyzer
        String sLow sWhere
        Integer i
        Boolean bClosed
        Boolean[] abMacro

        If (Trim(Recipe.sRefactorFn) = "") ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        If (iLine < 1 or iLine > SizeOfArray(asLines)) ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        Move "" to sWhere
        If (IsMacroDirectiveLine(ghoFuncLib, asLines[iLine - 1])) ;
            Move "a compiler directive line" to sWhere
        // Walk back for the nearest block directive: an opening one before its closing one means the line
        // is inside that block. Only a line holding '#' can be a directive, so every other line costs one
        // Pos. (A countdown While - DataFlex's For has no step.)
        Move False to bClosed
        Move (iLine - 1) to i
        While (sWhere = "" and bClosed = False and i >= 0)
            If (Pos("#", asLines[i]) > 0) Begin
                Move (Lowercase(Trim(Replaces(Character(9), asLines[i], " ")))) to sLow
                If (Left(sLow, 11) = "#endcommand" or Left(sLow, 8) = "#enddata") Begin
                    Move True to bClosed
                End
                Else If (Left(sLow, 8) = "#command") Begin
                    Move "inside a #COMMAND body" to sWhere
                End
                Else If (sLow = "#data" or Left(sLow, 6) = "#data ") Begin
                    Move "inside a #DATA block" to sWhere
                End
            End
            Move (i - 1) to i
        Loop
        If (sWhere = "") ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        // The walk back does not see strings: the engine's own flags have the last word (2026-10-29). A #COMMAND
        // line in a multi-line string's text opens no block, so the code after that string is no macro code.
        Get MacroCodeFlags of ghoFuncLib (&asLines) to abMacro
        If (abMacro[iLine - 1] = False) ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        Move (Recipe.sHint + " This line is " + sWhere + ", and DFRefactor never changes macro code - " + Trim(Recipe.sRefactorFn) + " leaves it as it is. Change it by hand if you want it changed.") to Recipe.sHint
        Move "" to Recipe.sRefactorFn
        Move eModExplain to Recipe.eCategory
        Get CategoryText eModExplain to Recipe.sCategoryText
        Function_Return Recipe
    End_Function

    /* THE GRID/LIST CONVERTERS ONLY REWRITE AN OBJECT DECLARATION - so a message sitting on a
       SUBCLASS or an AUGMENTATION of the retired class must not offer them (Nils 2026-08-30, on
       E 4345/E 4328 U_DBLIST at "Class cMyDbList is a DBList": "It is just plain wrong. It is a
       legacy subclassing of the old dbList class").

       The row's BAND was right - DBList is gone from DF26, so a subclass of it genuinely breaks the
       build - but the fix offered beside it could never run. cDbListTocDbCJGridPromptList's own
       matcher opens with `If (Pos("object ", sTrimLower) <> 1) Function_Return False`, so
       `Class cMyDbList is a DBList` and `Procedure Clear_All for DBGrid` are both invisible to it.

       THE DEEP SCAN CATALOGUE ALREADY DREW THIS LINE - "dbList class" (eDSClassDecl) carries the
       converter, "dbList subclass" (eDSClassSubclass) is Explain with none. What had no equivalent
       was the COMPILER side: {ModRecipe = 4345 | U_DBLIST} and its 4328 twin match on message text
       alone, which cannot see the shape of the line the message landed on. This is that missing
       half, and it is the same demotion VerifyBeginRow already applies for the wrong FAMILY - there
       for a converter whose IsGridDeclaration never matches the class, here for one whose
       IsGridDeclaration never matches the LINE.

       The function name is CLEARED, not just the category downgraded - see VerifyMacroBodyLine
       above for why (PlanFromMessages adds on sRefactorFn <> "" alone). The converter is still
       named in the hint, because instances of a plain dbList elsewhere in the file DO convert; what
       is withdrawn is the promise that running it fixes THIS line. */
    Function VerifyLegacyClassShape tModAnalyzer Recipe String[] ByRef asLines Integer iLine Returns tModAnalyzer
        String sFn sLC sW1 sShape sTarget

        Move (Lowercase(Trim(Recipe.sRefactorFn))) to sFn
        If (sFn <> "refactordblisttocdbcjgridpromptlist" and sFn <> "refactordbgridtocdbcjgrid") ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        If (iLine < 1 or iLine > SizeOfArray(asLines)) ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        Move (Lowercase(Trim(asLines[iLine - 1]))) to sLC
        Get _NthWord of ghoFuncLib sLC 1 to sW1
        Move "" to sShape
        // "Class cMyDbList is a DBList" - a subclass of the retired class.
        If (sW1 = "class") ;
            Move "a SUBCLASS of the retired class" to sShape
        // "Procedure Clear_All for DBGrid" - an augmentation of the retired class.
        If ((sW1 = "procedure" or sW1 = "function") and (sLC contains " for ")) ;
            Move "an AUGMENTATION of the retired class" to sShape
        If (sShape = "") ;
            Function_Return Recipe

        Move "cDbCJGrid" to sTarget
        If (sFn = "refactordblisttocdbcjgridpromptlist") ;
            Move "cDbCJGridPromptList" to sTarget
        Move (Recipe.sHint + " This line is " + sShape + ", not an object declaration - " ;
            + Trim(Recipe.sRefactorFn) + " only rewrites 'Object x is a ...' and would not touch it." ;
            + " Port it to " + sTarget + " by hand; plain object instances elsewhere in the file still" ;
            + " convert with " + Trim(Recipe.sRefactorFn) + ".") to Recipe.sHint
        Move "" to Recipe.sRefactorFn
        Move eModExplain to Recipe.eCategory
        Get CategoryText eModExplain to Recipe.sCategoryText
        Function_Return Recipe
    End_Function

    Function VerifySkipMarker tModAnalyzer Recipe String[] ByRef asLines Integer iLine Returns tModAnalyzer
        String  sLow sRaw sReason
        Integer iPos
        Boolean bMarked bHolds

        If (iLine < 1 or iLine > SizeOfArray(asLines)) ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        Move False to bMarked
        Move ""    to sRaw
        // TRAILING marker, on the warned line itself.
        Move (Lowercase(asLines[iLine - 1])) to sLow
        If ((sLow contains "(dfrefactor)") and ((sLow contains "todo") or (sLow contains "note"))) Begin
            Move True to bMarked
            Move asLines[iLine - 1] to sRaw
        End
        // ABOVE, on its own comment line.
        If (bMarked = False and iLine > 1) Begin
            Move (Lowercase(Trim(asLines[iLine - 2]))) to sLow
            If (Left(sLow, 2) = "//" and ((sLow contains "todo") or (sLow contains "note"))) Begin
                If ((sLow contains "skipped") or (sLow contains "dfrefactor")) Begin
                    Move True to bMarked
                    Move asLines[iLine - 2] to sRaw
                End
            End
        End
        If (bMarked = False) ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        // THE POPUP CONVERTER'S OWN MARKER IS A QUESTION, NOT AN ANSWER (2026-09-13): the version that
        // refused wrote it, and the version classifying may take the shape by now. A dry run of the
        // object decides - it still refuses, the demotion below stands; it converts now, the recipe
        // keeps its offer and the plan run replaces the marker. The scan lane asks the same question.
        If ((Lowercase(sRaw)) contains "changefloatingpopupmenutocodejock left") Begin
            Get _CJPopupMarkerStillHolds of ghoFuncLib (&asLines) (iLine - 1) to bHolds
            If (bHolds = False) ;
                Function_Return Recipe
        End
        // Quote the marker itself - the converter's reason is better than anything we could invent.
        Move (Pos(CS_CommentSymbol, sRaw)) to iPos
        If (iPos > 0) ;
            Move (Trim(Mid(sRaw, CI_EOL, iPos))) to sReason
        Else ;
            Move (Trim(sRaw)) to sReason
        Move eModExplain to Recipe.eCategory
        Get CategoryText eModExplain to Recipe.sCategoryText
        Move ("Already reviewed and skipped by a refactoring function - the note in the source says why: " + sReason) ;
            to Recipe.sHint
        Move "" to Recipe.sRefactorFn
        Function_Return Recipe
    End_Function

    /* The class a source line DECLARES - the second word of "Class <Name> is a ..." - or "" for any
       other line (first-token test, tabs folded; a trailing-comment "class" never counts). The 4345
       duplicate-name row reads the reported line through this: the compiler prints only the existing
       symbol's internal token (|CI134), never the name, so the line is the one place the name is
       (2026-09-03, WinServ regexp.pkg 206 "Class cRegEx is a cObject" vs DataFlex 26's own cRegEx). */
    Function ClassDeclaredOnLine String sLine Returns String
        String sLC sName
        Integer iSp

        Move (Trim(Replaces(Character(9), sLine, " "))) to sLC
        // Length first: DataFlex compares "class" = "class " as TRUE (trailing blanks are ignored), so a
        // bare "Class" line would pass the prefix test alone and come back as its own name.
        If (Length(sLC) <= 6 or Left(Lowercase(sLC), 6) <> "class ") ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move (Trim(Right(sLC, (Length(sLC) - 6)))) to sName
        Move (Pos(" ", sName)) to iSp
        If (iSp > 0) ;
            Move (Left(sName, (iSp - 1))) to sName
        Function_Return (Trim(sName))
    End_Function

    // The FIRST physical line (1-based) of the ';'-continued statement that line iLine belongs to: walks
    // back while the PREVIOUS line's code ends with ';'. Returns iLine itself when it is not a continuation
    // or is out of range (a file that would not read has 0 lines - the raw number then stands). The
    // compiler reports a continued statement at its LAST physical line, so a 4390 on a six-line
    // External_Function lands on its 'Returns' line and a row that jumps to the raw number opens the editor
    // mid-statement (Nils 2026-09-02: WinServ InternetOption.pkg 130 = "    Returns Integer"). Shared by
    // GuardLineForStatement (the join) and ModAnalyzerFT.dg RebuildCollisionSignal (the 4390 site row).
    // (asLines ByRef = copy-avoidance only, never modified.)
    Function StatementStartLine String[] ByRef asLines Integer iLine Returns Integer
        Integer iStart
        Boolean bPrevSemi

        If (iLine < 1 or iLine > SizeOfArray(asLines)) ;
            Function_Return iLine
        // asLines is 0-based, so the line above line iStart is asLines[iStart - 2].
        Move iLine to iStart
        Move True to bPrevSemi
        While (iStart > 1 and bPrevSemi = True)
            Get _HasLineEndSemiColon of ghoFuncLib asLines[iStart - 2] to bPrevSemi
            If (bPrevSemi = True) ;
                Move (iStart - 1) to iStart
        Loop
        Function_Return iStart
    End_Function

    // A compiler warning on a ';'-continued statement can be attributed to the CONTINUATION line while the
    // obsolete keyword sits on the statement's FIRST line - e.g. a multi-line External_Function32 declaration:
    // the keyword is on line N ('External_Function32 GetVersionEx ... ;') but the 4533 fires on line N+1
    // ('Pointer ... Returns Integer'). Walk back over the ';'-continuations and JOIN the statement so the
    // source-guard / dry-run sees the keyword. Returns the single warned line when it is NOT a continuation.
    // Called by BOTH the triage grid (ModAnalyzerFT.dg) and the refactor plan (PlanFromMessages) - keep them in
    // lock-step. (Nils 2026-07-02: LegacyApp cOSVersionInfo.h External_Function32 declaration read as Explain.)
    // (asLines is ByRef purely to avoid deep-copying the whole file's line array on EVERY call -
    // this runs per guarded message, and a by-value String[] parameter copies all of it each time,
    // the same interpreter tax the .prn tail's UChar[] params paid. Never modified.)
    Function GuardLineForStatement String[] ByRef asLines Integer iLine Returns String
        Integer iStart iSize i
        String sJoined

        Move (SizeOfArray(asLines)) to iSize
        If (iLine < 1 or iLine > iSize) ;
            Function_Return ""
        // Walk back over the ';'-continuations (StatementStartLine - shared with the 4390 site row).
        Get StatementStartLine (&asLines) iLine to iStart
        If (iStart >= iLine) ;
            Function_Return asLines[iLine - 1]
        // Join the statement's lines (first .. warned) so a leading-keyword swap still fires on the whole.
        Move asLines[iStart - 1] to sJoined
        For i from iStart to (iLine - 1)
            Move (RTrim(sJoined) + " " + Trim(asLines[i])) to sJoined
        Loop
        Function_Return sJoined
    End_Function

    // Walks UP from the warned line (1-based iLine) to the ENCLOSING "Object <name> is a <class>"
    // declaration, depth-counting End_Object/Object pairs so a nested child object above the warned
    // line is skipped. Returns the class name ("" when no enclosing object is found) and sets the
    // object name + its 1-based line number ByRef. Comments/strings are masked by _SplitSourceWords,
    // so an "Object" inside a literal never false-matches. PURE -> unit-testable. Used by
    // VerifyBeginRow to tell the user WHICH object (and class) a Begin_Row warning lives in.
    // (asLines ByRef = copy-avoidance only, never modified - see GuardLineForStatement.)
    Function EnclosingObjectClass String[] ByRef asLines Integer iLine String ByRef sObjName Integer ByRef iObjLine Returns String
        String[] asWords
        String sT0
        Integer i iDepth iN

        Move "" to sObjName
        Move 0 to iObjLine
        If (iLine < 1 or iLine > SizeOfArray(asLines)) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move (iLine - 2) to i          // 0-based index of the line ABOVE the warned line.
        Move 0 to iDepth
        While (i >= 0)
            Get _SplitSourceWords of ghoFuncLib asLines[i] to asWords
            Move (SizeOfArray(asWords)) to iN
            If (iN > 0) Begin
                Move (Lowercase(asWords[0])) to sT0
                If (sT0 = "end_object") Begin
                    Increment iDepth
                End
                Else If (sT0 = "object") Begin
                    If (iDepth > 0) Begin
                        Decrement iDepth
                    End
                    Else Begin
                        // The enclosing object: "Object <name> is a <class>" - either article
                        // ("is an" is legal DataFlex too; WinServ 2026-07-17).
                        If (iN >= 5 and Lowercase(asWords[2]) = "is" and (Lowercase(asWords[3]) = "a" or Lowercase(asWords[3]) = "an")) Begin
                            Move asWords[1] to sObjName
                            Move (i + 1) to iObjLine
                            Function_Return asWords[4]
                        End
                        // Malformed/unsupported declaration form - report "not found" (never guess).
                        Function_Return ""
                    End
                End
            End
            Decrement i
        Loop
        Function_Return ""
    End_Function

    // Context enrichment for a 4533 "Begin_Row is obsolete" row (match-text "Begin_Row"): resolve the
    // ENCLOSING object declaration and rewrite the row so the user can act on it. The Begin_Row message
    // maps to BOTH grid converters (RefactorDbGridTocDbCJGrid + RefactorDbListTocDbCJGridPromptList);
    // the enclosing class decides which one applies:
    //   - class name contains "dbgrid" -> the dbGrid converter row is kept, the dbList one demoted - with
    //     the hint left as it was, so the grid can tell a silent drop from an explained one (2026-10-04);
    //     contains "dblist" -> the converse. Neither -> both Explain, with ONE note that names no family.
    //   - the PLAIN class (dbGrid / dbList) -> the row is kept, and the hint names the object and says
    //     "run <fn>".
    //   - any other class is the workspace's own SUBCLASS (MyDbGrid, dbList0 ...) -> Explain, no function:
    //     the converter's EMPTY run leaves such an object as it is since 2026-10-03, so the hint names
    //     ConvertGridSubclassToCodeJock with the subclass as its parameter (2026-10-04): it ports the class
    //     in place and converts its objects with it. (Until 2026-10-03 a subclass row kept the
    //     converter: a name ending in "dbgrid"/"dblist" was taken as converted by the EMPTY run, and the
    //     dbList0 kind got the exact parameter to set - Nils' LegacyApp catch.)
    // No enclosing object found -> the recipe is returned unchanged (never demote on doubt). Called from
    // BOTH the GRID (ModAnalyzerFT.dg) and the PLAN (PlanFromMessages) right after VerifyAutoFix - keep
    // them in lock-step. NOTE: a file's rows merge per (file, function), so a file holding BOTH a dbList
    // and a dbList0 object shows the first-guarded object's hint; converting it and re-analyzing surfaces
    // the next. (Nils 2026-07-03.)
    // (asLines ByRef = copy-avoidance only, never modified - the early-outs below can't help a
    // by-value parameter: the whole-file copy happens at the CALL boundary, before the gate runs.)
    Function VerifyBeginRow tModAnalyzer Recipe String[] ByRef asLines Integer iLine Returns tModAnalyzer
        String sClass sObjName sClassLc sFnLc sFn sTargets sHint sTargetGrid sWhy
        Integer iObjLine iComma
        Boolean bListFamily bGridFamily bKnown

        If (Recipe.iMsgNumber <> 4533) ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        If (not(Lowercase(Recipe.sMatchText) contains "begin_row")) ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        Move (Lowercase(Trim(Recipe.sRefactorFn))) to sFnLc
        If (sFnLc <> "refactordbgridtocdbcjgrid" and sFnLc <> "refactordblisttocdbcjgridpromptlist") ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        Get EnclosingObjectClass (&asLines) iLine (&sObjName) (&iObjLine) to sClass
        If (sClass = "") ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        Move (Lowercase(sClass)) to sClassLc
        Move (sClassLc contains "dbgrid") to bGridFamily
        Move (sClassLc contains "dblist") to bListFamily
        // The class family picks the converter; the WRONG-family recipe is demoted so the row does not
        // offer a converter that would no-op (its IsGridDeclaration never matches this class).
        If (bGridFamily = True and bListFamily = False and sFnLc = "refactordblisttocdbcjgridpromptlist") Begin
            Move "" to Recipe.sRefactorFn
            Move eModExplain to Recipe.eCategory
            Function_Return Recipe
        End
        If (bListFamily = True and bGridFamily = False and sFnLc = "refactordbgridtocdbcjgrid") Begin
            Move "" to Recipe.sRefactorFn
            Move eModExplain to Recipe.eCategory
            Function_Return Recipe
        End
        If (sFnLc = "refactordbgridtocdbcjgrid") Begin
            Move "RefactorDbGridTocDbCJGrid" to sFn
            Move "cDbCJGrid,cDbCJGridColumn" to sTargets
        End
        Else Begin
            Move "RefactorDbListTocDbCJGridPromptList" to sFn
            Move "cDbCJGridPromptList,cDbCJGridColumn" to sTargets
        End
        Move ("Begin_Row lives in 'Object " + sObjName + " is a " + sClass + "' (line " + String(iObjLine) + "). ") to sHint
        If (sClassLc = "dbgrid" or sClassLc = "dblist") Begin
            Move (sHint + "Select and run " + sFn + " to convert the object (Begin_Row/Entry_Item -> column objects).") to sHint
        End
        /* A SUBCLASS OBJECT STAYS AS IT IS (Nils 2026-10-03: "a subclass can't be blindly swapped for a
           CodeJock grid class without first have converted the cEntryGrid subclass"). The converter's EMPTY
           run converts the plain dbGrid / dbList only (cGridToCJGridBase.SubclassToLeave), so a row that
           said "run it" for an MyDbGrid object promised a conversion the run refuses - 794 such rows on one
           workspace's first Check after the change, and none of them could ever clear. The row is Explain,
           with the way forward. */
        Else Begin
            Move (Pos(",", sTargets)) to iComma
            Move (Left(sTargets, (iComma - 1))) to sTargetGrid
            /* THE SUBCLASS CONVERTER TAKES IT, WHERE IT CAN (Nils 2026-10-04: "Why on earth are all grid
               conversions degraded to hand migration???" - 1,383 Begin_Row rows of one workspace, every one
               inside an object of its own subclass, with ConvertGridSubclassToCodeJock only named in the
               hint). The last scan asked that converter about each grid subclass (cDeepScanner.
               JudgeGridSubclasses); on a yes the row carries the converter and is Auto-fix, exactly like
               the scan's own row for the object. On a no it stays an explanation, with the reason. With no
               verdict (no scan this session) it keeps the wording that sends the user to the converter. */
            Get GridSubclassWhy of ghoFuncLib sClassLc (&bKnown) to sWhy
            If (bKnown = True and sWhy = "") Begin
                // It names the object converter the function runs itself (2026-10-05) - for a class named for
                // neither family both converters reach here, and their rows must read the same, so it names none.
                If (bGridFamily = False and bListFamily = False) ;
                    Move (sHint + "'" + sClass + "' is a workspace subclass: ConvertGridSubclassToCodeJock converts the class in place and runs the matching grid converter on this object itself (Begin_Row/Entry_Item -> column objects).") to Recipe.sHint
                Else ;
                    Move (sHint + "'" + sClass + "' is a workspace subclass: ConvertGridSubclassToCodeJock converts the class in place and runs " + sFn + " on this object itself (Begin_Row/Entry_Item -> column objects).") to Recipe.sHint
                Move "ConvertGridSubclassToCodeJock" to Recipe.sRefactorFn
                Move eModAutoFix to Recipe.eCategory
                Get CategoryText eModAutoFix to Recipe.sCategoryText
                Function_Return Recipe
            End
            // A class named for neither family: both converters reach here, so the note names neither family's.
            If (bKnown = True) ;
                Move (sHint + "'" + sClass + "' is a workspace subclass, and ConvertGridSubclassToCodeJock cannot convert it in place: " + sWhy + ". Port " + sClass + " by hand to a class based on " + sTargetGrid + "; its objects stay as they are until then.") to sHint
            Else If (bGridFamily = False and bListFamily = False) ;
                Move (sHint + "'" + sClass + "' is a workspace class, and the grid converters leave its objects as they are when their parameter is EMPTY. Run ConvertGridSubclassToCodeJock with the parameter '" + sClass + "': it ports the class in place and converts every object of it, or says in its run notes why it cannot.") to sHint
            Else ;
                Move (sHint + "'" + sClass + "' is a workspace subclass, and " + sFn + " leaves its objects as they are when its parameter is EMPTY: a plain " + sTargetGrid + " would lose what " + sClass + " adds. Run ConvertGridSubclassToCodeJock with the parameter '" + sClass + "': it ports " + sClass + " to " + sTargetGrid + " in place and converts every object of it, or says in its run notes why it cannot.") to sHint
            Move "" to Recipe.sRefactorFn
            Move eModExplain to Recipe.eCategory
            Get CategoryText eModExplain to Recipe.sCategoryText
        End
        Move sHint to Recipe.sHint
        Function_Return Recipe
    End_Function

    // The WHOLE-FILE counterpart to VerifyAutoFix: for an eOtherFunction auto-fix (RemovePropertyPrivate /
    // RemovePropertyPublic) whose guard is file-wide, DRY-RUN the real function on the file's lines
    // (AutoFixWholeFileWouldChange) and demote to Explain when it would change NOTHING - a guarded property
    // (same-named backing store / mixin) the compiler still warns about but the function correctly leaves
    // alone, so the row could never clear as "Auto-fix" (the rows that recur forever on record_set_mixin.pkg /
    // aps.pkg). A no-op for a line-by-line / non-auto-fix / function-less recipe (the helper returns True on
    // any of those). Called from BOTH the GRID and the PLAN right after VerifyAutoFix, so the two stay
    // consistent. (Nils, LegacyApp 2026-07-01.)
    // (asFileLines ByRef = copy-avoidance only, never modified - see VerifyBeginRow's note.)
    Function VerifyAutoFixWholeFile tModAnalyzer Recipe String[] ByRef asFileLines String sFilePath Returns tModAnalyzer
        Boolean bApplies
        String sMatch sFnLc sCacheKey sEngineFile
        Integer iHit iVal
        String[] asKeys
        Integer[] aiVals

        If (Recipe.eCategory <> eModAutoFix or Trim(Recipe.sRefactorFn) = "") ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        // Deep Scan rows (pseudo msg numbers 9001+) are guarded PER LINE by VerifyAutoFix's dry-run -
        // that pass owns DS honesty. Never whole-file-probe them here: for a line-by-line converter it
        // re-answers the same question over the whole file, and for the DS whole-file class converters
        // (ChangeDfTimerTocTimer / RefactorDbListTocDbCJGridPromptList) it would run a heavy full
        // conversion per (file, function) for no honesty gain - the scanner already shape-verified the
        // Object declaration those converters transform, and a designed refusal writes a skip-TODO
        // (= a change), so the probe could never demote it anyway.
        If (Recipe.iMsgNumber >= CI_DeepScanFirstDSNumber) ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        // VerifyAutoFix already dry-ran / keyword-guarded the families it owns on the single warned line -
        // "command: X", "use of X", and the indicator declaration - so don't probe those again here (a
        // redundant, heavier whole-file pass). This step exists for the REST: the property removers, whose
        // warning is one shared 4535 recipe ("Private or Public keyword") and whose applicability is only
        // knowable by running the real function over the file.
        Move (Lowercase(Trim(Recipe.sMatchText)))  to sMatch
        Move (Lowercase(Trim(Recipe.sRefactorFn))) to sFnLc
        If (Left(sMatch, 9) = "command: " or Left(sMatch, 7) = "use of ") ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        If (sFnLc = "changelegacyindicators" or sFnLc = "changesquarebracketsindicators" or sFnLc = "changeindicatortoboolean") ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        // The probe below (AutoFixWholeFileWouldChange) copies the whole file + runs the real refactor -
        // a full per-line tokenize the Unicode pointer pair / ChangeObjectFunctionCallToGet pay ~1.58s/file
        // (271s of a 40k-warning WinServ analyze). Memoize per (file, function) so a file's many auto-fix
        // rows probe ONCE. The cache is SESSION-scoped (on ghoFuncLib, not this per-analyze object), so a
        // RE-analyze of an unchanged file is instant - the honesty demote is a pure function of (file
        // content, refactor). Keyed with the file's line count so an edit self-invalidates that key; a
        // same-line-count edit is the documented residual staleness (like the pinned cache), harmless -
        // it only mis-labels a grid row's category for a cycle, never the engine RUN, and
        // _InitializeFuncLib clears the cache before every run anyway. (Nils 2026-07-17, option 1.)
        Move (Lowercase(sFilePath) + "|" + sFnLc + "|" + String(SizeOfArray(asFileLines))) to sCacheKey
        Get pasWholeFileSessKey of ghoFuncLib to asKeys
        Move (SearchArray(sCacheKey, asKeys)) to iHit
        If (iHit <> -1) Begin
            Get paiWholeFileSessVal of ghoFuncLib to aiVals
            Move (aiVals[iHit] <> 0) to bApplies
        End
        Else Begin
            /* THE FILE THE PROBE IS ABOUT, where the function asks the engine for it (2026-11-13): MakeFileAutonomous
               scopes itself by the file's extension, and the engine's current file is whatever the last run left -
               "" after a start-up, and then every probe answered "no change". Put back afterwards. */
            If (ghoEngine <> 0) Begin
                Get psCurrentSourceFileName of ghoEngine to sEngineFile
                Set psCurrentSourceFileName of ghoEngine to sFilePath
            End
            Get AutoFixWholeFileWouldChange of ghoFuncLib (Trim(Recipe.sRefactorFn)) (&asFileLines) to bApplies
            If (ghoEngine <> 0) ;
                Set psCurrentSourceFileName of ghoEngine to sEngineFile
            Move 0 to iVal
            If (bApplies) ;
                Move 1 to iVal
            Get paiWholeFileSessVal of ghoFuncLib to aiVals
            Move sCacheKey to asKeys[SizeOfArray(asKeys)]
            Move iVal      to aiVals[SizeOfArray(aiVals)]
            Set pasWholeFileSessKey of ghoFuncLib to asKeys
            Set paiWholeFileSessVal of ghoFuncLib to aiVals
        End
        If (bApplies = True) ;
            Function_Return Recipe
        Move eModExplain to Recipe.eCategory
        Get CategoryText eModExplain to Recipe.sCategoryText
        // Function-appropriate demote hint: a 4544 the pass can't touch (a Get_ accessor / COM property, or a
        // nested call - not a same-file object-method call) points at the general 4544 remedy; the property
        // removers keep their guarded-property wording.
        If (sFnLc = "changeobjectfunctioncalltoget") ;
            Move "Ambiguous return value used in an expression, but not a same-file object-method call this pass can rewrite (a Get_ accessor / COM property, or a nested call) - assign it to a typed local first, then use the local." to Recipe.sHint
        Else ;
            Move "Not fixable here - the whole-file refactor leaves this file unchanged (a guarded property: a same-named backing store, or a mixin). Handle by hand." to Recipe.sHint
        Move "" to Recipe.sRefactorFn
        Function_Return Recipe
    End_Function

    // True if a recipe must be SOURCE-GUARDED (VerifyAutoFix, against the actual warned line)
    // before it can be trusted as an applicable fix: the "use of X" obsolete-function auto-fixes
    // and the legacy-indicator review. Shared by the triage GRID (ModAnalyzer.dg) and the refactor
    // PLAN (PlanFromMessages) so both gate IDENTICALLY - the two must never disagree on whether a
    // message yields a refactor (the divergence Nils spotted: grid showed no UnicodeOemAnsiFunctions
    // row while the plan offered it). (Nils 2026-06-12.)
    Function RecipeNeedsSourceGuard tModAnalyzer Recipe Returns Boolean
        String sMatchLc sFnLc
        Boolean bAuto bActionable

        // Compute the lowercased match-text / function ONCE - this runs per matched recipe on every one of the
        // ~26000 messages, and the old form re-did Lowercase(Trim(...)) up to 9x per call (a real slice of the
        // per-message cost). Same tests, far fewer string allocations.
        Move (Recipe.eCategory = eModAutoFix)      to bAuto
        Move ((Recipe.eCategory = eModAutoFix) or (Recipe.eCategory = eModSuggest)) to bActionable
        Move (Lowercase(Trim(Recipe.sMatchText)))  to sMatchLc
        Move (Lowercase(Trim(Recipe.sRefactorFn))) to sFnLc
        // The "use of X" / "command: X" families - guard so a 4531/4533/4534 fired from a macro /
        // #COMMAND expansion (no literal X on the invocation line) demotes to Explain instead of pointing
        // the user at a line the construct is not on. See VerifyAutoFix Guards A / D.
        // ACTIONABLE, not just auto-fix (Nils 2026-07-26): this was gated on eModAutoFix, so a REVIEW row
        // never got the macro guard - and a Review row pointing at the wrong line is just as wrong as an
        // Auto-fix one. LegacyApp's Deferred_View macro carries 'Gosub Create_!1' in its body, so the
        // 4533 "command: Gosub" fired at EVERY deferred-view invocation site and each one became a Review
        // row against a line reading 'Object <name> is a cTrDbView' - no Gosub anywhere near it. Explain
        // rows stay unguarded: they are already the lowest tier, so a file read would buy nothing.
        If (bActionable and (Left(sMatchLc, 7) = "use of " or Left(sMatchLc, 9) = "command: ")) ;
            Function_Return True
        // The legacy-indicator USAGE reviews (line-guarded to the warned [bracket] / Indicate line).
        If (sFnLc = "changelegacyindicators" or sFnLc = "changesquarebracketsindicators" or sFnLc = "changeindicatortoboolean") ;
            Function_Return True
        // The error-52 repair is Auto-fix BY SHAPE: every 52 "(operator)" row is dry-run against its
        // line - the wrapped-command shape stays Auto-fix, every other 52 goes back to Explain with the
        // general advice (VerifyAutoFix, 2026-09-05).
        If (bAuto and sFnLc = "splitcommandoutofcondition") ;
            Function_Return True
        // The legacy type-name CAST rows (ChangeLegacyTypeCast: 4328 keyed on the type's INTERNAL name).
        // The warned line must carry the type's SOURCE name followed by '(' - Guard F (2026-09-14).
        If (bActionable and sFnLc = "changelegacytypecast") ;
            Function_Return True
        // The CLASS-SWAP family ("class: X"). Its converters may legitimately DECLINE an object and
        // write a skip-marker instead of converting it - ChangeDfTimerTocTimer leaves a
        // Timer_Message-dispatch object alone with a //TODO above it, and the grid/list converters
        // do the same for shapes they will not touch. The declaration itself is unchanged, so the
        // compiler keeps warning; without reading the line the row came back as Auto-fix on every
        // Check and the plan re-ran a converter that only declines again (Nils 2026-07-26). Reading
        // it is what lets VerifySkipMarker see the marker. NOT gated on bAuto: the Suggest class
        // rows (the grid/list converters) reach a plan too, so they need the same guard.
        If (Left(sMatchLc, 7) = "class: ") ;
            Function_Return True
        // The WHOLE-FILE auto-fixes (RemovePropertyPrivate / RemovePropertyPublic, and ChangeObjectFunctionCallToGet
        // for 4544): their guard is file-wide, so the caller reads the WHOLE file and runs VerifyAutoFixWholeFile -
        // which demotes the row to Explain when the function leaves the file unchanged (a guarded property, or a
        // 4544 that isn't a same-file object-method call this pass can rewrite - a Get_ accessor / COM property).
        // (Nils 2026-07-01 / 07-07.) The Unicode 4539 pair (UnicodePointerToString / UnicodeStringToPointer) joins
        // them for the SAME macro-expansion reason as the "use of X" / "command: X" families above: a 4539 fired
        // from a #COMMAND expansion (e.g. Local_Buffer) reports at the invocation line, which is NOT the bare
        // 'Move <ptr> to <str>' the converter rewrites - so the whole-file dry-run changes nothing and the row
        // demotes to Explain instead of promising a no-op auto-fix. (Nils 2026-07-08.)
        // Win64IntegerToLongPtr (2026-11-12) the same way: a 4539 on a parameter, a global or a ByRef
        // variable is one it leaves, and the dry run then demotes the row. ChangeStructMemberLegacyTypes too: a
        // "Command not found WORD" outside a Struct is not its business. MakeFileAutonomous too (2026-11-13): a
        // class it does not know, or one defined in two packages, gets no Use from it.
        If (bAuto and (sFnLc = "removepropertyprivate" or sFnLc = "removepropertypublic" or sFnLc = "changeobjectfunctioncalltoget" or sFnLc = "unicodepointertostring" or sFnLc = "unicodestringtopointer" or sFnLc = "win64integertolongptr" or sFnLc = "changestructmemberlegacytypes" or sFnLc = "makefileautonomous")) ;
            Function_Return True
        /* THE GRID / LIST CONVERTERS ON A CLASS-MISSING ERROR (2026-11-13): 4345 / 4328 U_DBGRID and U_DBLIST. Only an
           Object line converts; on "Class cMyDbList is a DBList" or "Procedure Clear_All for DBGrid" the converter
           changes nothing, and VerifyLegacyClassShape says so - but it runs only on a guarded recipe, and nothing here
           asked for these, so since 2026-08-30 it never ran for them and the plan offered both converters every round
           on such lines. MakeFileAutonomous is the fix there: it adds the missing Use. */
        If ((sFnLc = "refactordbgridtocdbcjgrid" or sFnLc = "refactordblisttocdbcjgridpromptlist") and Left(sMatchLc, 2) = "u_") ;
            Function_Return True
        // The 4533 Begin_Row rows: VerifyBeginRow reads the file to resolve the ENCLOSING object declaration
        // (which converter applies + the subclass parameter hint, e.g. dbList0). (Nils 2026-07-03.)
        If (sMatchLc = "begin_row") ;
            Function_Return True
        // Deep Scan AUTO-FIX rows (pseudo msg numbers 9001+): the rule's category is the catalog's
        // STATIC default, not a per-line guarantee - guard them so VerifyAutoFix can dry-run the
        // mapped converter against the real hit line and demote a row the fix would no-op on
        // ("the scan promises what the run does"). (Nils 2026-07-16, Deep Scan auto-fix honesty.)
        If (bAuto and Recipe.iMsgNumber >= CI_DeepScanFirstDSNumber and sFnLc <> "") ;
            Function_Return True
        Function_Return False
    End_Function

    // A recipe with NO refactor function can't be applied - it is informational, so its category must read
    // Explain, never Auto-fix/Review. ClassifyFromRecipes already forces this for its single result; mirror it
    // for the MULTI-match path so the grid (MatchingRecipesFromList -> ModAnalyzerFT) shows a function-less
    // Review - e.g. 4544 GET_COMSIZE/GET_COMID "ambiguous return", or the generic 4345 - as Explain, not Review.
    // The grid's own source-guard only demotes recipes that NEED guarding, so a plain function-less one leaked
    // its raw eModSuggest through. (Category only; the refactor PLAN reads sRefactorFn, not eCategory.)
    Function _DemoteFunctionlessToExplain tModAnalyzer[] aRecipes Returns tModAnalyzer[]
        Integer i iSize
        Move (SizeOfArray(aRecipes) - 1) to iSize
        For i from 0 to iSize
            If (Trim(aRecipes[i].sRefactorFn) = "") Begin
                Move eModExplain to aRecipes[i].eCategory
                Get CategoryText eModExplain to aRecipes[i].sCategoryText
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return aRecipes
    End_Function

    // A character a compiler-reported SYMBOL is made of: [a-z0-9_] plus '$', '@' and '#', which
    // DataFlex names carry ('IS$DONE', 'NAME@'). Lowercase input - the matchers lowercase first.
    Function _IsSymbolChar String sCh Returns Boolean
        Integer iA
        Move (Ascii(sCh)) to iA
        Function_Return ((iA >= 97 and iA <= 122) or (iA >= 48 and iA <= 57) or iA = 95 or iA = 36 or iA = 64 or iA = 35)
    End_Function

    /* RECIPE MATCH TEXT SEMANTICS (Nils 2026-09-14). A plain match text is a SUBSTRING of the
       lowercased message text, as it always was, and the stem families rely on that: '4298 | DFWRITE'
       covers DFWRITELN and DFWRITEEOL, '4306 | XTPREPORT' every MSG_XTPREPORT... constant (the suite
       said so when a blanket whole-word rule was tried first). A match text written 'symbol: X' hits
       only where X occurs as a WHOLE symbol, bounded by non-symbol characters - on the side where its
       own edge is a symbol character. The 4328 cast recipes are keyed on a type's internal name, and
       as substrings 'DATETIME' fired on "Undefined symbol in argument DWHIGHDATETIME": a bare Type
       member on an 'Add 10 to dwHighDateTime' line, offered as a cast rewrite the run then found
       nothing to rewrite. Same vocabulary as 'class: X' / 'use of X' / 'command: X'. Both inputs
       lowercased. */
    Function MatchTextHits String sTxtLc String sMatchLc Returns Boolean
        Integer iPos iLen iTxtLen iFrom
        Boolean bLeft bRight bMore bOk bBad

        If (Left(sMatchLc, 8) <> "symbol: ") ;
            Function_Return (sTxtLc contains sMatchLc)
        Move (Trim(Mid(sMatchLc, (Length(sMatchLc) - 8), 9))) to sMatchLc
        Move (Length(sMatchLc)) to iLen
        If (iLen = 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        Move (Length(sTxtLc)) to iTxtLen
        Get _IsSymbolChar (Left(sMatchLc, 1))  to bLeft
        Get _IsSymbolChar (Right(sMatchLc, 1)) to bRight
        Move 1 to iFrom
        Move True to bMore
        While (bMore = True and iFrom <= iTxtLen)
            Move (Pos(sMatchLc, Mid(sTxtLc, iTxtLen, iFrom))) to iPos
            If (iPos = 0) Begin
                Move False to bMore
            End
            Else Begin
                Move (iPos + iFrom - 1) to iPos
                Move True to bOk
                If (bLeft = True and iPos > 1) Begin
                    Get _IsSymbolChar (Mid(sTxtLc, 1, (iPos - 1))) to bBad
                    If (bBad = True) ;
                        Move False to bOk
                End
                If (bRight = True and (iPos + iLen) <= iTxtLen) Begin
                    Get _IsSymbolChar (Mid(sTxtLc, 1, (iPos + iLen))) to bBad
                    If (bBad = True) ;
                        Move False to bOk
                End
                If (bOk = True) ;
                    Function_Return True
                Move (iPos + 1) to iFrom
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return False
    End_Function

    // The name a legacy cast is WRITTEN with, for the internal name a 4328 message carries: the
    // compiler says LONGPTR for Handle(x) and ADDRESS for Pointer(x); every other type is reported
    // under its own name. Lowercased, for the line probes (Guard F).
    Function CastSourceName String sInternal Returns String
        String sLc
        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sInternal))) to sLc
        If (Left(sLc, 8) = "symbol: ") ;
            Move (Trim(Mid(sLc, (Length(sLc) - 8), 9))) to sLc
        If (sLc = "longptr") ;
            Function_Return "handle"
        If (sLc = "address") ;
            Function_Return "pointer"
        Function_Return sLc
    End_Function

    // Like ClassifyFromRecipes, but returns ALL recipes matching (number + text) at the winning
    // precedence level: every specific (MatchText-in-text) row if any exist, else every generic
    // (blank-MatchText) row. Lets ONE message map to more than one refactor function when several
    // genuinely apply (each only touches its own construct, so running all is safe). NOTE: the 4537
    // grid family is NOT such a case - the compiler names the ACTUAL class ("Obsolete class: DBGRID"
    // for a dbGrid, "Obsolete class: DBLIST" for a dbList), so each maps 1:1 to its own refactor; do
    // NOT key the dbList recipe on "DBGRID" (it never matches a dbList message - the 2026-06-19 bug).
    // (aRecipes ByRef purely to avoid deep-copying the FULL recipe table per call - this runs once
    // per message, so a 26000-message analyze paid 26000 copies of the whole table. Never modified.)
    Function MatchingRecipesFromList Integer iMsgNo String sMsgTxt tModAnalyzer[] ByRef aRecipes Returns tModAnalyzer[]
        tModAnalyzer[] aSpecific aGeneric
        Integer i iSize
        String sTxt sMatch
        Boolean bAnyFn bHit

        Move (Lowercase(sMsgTxt)) to sTxt
        Move (SizeOfArray(aRecipes) -1) to iSize
        For i from 0 to iSize
            If (aRecipes[i].iMsgNumber = iMsgNo) Begin
                If (aRecipes[i].sMatchText = "") Begin
                    Move aRecipes[i] to aGeneric[SizeOfArray(aGeneric)]
                End
                Else Begin
                    Move (Lowercase(aRecipes[i].sMatchText)) to sMatch
                    // A substring, or a whole symbol for a 'symbol: X' text - see MatchTextHits (2026-09-14).
                    Get MatchTextHits sTxt sMatch to bHit
                    If (bHit = True) ;
                        Move aRecipes[i] to aSpecific[SizeOfArray(aSpecific)]
                End
            End
        Loop

        If (SizeOfArray(aSpecific) > 0) Begin
            // A function-less SPECIFIC hint must not SHADOW a function-bearing GENERIC
            // recipe out of the refactor plan: the install-catalog scan seeds a specific
            // Explain row for every obsolete command, which starved TypeToStructReport
            // (generic 4532 | Suggest) of every Type-family message - the function never
            // appeared in Auto Select (Nils 2026-06-11). When NO winning specific carries
            // a refactor function, append the generics that do; when one does, behavior
            // is unchanged (the dbGrid/dbList multi-match case).
            Move False to bAnyFn
            Move (SizeOfArray(aSpecific) - 1) to iSize
            For i from 0 to iSize
                If (Trim(aSpecific[i].sRefactorFn) <> "") ;
                    Move True to bAnyFn
            Loop
            If (bAnyFn = False) Begin
                Move (SizeOfArray(aGeneric) - 1) to iSize
                For i from 0 to iSize
                    If (Trim(aGeneric[i].sRefactorFn) <> "") ;
                        Move aGeneric[i] to aSpecific[SizeOfArray(aSpecific)]
                Loop
            End
            Get _DemoteFunctionlessToExplain aSpecific to aSpecific
            Function_Return aSpecific
        End
        Get _DemoteFunctionlessToExplain aGeneric to aGeneric
        Function_Return aGeneric
    End_Function

    // Map a compiler message (number + text) to a fix recipe, using the ModAnalyzer table
    // (seeded from the built-in defaults on first use). sMsgTxt lets a recipe sub-classify
    // - e.g. a 4539 "Pointer to String" vs "String to Pointer" picks the matching function.
    Function ClassifyMessage Integer iMsgNo String sMsgTxt Returns tModAnalyzer
        tModAnalyzer[] aR
        tModAnalyzer Recipe
        Send EnsureRecipesLoaded
        Get paRecipes to aR
        Get ClassifyFromRecipes iMsgNo sMsgTxt aR to Recipe
        Function_Return Recipe
    End_Function

    // Append the SOURCE-driven indicator companion the plan needs, so Auto-Select ticks it only when it is
    // genuinely required (Nils, 2026-07-06 - a built-in-only Found/Err workspace must NOT drag it in). When the
    // plan contains an indicator converter (ChangeLegacyIndicators / ChangeSquareBracketsIndicators /
    // ChangeIndicatorToBoolean) AND scope source uses a named indicator that will be left undeclared, add
    // DeclareRefactoredIndicators - decided by the SAME scan the refactor itself runs
    // (ghoFuncLib._ScopeHasUndeclaredNamedIndicator over ONLY the indicator-converter files), so preview + run
    // agree. Only the source-based companion belongs here; DefineSelectMacro is pulled in at run time by the
    // engine, and only when BOTH a converter runs under eModernizeSelect AND the source has a [Select]/Indicate
    // Select to modernise (engine's _ScopeHasSelectIndicator gate + the DD's HasModernizeSelectConverterSelected)
    // - so it never runs as a no-op. Called by BOTH plan builders (PlanFromMessages + PlanFromSavedRows) so the
    // live + saved-plan paths behave the same.

    // The three whole-file indicator converters whose rewrites can leave a named indicator undeclared. ONE place
    // to name them - both plan builders key their indicator-file collection off this.
    Function _IsIndicatorConverterName String sFn Returns Boolean
        String sLC
        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sFn))) to sLC
        Function_Return (sLC = "changelegacyindicators" or sLC = "changesquarebracketsindicators" or sLC = "changeindicatortoboolean")
    End_Function

    Procedure AddNeededIndicatorCompanions tModRefactorPlan ByRef Plan String[] asScanFiles String[] asLineKeys
        Boolean bNeed

        // asScanFiles = ONLY the files an indicator converter touches (collected by the caller). Empty => no
        // indicator converter in the plan => nothing to do. Scanning the WHOLE plan file set here read the
        // entire workspace off disk with no yield - ~7s frozen on a big plan (Nils 2026-07-06); limiting to the
        // indicator files (the only ones where a named indicator could be left undeclared) makes it a handful.
        If (SearchArray("DeclareRefactoredIndicators", Plan.asFunctions) <> -1) ;
            Procedure_Return
        If (SizeOfArray(asScanFiles) = 0) ;
            Procedure_Return
        If (ghoFuncLib = 0) ;
            Procedure_Return
        // This scan (tokenize-every-line over each indicator file) runs AFTER PlanFromMessages' yielding
        // message loop, so on a big legacy file it is the last un-pumped stretch before the plan dialog
        // shows (a ~9700-line legacy file; Nils 2026-07-09). BRIDGE our FT pump target onto ghoFuncLib
        // just for this scan so _CollectIndicatorNames' inner beat drives DoYieldToUI (marquee AND the
        // elapsed-seconds status), then clear it so the later engine RUN's own tokenize passes fall back
        // to a bare pump. Refresh once going in too. Both are no-ops when phoUIYieldTarget is 0 (console).
        If (phoUIYieldTarget(Self) <> 0) ;
            Send DoYieldToUI of (phoUIYieldTarget(Self))
        Set phoUIYieldTarget of ghoFuncLib to (phoUIYieldTarget(Self))
        Get _ScopeNeedsIndicatorCompanion of ghoFuncLib asScanFiles asLineKeys to bNeed
        Set phoUIYieldTarget of ghoFuncLib to 0
        If (bNeed = True) Begin
            Move "DeclareRefactoredIndicators" to Plan.asFunctions[SizeOfArray(Plan.asFunctions)]
            // Keep asFunctionHints PARALLEL to asFunctions (downstream indexes both by the same i).
            Move "Declares the named indicators the converters rewrite but leave undeclared" ;
                to Plan.asFunctionHints[SizeOfArray(Plan.asFunctionHints)]
        End
    End_Procedure

    // Aggregate parsed compiler messages into the actionable plan: the distinct refactor
    // functions to apply + the distinct workspace-source files to process. Files are
    // scoped to sScopeRoot (the workspace home), so system/library files - e.g. the
    // DataFlex install packages flagged by 4536 - are never included. Only messages that
    // map to a refactor function (Auto-fix / Suggest) contribute; Explain/Unknown skipped.
    Function PlanFromMessages tCompileError[] aMessages String sScopeRoot Returns tModRefactorPlan
        tModRefactorPlan Plan
        tModAnalyzer[] aR aMatched
        tModAnalyzer rVerified
        Integer i j iSize iLen iMatch iGuardLn
        String sFn sFile sGuardFile sSrcLine sLastPlanFile
        String[] asGuardLines asIndicatorFiles asIndicatorLineKeys asPinned asEmptyPin
        String[] asNoOpFiles asNoOpStamps asNoOpFnsAll asNoOpFns
        String sNoOpFile
        Handle hoNoOps
        Boolean bAnyFn bNeedsGuard bPinnedDone

        Send EnsureRecipesLoaded
        // What the last plans ran without changing a file (cPlanNoOps): read once, looked up once per file.
        Get Create (RefClass(cPlanNoOps)) to hoNoOps
        If (hoNoOps <> 0) Begin
            Send LoadRecord of hoNoOps
            Get pasRecFiles  of hoNoOps to asNoOpFiles
            Get pasRecStamps of hoNoOps to asNoOpStamps
            Get pasRecFns    of hoNoOps to asNoOpFnsAll
            Send Destroy of hoNoOps
        End
        Move (Character(1)) to sNoOpFile
        // The include lift judges "every user is in the run" optimistically here ("*": the plan's file
        // list is not final while it builds); the engine re-judges exactly at run time - see pasRunFiles
        // (cBaseFuncLib). A lifted include is deliberately NOT added to Plan.asFiles: were it, a lift the
        // run cannot honour would still convert the include's reads while the program's write stayed
        // pinned. The engine appends it only when the exact verdict holds.
        Send _SetRunFilesAll of ghoFuncLib
        Get paRecipes to aR
        // Studio COM proxy wrappers are never refactored (the engine skips them) - keep their diagnostics out
        // of the plan too, the SAME drop the triage grid applies, so grid + plan agree.
        Get DropCOMProxyMessages aMessages to aMessages
        Move (SizeOfArray(aMessages) - 1) to iSize
        Move (Length(sScopeRoot)) to iLen
        Move "" to sGuardFile
        For i from 0 to iSize
            If (phoUIYieldTarget(Self) <> 0 and (Mod(i, 64) = 0)) ;
                Send DoYieldToUI of (phoUIYieldTarget(Self))
            Move aMessages[i].sFileName to sFile
            // Scope filter: keep only files under the workspace root (iLen = 0 => no filter).
            If ((iLen = 0) or (Left(sFile, iLen) = sScopeRoot)) Begin
                If (sFile <> sNoOpFile) Begin
                    Move sFile to sNoOpFile
                    Get PlanNoOpFunctionsIn (&asNoOpFiles) (&asNoOpStamps) (&asNoOpFnsAll) sFile to asNoOpFns
                End
                // One message can map to MORE THAN ONE function (e.g. a 4533 "command: Begin_Row"
                // is emitted by both a dbGrid and a dbList) - pull in every matching refactor.
                Get MatchingRecipesFromList aMessages[i].iErrorNo aMessages[i].sErrorTxt (&aR) to aMatched
                Move (SizeOfArray(aMatched)) to iMatch
                Move False to bAnyFn
                For j from 0 to (iMatch - 1)
                    Move aMatched[j] to rVerified
                    // A LIBRARY file's message never carries a refactor function - the library is
                    // replaced, not edited. The GRID applies the same call at the same point
                    // (ModAnalyzerFT) - keep the two in lock-step.
                    Get VerifyLibraryOwned rVerified sFile to rVerified
                    // The Deep Scan's own verdict for THIS hit outranks its rule's defaults - see
                    // VerifyScanVerdict. The GRID applies the same call at the same point.
                    Get VerifyScanVerdict rVerified aMessages[i] to rVerified
                    // A function the last plan ran on this file without changing it (cPlanNoOps). The GRID
                    // applies the same call at the same point.
                    Get VerifyPlanNoOp rVerified (&asNoOpFns) to rVerified
                    // SOURCE-GUARD each matched recipe against the real warned line - the SAME guard
                    // the triage grid applies (ModAnalyzer.dg) - so a fix the grid demotes to Explain
                    // because its obsolete construct is NOT on that line (a macro/command expansion,
                    // e.g. ToOEM inside File_Exist_OEM, or [Found] inside Deferred_View) is not offered
                    // in the plan either. Read lazily + cache the last file; never demote on a failed
                    // or short read (Nils 2026-06-12 - grid/plan must agree).
                    Get RecipeNeedsSourceGuard rVerified to bNeedsGuard
                    // Skip the guard when this function is ALREADY in the plan (an earlier warned line confirmed
                    // it actionable): it runs file-wide regardless, so re-guarding every other warning that maps
                    // to it is wasted work - the workspace's many same-function warnings collapse to ~one guard.
                    // (Nils, perf 2026-07-02 - mirrors the grid's per-(file,function) guard-skip in ModAnalyzerFT.)
                    If (bNeedsGuard = True and (SearchArray(Trim(rVerified.sRefactorFn), Plan.asFunctions) = -1)) Begin
                        If (sFile <> sGuardFile) Begin
                            Move (rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArray(sFile)) to asGuardLines
                            Move sFile to sGuardFile
                            // LAZY pinned compute (Nils 2026-07-17: 100 of a 110s plan build was
                            // _ComputePinnedIndicators run EAGERLY for every guarded file, while its set
                            // is consumed ONLY by the three indicator converters' pinned branch). On a
                            // file change just EMPTY the set (a stale previous-file set must never leak
                            // into a verify); the compute runs below on the FIRST indicator-fn recipe
                            // guarded for this file - and never at all otherwise. The grid does the SAME
                            // (ModAnalyzerFT LoadData) so grid == plan verdicts stay identical.
                            Move False to bPinnedDone
                            Set pasPinnedIndicators of ghoFuncLib to asEmptyPin
                            // The reasons go with the names: a reason outliving its pin could attach
                            // the previous file's include to a later pin of the same identifier.
                            Set pasPinnedReasons of ghoFuncLib to asEmptyPin
                        End
                        If (bPinnedDone = False and _IsIndicatorConverterName(Self, rVerified.sRefactorFn)) Begin
                            Get _ComputePinnedIndicators of ghoFuncLib (&asGuardLines) sFile to asPinned
                            Set pasPinnedIndicators of ghoFuncLib to asPinned
                            Move True to bPinnedDone
                        End
                        Move aMessages[i].iLine to iGuardLn
                        If (iGuardLn >= 1 and iGuardLn <= SizeOfArray(asGuardLines)) Begin
                            // Walk back over ';'-continuations so a multi-line declaration's keyword is seen.
                            Get GuardLineForStatement (&asGuardLines) iGuardLn to sSrcLine
                            Get VerifyAutoFix rVerified sSrcLine to rVerified
                            // A converter's own skip-marker outranks the recipe: demote to Explain so a
                            // refusal it already wrote into the source is not re-offered as a fix. The
                            // GRID applies the same call right here - keep the two in lock-step.
                            Get VerifySkipMarker rVerified (&asGuardLines) iGuardLn to rVerified
                            // A line inside a #COMMAND body is never Auto-fix (see the GRID, which
                            // applies the same call at this point).
                            Get VerifyMacroBodyLine rVerified (&asGuardLines) iGuardLn to rVerified
                            // A Class/Procedure line is never fixable by the object converters
                            // (see the GRID, which applies the same call at this point).
                            Get VerifyLegacyClassShape rVerified (&asGuardLines) iGuardLn to rVerified
                            // Begin_Row rows: resolve the enclosing object so the row picks the RIGHT grid/list
                            // converter and the hint names the object + the subclass parameter (e.g. dbList0).
                            // The SAME enrichment the grid applies - keep in lock-step.
                            Get VerifyBeginRow rVerified (&asGuardLines) iGuardLn to rVerified
                        End
                        // Whole-file auto-fixes (RemovePropertyPrivate/Public): drop them from the PLAN too
                        // when running the real function on the WHOLE file changes nothing (a guarded property),
                        // so Auto Select never offers a no-op fix - the SAME demote the grid applies. No-op for
                        // line-by-line recipes; skipped on a failed/empty read.
                        If (SizeOfArray(asGuardLines) > 0) ;
                            Get VerifyAutoFixWholeFile rVerified (&asGuardLines) sGuardFile to rVerified
                    End
                    Move (Trim(rVerified.sRefactorFn)) to sFn
                    If (sFn <> "") Begin
                        Move True to bAnyFn
                        If (SearchArray(sFn, Plan.asFunctions) = -1) Begin
                            Move sFn to Plan.asFunctions[SizeOfArray(Plan.asFunctions)]
                            // Carry the function's hint (its {Description} first line) alongside,
                            // so the preview dialog can show it as a per-row tooltip. Parallel index.
                            Move (Trim(rVerified.sHint)) to Plan.asFunctionHints[SizeOfArray(Plan.asFunctionHints)]
                        End
                        // Collect the DISTINCT files an indicator converter touches - the ONLY files where the
                        // DeclareRefactoredIndicators companion can matter, so AddNeededIndicatorCompanions scans
                        // just these instead of reading the whole plan file set off disk.
                        If (_IsIndicatorConverterName(Self, sFn)) Begin
                            If (SearchArray(sFile, asIndicatorFiles) = -1) ;
                                Move sFile to asIndicatorFiles[SizeOfArray(asIndicatorFiles)]
                            // EVERY warned line rides along (not just the first per file): the companion
                            // test reads exactly these lines instead of tokenizing the whole file.
                            Move (Lowercase(sFile) + "|" + String(aMessages[i].iLine)) to asIndicatorLineKeys[SizeOfArray(asIndicatorLineKeys)]
                        End
                    End
                Loop
                // Add this file to the plan's DISTINCT file set. The old form ran a SearchArray over a file
                // list growing to thousands on EVERY actionable message (O(messages x files) - the bulk of
                // the time a 6151-row plan spent building, Nils 2026-07-17). Messages arrive FILE-GROUPED
                // (the grid's single-slot source-read cache never thrashed, proving contiguity), so once a
                // file has been added, its remaining messages match sLastPlanFile and skip the lookup
                // entirely - collapsing it to ~one SearchArray per actionable file. sLastPlanFile tracks the
                // last file we ran the add-check for (only under bAnyFn), so a file whose FIRST message has
                // no fn but a LATER one does is still added. The SearchArray stays as the correctness
                // fallback: a non-contiguous reappearance can't create a duplicate (which would make the
                // engine process that file twice).
                If (bAnyFn and sFile <> sLastPlanFile) Begin
                    If (SearchArray(sFile, Plan.asFiles) = -1) ;
                        Move sFile to Plan.asFiles[SizeOfArray(Plan.asFiles)]
                    Move sFile to sLastPlanFile
                End
            End
        Loop
        Send AddNeededIndicatorCompanions (&Plan) asIndicatorFiles asIndicatorLineKeys
        Function_Return Plan
    End_Function

    // Load the recipe cache once per target, seeding the table from the defaults if it is empty.
    // Reloads when the app's TARGET DataFlex version changes (a different compiler selected), so the
    // per-version cache + catalog follow the target the user is migrating to.
    Procedure EnsureRecipesLoaded
        Boolean bLoaded
        String sVer sLoaded

        // The app pre-resolves the current target version; compare it to what THIS cache last loaded
        // for, so a target switch (new compiler) forces a re-scan + re-seed.
        Get psTargetDfVersion of ghoApplication to sVer
        Get psLoadedDfVersion to sLoaded
        Get pbRecipesLoaded to bLoaded
        If ((bLoaded = False) or (sVer <> sLoaded)) Begin
            Send RefreshRecipeTable
            Send LoadRecipes
            Set psLoadedDfVersion to sVer
            Set pbRecipesLoaded to True
        End
    End_Procedure

    // Read every ModAnalyzer row into the in-memory cache (paRecipes).
    Procedure LoadRecipes
        tModAnalyzer[] TheData
        Integer i 
        
        Move 0 to i
        Clear ModAnalyzer
        Find Gt ModAnalyzer by 1
        While (Found)
            Move ModAnalyzer.MsgNumber            to TheData[i].iMsgNumber
            Move (ModAnalyzer.IsWarning <> 0)     to TheData[i].bIsWarning
            Move (Trim(ModAnalyzer.MatchText))    to TheData[i].sMatchText
            Move ModAnalyzer.Category             to TheData[i].eCategory
            Move (Trim(ModAnalyzer.Hint))         to TheData[i].sHint
            Move (Trim(ModAnalyzer.RefactorFn))   to TheData[i].sRefactorFn
            Get CategoryText TheData[i].eCategory to TheData[i].sCategoryText
            Increment i
            Find Gt ModAnalyzer by 1
        Loop
        Set paRecipes to TheData
    End_Procedure

    // Full path to the editable recipe JSON: the workspace Data folder + the filename. Uses the
    // phoWorkspace built-in (NOT pOrgWS) so this class still compiles in the cApplication-based
    // DFUnit test runner; during Analyze the active workspace is DFRefactor's own. Pass
    // ghoApplication (NOT Self): phoWorkspace resolves off the object handed to it, and the
    // workspace hangs off the APP - Self here is this class, giving a bad handle (psDataPath faults).
    Function RecipeJsonPath Returns String
        String sData sVer sFile
        Get psDataPath of (phoWorkspace(ghoApplication)) to sData
        // Key the cache file by the TARGET DataFlex version (resolved by the app) so each target keeps
        // its own scanned catalog (e.g. ModAnalyzerRecipes-26.0.json). Unversioned name when blank.
        // Only ever called via RefreshRecipeTable (never from the test fixtures), so reading the app here is
        // safe in the cApplication-based test runner.
        Get psTargetDfVersion of ghoApplication to sVer
        Move CS_ModAnalyzerRecipesJson to sFile
        If (Trim(sVer) <> "") ;
            Move (Replace(".json", sFile, ("-" + sVer + ".json"))) to sFile
        Function_Return (vFolderFormat(sData) + sFile)
    End_Function

    // Serialize recipes + version to the JSON file (pretty, NO BOM - a BOM breaks ParseUtf8).
    // Mirrors cExportImportFunctions.GenerateJsonExportFile / WriteJsonToFile.
    Procedure WriteRecipesToJson tModAnalyzer[] aRecipes Integer iVersion
        Handle hoJson
        tModRecipeFile RecipeFile
        UChar[] ucJson
        Integer iCh
        String sFile
        
        Move iVersion to RecipeFile.iVersion
        // Never the Deep Scan rows: they are the rules catalog's, re-derived at every refresh (v152).
        Get _WithoutDeepScanRecipes aRecipes to RecipeFile.aRecipes
        Get Create (RefClass(cJsonObject)) to hoJson
        Send DataTypeToJson of hoJson RecipeFile
        Set peWhiteSpace  of hoJson to jpWhitespace_Pretty
        Get StringifyUtf8 of hoJson to ucJson
        Send Destroy of hoJson
        Get RecipeJsonPath to sFile
        Get rfOpenOutput sFile to iCh
        If (iCh < 0) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Write channel iCh ucJson
        Send rfCloseOutput iCh
    End_Procedure

    // Read recipes + version from the JSON file. Returns False if the file is absent or does
    // not parse; on True, aRecipes / iVersion are filled. Mirrors ReadJsonFile / ParseJsonData.
    Function ReadRecipesFromJson tModAnalyzer[] ByRef aRecipes Integer ByRef iVersion Returns Boolean
        Handle hoJson
        tModRecipeFile RecipeFile
        String sFile

        Get RecipeJsonPath to sFile
        Get rfParseJsonFile sFile to hoJson
        If (hoJson = 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        Get JsonToDataType of hoJson to RecipeFile
        Send Destroy of hoJson
        Move RecipeFile.aRecipes to aRecipes
        Move RecipeFile.iVersion to iVersion
        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    // ---- Saved "last refactoring plan" JSON (per-WORKSPACE, latest-only) ---------------------------
    // Full path to the saved-plan file (Data folder + LastPlan-<wskey>.json). The path build lives on
    // ghoApplication now (cRefactorApplication.SavedPlanJsonPath) so the dashboard can reach it without a
    // dock handle + there is one source of truth; this just delegates. Only ever called from the FT analyzer
    // grid / saved-plan viewer, never the cApplication-based test fixtures.
    Function SavedPlanJsonPath Returns String
        String sPath
        Get SavedPlanJsonPath of ghoApplication to sPath
        Function_Return sPath
    End_Function

    /* The provenance stamp of a compile output file: its last-write time and its size, as one string
       ("" when there is no such file). Written into tSavedPlan.sSourcePrnStamp beside sSourcePrn and
       compared on read - two things a recreated workspace at the same path cannot reproduce by
       accident, and cheap (one find + one metadata query; the .prn is 55 MB on WinServ and is NOT
       read). Both halves come from the same machine's own formatting, so the comparison is exact. */
    Function SourcePrnStamp String sPrn Returns String
        DateTime dtWrite
        Move (Trim(sPrn)) to sPrn
        If (sPrn = "" or vFilePathExists(sPrn) = 0) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move (rfFileDate(sPrn)) to dtWrite
        Function_Return (String(dtWrite) + "|" + String(rfFileSizeLive(sPrn)))
    End_Function

    // Serialize the saved plan to its per-workspace JSON (pretty, NO BOM - a BOM breaks ParseUtf8).
    // Mirrors WriteRecipesToJson. iVersion is stamped here so ReadSavedPlan can reject a stale layout,
    // and the source .prn's provenance stamp is taken HERE - the one writer, at the moment of writing.
    Procedure WriteSavedPlan tSavedPlan ThePlan
        Handle hoJson
        UChar[] ucJson
        Integer iCh
        String sFile

        Move CI_SavedPlanVersion to ThePlan.iVersion
        Move "" to ThePlan.sSourcePrnStamp
        If (ThePlan.bDeepScan = False and Trim(ThePlan.sSourcePrn) <> "") ;
            Get SourcePrnStamp ThePlan.sSourcePrn to ThePlan.sSourcePrnStamp
        Get Create (RefClass(cJsonObject)) to hoJson
        Send DataTypeToJson of hoJson ThePlan
        Set peWhiteSpace of hoJson to jpWhitespace_Pretty
        Get StringifyUtf8 of hoJson to ucJson
        Send Destroy of hoJson
        Get SavedPlanJsonPath to sFile
        Get rfOpenOutput sFile to iCh
        If (iCh < 0) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Write channel iCh ucJson
        Send rfCloseOutput iCh
    End_Procedure

    /* Read the saved plan for the current workspace. Returns False if absent, unparseable, a stale
       layout (iVersion <> CI_SavedPlanVersion) - or, for a compile plan, one whose PROVENANCE no longer
       holds (psSavedPlanStaleWhy then says why; "" in every other case). On True, ThePlan is filled.
       Mirrors ReadRecipesFromJson.

       THE PROVENANCE CHECK (Nils 2026-08-17): the plan file is keyed by the workspace's PATH, so wiping
       E:\WinServ and copying it again handed the new copy the OLD copy's last Check - 42 errors on the
       board before a single compile had run here - and the copied WINSERV.prn made the restore's
       re-parse agree. A compile plan is therefore accepted only while sSourcePrn is still there AND
       carries the stamp recorded at write time. Gone, unstamped (written before 2026-08-17) or rewritten
       since -> False, with the reason on psSavedPlanStaleWhy for the caller to show. A Deep Scan plan
       has no .prn and is not judged this way. Missing is deliberately stale too: the copy step no
       longer carries .prn/.err across, so a fresh copy has none - and that copy must NOT inherit. */
    Function ReadSavedPlan tSavedPlan ByRef ThePlan Returns Boolean
        Handle hoJson
        tSavedPlan ThePlanEmpty
        String sFile sNow sWhy

        Set psSavedPlanStaleWhy to ""
        Get SavedPlanJsonPath to sFile
        Get rfParseJsonFile sFile to hoJson
        If (hoJson = 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        Get JsonToDataType of hoJson to ThePlan
        Send Destroy of hoJson
        If (ThePlan.iVersion <> CI_SavedPlanVersion) Begin
            Move ThePlanEmpty to ThePlan
            Function_Return False
        End
        // Provenance - compile plans only (see the header note).
        If (ThePlan.bDeepScan = False and Trim(ThePlan.sSourcePrn) <> "") Begin
            Move "" to sWhy
            Get SourcePrnStamp ThePlan.sSourcePrn to sNow
            If (sNow = "") ;
                Move ("its compile output is no longer there (" + Trim(ThePlan.sSourcePrn) + ") - a fresh copy, or cleaned since.") to sWhy
            Else If (Trim(ThePlan.sSourcePrnStamp) = "") ;
                Move "it was saved before DFRefactor recorded which compile output it came from, so it cannot be tied to this workspace as it stands." to sWhy
            Else If (sNow <> ThePlan.sSourcePrnStamp) ;
                Move ("the compile output it was read from (" + Trim(ThePlan.sSourcePrn) + ") has been written since.") to sWhy
            If (sWhy <> "") Begin
                Set psSavedPlanStaleWhy to ("The last Check for this workspace (" + Trim(ThePlan.sDate) + ") is not shown because " + sWhy + " Run Check to get a current one.")
                Move ThePlanEmpty to ThePlan
                Function_Return False
            End
        End
        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    // Per-workspace path of the analyzer ignore-list (Data\Ignored-<wskey>.json). Mirrors the saved-plan
    // path (same Data folder + workspace fold), swapping the file base - both bases come from their CS_*Json
    // Defines (drop the ".json" suffix), so a rename of either constant stays consistent here.
    Function IgnoredJsonPath Returns String
        String sPath sSavedBase sIgnoredBase
        Get SavedPlanJsonPath to sPath
        Move (Left(CS_SavedPlanJson, (Length(CS_SavedPlanJson) - 5))) to sSavedBase     // "LastPlan"
        Move (Left(CS_IgnoredJson,   (Length(CS_IgnoredJson)   - 5))) to sIgnoredBase   // "Ignored"
        Function_Return (Replaces(sSavedBase, sPath, sIgnoredBase))
    End_Function

    // Read the dismissed-row list for the current workspace. Empty array when absent / unparseable /
    // stale layout (iVersion <> CI_IgnoredVersion). Mirrors ReadSavedPlan.
    Function ReadIgnored Returns tModIgnore[]
        Handle hoJson
        tModIgnoreFile TheFile
        tModIgnore[] aEmpty
        String sFile

        Get IgnoredJsonPath to sFile
        Get rfParseJsonFile sFile to hoJson
        If (hoJson = 0) ;
            Function_Return aEmpty
        Get JsonToDataType of hoJson to TheFile
        Send Destroy of hoJson
        If (TheFile.iVersion <> CI_IgnoredVersion) ;
            Function_Return aEmpty
        Function_Return TheFile.aRows
    End_Function

    // Persist the dismissed-row list (pretty, NO BOM - a BOM breaks ParseUtf8). Mirrors WriteSavedPlan.
    Procedure WriteIgnored tModIgnore[] aRows
        Handle hoJson
        UChar[] ucJson
        Integer iCh
        String sFile
        tModIgnoreFile TheFile

        Move CI_IgnoredVersion to TheFile.iVersion
        Move aRows to TheFile.aRows
        Get Create (RefClass(cJsonObject)) to hoJson
        Send DataTypeToJson of hoJson TheFile
        Set peWhiteSpace of hoJson to jpWhitespace_Pretty
        Get StringifyUtf8 of hoJson to ucJson
        Send Destroy of hoJson
        Get IgnoredJsonPath to sFile
        Get rfOpenOutput sFile to iCh
        If (iCh < 0) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Write channel iCh ucJson
        Send rfCloseOutput iCh
    End_Procedure

    // Split a Msg# grid cell into its individual "W/E <n>" tokens. A MERGED row (a function-backed row, or
    // several messages sharing one file+line) renders the cell as a ", "-joined LIST, e.g.
    // "W 4532, W 4533, W 4534". IgnoreSelectedRow must ignore EACH token, because DropIgnoredMessages /
    // IsIgnored key per SINGLE message ("W <n>") - storing the whole list as one key never matches, so
    // ignoring a merged row did nothing (the row stayed on screen). PURE, so it is unit-testable.
    Function SplitMsgList String sMsgCell Returns String[]
        String[] asTokens
        String sRest sTok
        Integer iComma

        Move (Trim(sMsgCell)) to sRest
        While (sRest <> "")
            Move (Pos(", ", sRest)) to iComma
            If (iComma > 0) Begin
                Move (Trim(Left(sRest, iComma - 1))) to sTok
                Move (Mid(sRest, CI_EOL, iComma + 2)) to sRest
            End
            Else Begin
                Move (Trim(sRest)) to sTok
                Move "" to sRest
            End
            If (sTok <> "") ;
                Move sTok to asTokens[SizeOfArray(asTokens)]
        Loop
        Function_Return asTokens
    End_Function

    /* How many messages a row actually stands for. A row that folded repeated occurrences renders its
       line cell with a multiplier - "256  (x7)" - and the row is then ONE row speaking for seven
       findings. Anything counting rows understates by exactly that factor: the read-only packages band
       said "2 warning(s)" over a package holding 7 (Nils 2026-08-30, the one file it names).

       1 for an ordinary cell, and 1 for anything malformed - a count must never come back as 0 and
       make a real finding vanish from a tally. PURE, so it is unit-testable. */
    Function OccurrencesFromLineCell String sLineCell Returns Integer
        String  sRest sNum sCh
        Integer iAt iClose iLen i iCount

        Move (Pos("(x", Lowercase(Trim(sLineCell)))) to iAt
        If (iAt = 0) ;
            Function_Return 1
        Move (Trim(sLineCell)) to sRest
        Move (Mid(sRest, (Length(sRest) - (iAt + 1)), (iAt + 2))) to sRest
        Move (Pos(")", sRest)) to iClose
        If (iClose < 2) ;
            Function_Return 1
        Move (Trim(Left(sRest, (iClose - 1)))) to sNum
        // Digits only - Move-ing a non-numeric token to an Integer FAULTS in DF26, it does not
        // quietly yield 0 (the .err parser learned this the hard way).
        Move (Length(sNum)) to iLen
        If (iLen = 0) ;
            Function_Return 1
        For i from 1 to iLen
            Move (Mid(sNum, 1, i)) to sCh
            If (sCh < "0" or sCh > "9") ;
                Function_Return 1
        Loop
        Move sNum to iCount
        If (iCount < 1) ;
            Function_Return 1
        Function_Return iCount
    End_Function

    /* Does asOcc - a folded row's absorbed occurrences, "line<TAB>msg#<TAB>text" each - already hold a
       message on iLine? Matched up to the TAB, so 27 is not 270.

       The grid's fold asks this before a Deep Scan hit counts as a new place (Nils 2026-09-11:
       "27 (x6)" over three GetAddress lines). The scan lane is appended after the compiler lane and
       often finds the same construct the compiler warned about, so counting messages doubled every
       such row. PURE, so it is unit-testable. */
    Function OccurrencesHoldLine String[] asOcc Integer iLine Returns Boolean
        String sPrefix
        Integer i iMax iLen

        Move (String(iLine) + Character(9)) to sPrefix
        Move (Length(sPrefix)) to iLen
        Move (SizeOfArray(asOcc) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (Left(asOcc[i], iLen) = sPrefix) ;
                Function_Return True
        Loop
        Function_Return False
    End_Function

    /* The ignore list, pre-normalised ONCE into the two things a per-message test actually needs: the
       distinct FILES it mentions, and the full "<lowercased file>|<msg token>" keys.

       IsIgnored re-normalised BOTH SIDES of every comparison - Lowercase(Trim(...)) on the row and on the
       argument, inside its loop. With 40,087 messages against 552 ignore rows that is 22 million
       comparisons and something like 110 million string allocations: 16,922 ms of a 72-second Analyze,
       measured 2026-07-22. Passing the list ByRef first changed nothing, because the copy was never the
       cost - the comparisons were.

       The file list is the lever. 552 ignore rows name only a handful of distinct files, so a message
       whose file is not among them can be settled by one small search and never built into a key at all,
       which is almost every message. PURE -> unit-testable. */
    Function IgnoreFilesFromList tModIgnore[] ByRef aRows Returns String[]
        String[] asFiles
        String sFile
        Integer i iLast
        Move (SizeOfArray(aRows) - 1) to iLast
        For i from 0 to iLast
            Move (Lowercase(Trim(aRows[i].sFile))) to sFile
            If (SearchArray(sFile, asFiles) = -1) ;
                Move sFile to asFiles[SizeOfArray(asFiles)]
        Loop
        // SORTED, because the caller looks these up with BinarySearchArray - which, like DataFlex's
        // own cBaseErrorHandler, requires a sorted array (it SortArrays paUserErrors for exactly this).
        Move (SortArray(asFiles)) to asFiles
        Function_Return asFiles
    End_Function

    // The same list as full "<lowercased file>|<msg token>" keys, normalised once. A DataFlex path or
    // message token cannot contain "|", so the separator is unambiguous. PURE -> unit-testable.
    Function IgnoreKeysFromList tModIgnore[] ByRef aRows Returns String[]
        String[] asKeys
        Integer i iLast
        Move (SizeOfArray(aRows) - 1) to iLast
        For i from 0 to iLast
            Move (Lowercase(Trim(aRows[i].sFile)) + "|" + Trim(aRows[i].sMsg)) to asKeys[i]
        Loop
        Move (SortArray(asKeys)) to asKeys
        Function_Return asKeys
    End_Function

    // True when (sFile, sMsg) is in the ignore-list. PURE (takes the list) so it is unit-testable.
    // File path is matched case-insensitively (Windows); the message token exactly as the grid renders it.
    //
    // ByRef, and it matters more here than anywhere: DropIgnoredMessages calls this ONCE PER MESSAGE -
    // 40,000 times on a big workspace - and a by-value struct-array parameter deep-copies the whole
    // ignore list at every one of those calls. Measured 2026-07-22: 16,922 ms of a 72-second Analyze,
    // in a function whose actual work is a walk of a handful of rows. Note the copy was at the INNER
    // call, not the outer one - putting ByRef on DropIgnoredMessages first changed nothing at all.
    Function IsIgnored String sFile String sMsg tModIgnore[] ByRef aRows Returns Boolean
        Integer i iSize
        String sWantFile sWantMsg

        // Normalise the ARGUMENT once, not once per row. The high-volume path no longer comes
        // through here (DropIgnoredMessages uses the pre-normalised key set), but the same waste
        // was in here for every caller.
        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sFile))) to sWantFile
        Move (Trim(sMsg)) to sWantMsg
        Move (SizeOfArray(aRows) - 1) to iSize
        For i from 0 to iSize
            If ((Lowercase(Trim(aRows[i].sFile)) = sWantFile) and (Trim(aRows[i].sMsg) = sWantMsg)) ;
                Function_Return True
        Loop
        Function_Return False
    End_Function

    // True when a GRID/SAVED-PLAN ROW is dismissed - i.e. EVERY one of its Msg# tokens is in the ignore
    // list. sMsgCell may be a ", "-joined LIST for a merged row (e.g. "W 4532, W 4533, W 4534"), but the
    // flat ignore list keys per SINGLE message ("W <n>"), so a raw IsIgnored on the whole cell never
    // matches a merged row - the dismissed row then leaks back (it showed in "View last plan" while the
    // live grid, which drops per single message, correctly hid it). Split the cell and require ALL tokens
    // ignored, mirroring DropIgnoredMessages (a row vanishes only when every one of its messages is
    // dropped). PURE, so it is unit-testable. (Nils, LegacyApp cMapiHandler 315, 2026-07-01.)
    Function IsRowIgnored String sFile String sMsgCell tModIgnore[] ByRef aRows Returns Boolean
        String[] asTok
        Integer i iSize
        Boolean bHit

        Get SplitMsgList sMsgCell to asTok
        Move (SizeOfArray(asTok)) to iSize
        If (iSize = 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        Decrement iSize
        For i from 0 to iSize
            Get IsIgnored sFile asTok[i] (&aRows) to bHit
            If (bHit = False) ;
                Function_Return False
        Loop
        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    // Return aMsgs minus every message whose (file, "W/E <n>" token) is in the ignore-list. The grid
    // applies this BEFORE scoping + display, so a dismissed row leaves BOTH the grid and the refactor
    // plan (Auto Select). A no-op (returns aMsgs unchanged) when the list is empty. The token is built
    // exactly as LoadData renders the Msg# cell, so the key round-trips through the saved JSON.
    // ByRef on the incoming array: a by-value struct-array parameter deep-copies at the CALL
    // boundary, and this one is handed 40,000 tCompileError structs on a big workspace. Measured
    // 2026-07-22 - the four filters over that array cost ~20s of a 72s Analyze between them.
    // Standing rule from Nils: arrays and struct arrays are passed ByRef by default.
    Function DropIgnoredMessages tCompileError[] ByRef aMsgs tModIgnore[] ByRef aIgnored Returns tCompileError[]
        tCompileError[] aKept
        Integer i iSize iKept
        String sMsgTok sLcFile
        String[] asIgnoreFiles asIgnoreKeys
        Boolean bIgnoredHit

        If (SizeOfArray(aIgnored) = 0) ;
            Function_Return aMsgs
        // Normalise ONCE for the whole pass, not on every comparison.
        Get IgnoreFilesFromList (&aIgnored) to asIgnoreFiles
        Get IgnoreKeysFromList  (&aIgnored) to asIgnoreKeys
        Move (SizeOfArray(aMsgs) - 1) to iSize
        Move 0 to iKept
        For i from 0 to iSize
            // Token exactly as LoadData renders the Msg# cell - a Deep Scan row wears "DS <n>"
            // (without this, an IGNORED scan row still entered Auto Select: the drop looked for
            // "W 9010" while the list held "DS 9010"; Nils 2026-07-11).
            If (aMsgs[i].bDeepScan) Begin
                Move ("DS " + String(aMsgs[i].iErrorNo)) to sMsgTok
            End
            Else If (aMsgs[i].bError) Begin
                Move ("E " + String(aMsgs[i].iErrorNo)) to sMsgTok
            End
            Else Begin
                Move ("W " + String(aMsgs[i].iErrorNo)) to sMsgTok
            End
            // FILE GATE FIRST. asIgnoreFiles holds only the distinct files the list mentions, so the
            // overwhelming majority of messages are settled by one small search and never have a key
            // built for them at all.
            Move (Lowercase(Trim(aMsgs[i].sFileName))) to sLcFile
            Move False to bIgnoredHit
            // BinarySearchArray, not SearchArray: both sets are sorted above, and this runs once per
            // message. DataFlex ships this and uses it 76 times in its own packages; DFRefactor had
            // not used it once, while the analyzer alone holds 28 linear SearchArray calls
            // (Nils asked the right question, 2026-07-22).
            If (BinarySearchArray(sLcFile, asIgnoreFiles) <> -1) ;
                Move (BinarySearchArray((sLcFile + "|" + sMsgTok), asIgnoreKeys) <> -1) to bIgnoredHit
            If (bIgnoredHit = False) Begin
                Move aMsgs[i] to aKept[iKept]
                Increment iKept
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return aKept
    End_Function

    // Return aMessages minus every message whose file is a DataFlex Studio COM proxy wrapper (its FIRST line
    // is the "DataFlex COM proxy classes generated from ..." marker). Those files are excluded from the
    // refactor at collection (cRefactorApplication) and skipped at processing (cRefactorEngine) - they are
    // never modernised - so their compiler diagnostics are noise in the triage grid and must never enter the
    // plan. Called by BOTH ingestion paths (the grid in ModAnalyzerFT.dg AND PlanFromMessages) so the grid
    // and the plan stay in lock-step. Detection is the ONE canonical _IsDataFlexCOMProxyClassesFile
    // (cRefactorUtilities), cached per unique file (it opens + reads the first line). Empty / missing file
    // names (link errors; a file deleted since compile) are KEPT - never a proxy, and the helper errors on a
    // missing file, so it is only called for a name that exists.
    // ByRef on the incoming array: a by-value struct-array parameter deep-copies at the CALL
    // boundary, and this one is handed 40,000 tCompileError structs on a big workspace. Measured
    // 2026-07-22 - the four filters over that array cost ~20s of a 72s Analyze between them.
    // Standing rule from Nils: arrays and struct arrays are passed ByRef by default.
    Function DropCOMProxyMessages tCompileError[] ByRef aMessages Returns tCompileError[]
        tCompileError[] aKept
        String[] asSeenFile
        Boolean[] abSeenProxy
        Integer i iSize iIdx iKept
        Boolean bProxy bExists
        String sFile

        Move (SizeOfArray(aMessages) - 1) to iSize
        Move 0 to iKept
        For i from 0 to iSize
            Move aMessages[i].sFileName to sFile
            Move (SearchArray(sFile, asSeenFile)) to iIdx
            If (iIdx = -1) Begin
                Move False to bProxy
                If (sFile <> "") Begin
                    Get vFilePathExists sFile to bExists
                    If (bExists = True) ;
                        Get _IsDataFlexCOMProxyClassesFile of ghoFuncLib sFile to bProxy
                End
                Move sFile  to asSeenFile[SizeOfArray(asSeenFile)]
                Move bProxy to abSeenProxy[SizeOfArray(abSeenProxy)]
            End
            Else ;
                Move abSeenProxy[iIdx] to bProxy
            /* THE ONE PROXY MESSAGE THAT MUST SURVIVE. This filter exists because findings inside
               a generated wrapper are noise - the file is never refactored. DS 9220 is the
               opposite: its entire subject IS the proxy file (a FlexCOM-era wrapper that halts
               all refactoring), and the sweep that produced it read the file on purpose.
               Dropping it here silently unmade that work (2026-08-27). */
            If (bProxy = False or aMessages[i].iErrorNo = 9220) Begin
                Move aMessages[i] to aKept[iKept]
                Increment iKept
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return aKept
    End_Function

    // Build the actionable plan (distinct refactor function names + distinct workspace files) straight from
    // a SAVED plan's frozen rows - the path "Refactor Plan" takes when the analyzer grid is in saved-view
    // mode (View last plan), where no live messages exist. Only Auto-fix / Review rows that carry a refactor
    // function contribute; a row's Fn cell can be a ", "-joined list (the grid merges several functions per
    // file row), so each is split out and de-duped. Produces the same tModRefactorPlan as PlanFromMessages.
    // IsRowIgnored against PRE-NORMALISED sorted ignore keys (IgnoreFilesFromList / IgnoreKeysFromList
    // + BinarySearchArray) - same semantics, built for the per-row loop below where the original
    // (which re-normalises the whole ignore list per call) is the 17-second DropIgnoredMessages
    // pattern all over again. A row is dismissed only when EVERY token of its (possibly ", "-joined)
    // Msg cell is ignored; an empty token list is never dismissed. File-gate first: a file the ignore
    // list never mentions settles in one binary search.
    Function _IsRowIgnoredFast String sFile String sMsgCell String[] ByRef asIgnoreFiles String[] ByRef asIgnoreKeys Returns Boolean
        String[] asTok
        String sFileLc
        Integer i iSize

        If (SizeOfArray(asIgnoreKeys) = 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sFile))) to sFileLc
        If (BinarySearchArray(sFileLc, asIgnoreFiles) = -1) ;
            Function_Return False
        Get SplitMsgList sMsgCell to asTok
        Move (SizeOfArray(asTok)) to iSize
        If (iSize = 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        For i from 0 to (iSize - 1)
            If (BinarySearchArray((sFileLc + "|" + Trim(asTok[i])), asIgnoreKeys) = -1) ;
                Function_Return False
        Loop
        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    // asExtraLineKeys: additional "<pathLc>|<line>" keys for the companion oracle. A merged grid row
    // carries only its FIRST line, but its folded occurrences are more warned indicator-use lines -
    // the live caller passes them (IndicatorOccurrenceLineKeys) so the companion test sees EVERY
    // warned line, not one per row. The saved-plan caller passes an empty array (a saved plan
    // carries no occurrences).
    Function PlanFromSavedRows tSavedPlanRow[] aRows tModIgnore[] aIgnored String[] asExtraLineKeys Returns tModRefactorPlan
        tModRefactorPlan Plan
        Integer i iSize iPos
        String sFns sFn sFile sLastPlanFile
        String[] asIndicatorFiles asIndicatorLineKeys asIgnoreFiles asIgnoreKeys
        Boolean bIgnored

        // aIgnored = the per-workspace Ignore list (the caller's ReadIgnored) - honoured here too.
        // The saved-plan JSON deliberately keeps ALL rows (ignores are a view-layer concern
        // re-applied at every load), and the saved-VIEW grid already skips them (LoadSavedPlan's
        // IsRowIgnored) - but this builder read the raw rows, so an IGNORED row's function came
        // back ticked in Run Plan after a restart (Nils's WinServ lap, 2026-07-17). Same
        // merged-cell-aware matcher as the grid: grid == plan, always.
        // Normalise the ignore list ONCE for the whole pass - per-row IsRowIgnored re-normalised all
        // of it per call, the exact shape that cost 17s in DropIgnoredMessages (2026-07-22).
        Get IgnoreFilesFromList (&aIgnored) to asIgnoreFiles
        Get IgnoreKeysFromList  (&aIgnored) to asIgnoreKeys
        Move (SizeOfArray(aRows) - 1) to iSize
        For i from 0 to iSize
            If (phoUIYieldTarget(Self) <> 0 and (Mod(i, 64) = 0)) ;
                Send DoYieldToUI of (phoUIYieldTarget(Self))
            Move (Trim(aRows[i].sFn)) to sFns
            Move False to bIgnored
            If (sFns <> "") ;
                Get _IsRowIgnoredFast aRows[i].sFile aRows[i].sMsg (&asIgnoreFiles) (&asIgnoreKeys) to bIgnored
            If (((aRows[i].eCategory = eModAutoFix) or (aRows[i].eCategory = eModSuggest)) and (sFns <> "") and (bIgnored = False)) Begin
                Move (Trim(aRows[i].sFile)) to sFile
                // Functions - the Fn cell may hold a ", "-joined list of names.
                While (sFns <> "")
                    Move (Pos(", ", sFns)) to iPos
                    If (iPos > 0) Begin
                        Move (Trim(Left(sFns, iPos - 1)))                  to sFn
                        Move (Trim(Mid(sFns, (Length(sFns)), (iPos + 2)))) to sFns
                    End
                    Else Begin
                        Move sFns to sFn
                        Move ""   to sFns
                    End
                    If ((sFn <> "") and (SearchArray(sFn, Plan.asFunctions) = -1)) Begin
                        Move sFn to Plan.asFunctions[SizeOfArray(Plan.asFunctions)]
                        // Keep asFunctionHints PARALLEL to asFunctions (carry the row's suggested-fix hint) -
                        // PlanFromMessages does, and downstream code indexes both by the same i,
                        // so a short hints array here would index out of bounds (err 4509) on Run Modernization Plan.
                        Move (Trim(aRows[i].sHint)) to Plan.asFunctionHints[SizeOfArray(Plan.asFunctionHints)]
                    End
                    // Collect this row's file when a split-out function is an indicator converter (distinct) -
                    // the only files AddNeededIndicatorCompanions needs to scan.
                    If (_IsIndicatorConverterName(Self, sFn) and (sFile <> "")) Begin
                        If (SearchArray(sFile, asIndicatorFiles) = -1) ;
                            Move sFile to asIndicatorFiles[SizeOfArray(asIndicatorFiles)]
                        Move (Lowercase(sFile) + "|" + String(aRows[i].iLine)) to asIndicatorLineKeys[SizeOfArray(asIndicatorLineKeys)]
                    End
                Loop
                // File - de-duped. Saved rows are one-per-(file,fn) in the grid's file-grouped order, so the
                // last-file short-circuit collapses the SearchArray the same way PlanFromMessages does; the
                // SearchArray stays as the correctness fallback if the rows ever arrive un-grouped (no dup).
                If ((sFile <> "") and (sFile <> sLastPlanFile)) Begin
                    If (SearchArray(sFile, Plan.asFiles) = -1) ;
                        Move sFile to Plan.asFiles[SizeOfArray(Plan.asFiles)]
                    Move sFile to sLastPlanFile
                End
            End
        Loop
        // The live caller's per-occurrence keys ride along - see the header.
        For i from 0 to (SizeOfArray(asExtraLineKeys) - 1)
            Move asExtraLineKeys[i] to asIndicatorLineKeys[SizeOfArray(asIndicatorLineKeys)]
        Loop
        Send AddNeededIndicatorCompanions (&Plan) asIndicatorFiles asIndicatorLineKeys
        Function_Return Plan
    End_Function

    /* THE FILES THE TICKED FUNCTIONS ACTUALLY NEED (2026-08-16). The plan dialog offers two tick
       lists - functions and files - and the file list stayed the union of EVERY row's file whatever
       the user unticked among the functions. Nils approved only the auto-fixes of a plan whose 784
       files came mostly from Review rows (539 square-bracket indicators, 363 dbGrids ...): "Total
       number of files changed: 31 out of 784 Files. Only 31 changed out of 784? That seems like a
       big waste of time". It was - 750 files opened and tokenised for nothing.

       Given the plan's rows (+ the collapsed rules' file lists) and the functions the user ticked,
       returns the distinct files that at least one ticked function has an actionable, non-dismissed
       row in - the same predicate PlanFromSavedRows / ExpandFloodFilesIntoPlan use to build the
       plan, applied per function. The caller intersects the dialog's ticked files with this, so a
       file the user unticked stays out too. PURE -> unit-tested (oModAnalyzer-Tests). */
    Function FilesForFunctions tSavedPlanRow[] ByRef aRows tSavedPlanFlood[] ByRef aFloods tModIgnore[] aIgnored String[] asFns Returns String[]
        String[] asOut asIgnoreFiles asIgnoreKeys
        String sFns sFn sFile
        Integer i j iSize iPos jMax
        Boolean bIgnored bWanted

        Get IgnoreFilesFromList (&aIgnored) to asIgnoreFiles
        Get IgnoreKeysFromList  (&aIgnored) to asIgnoreKeys
        Move (SizeOfArray(aRows) - 1) to iSize
        For i from 0 to iSize
            Move (Trim(aRows[i].sFn)) to sFns
            If (((aRows[i].eCategory = eModAutoFix) or (aRows[i].eCategory = eModSuggest)) and (sFns <> "") and (Trim(aRows[i].sFile) <> "")) Begin
                Get _IsRowIgnoredFast aRows[i].sFile aRows[i].sMsg (&asIgnoreFiles) (&asIgnoreKeys) to bIgnored
                If (bIgnored = False) Begin
                    // any of the row's (", "-joined) functions ticked?
                    Move False to bWanted
                    While (sFns <> "" and bWanted = False)
                        Move (Pos(", ", sFns)) to iPos
                        If (iPos > 0) Begin
                            Move (Trim(Left(sFns, iPos - 1)))                  to sFn
                            Move (Trim(Mid(sFns, (Length(sFns)), (iPos + 2)))) to sFns
                        End
                        Else Begin
                            Move sFns to sFn
                            Move ""   to sFns
                        End
                        If (SearchArray(sFn, asFns, Desktop, RefFunc(DFSTRICMP)) <> -1) ;
                            Move True to bWanted
                    Loop
                    If (bWanted = True) Begin
                        Move (Trim(aRows[i].sFile)) to sFile
                        If (SearchArray(sFile, asOut, Desktop, RefFunc(DFSTRICMP)) = -1) ;
                            Move sFile to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                    End
                End
            End
        Loop
        Move (SizeOfArray(aFloods) - 1) to iSize
        For i from 0 to iSize
            Move (Trim(aFloods[i].sFn)) to sFn
            If (sFn <> "" and SearchArray(sFn, asFns, Desktop, RefFunc(DFSTRICMP)) <> -1) Begin
                Get IsRowIgnored aFloods[i].sFirstFile ("DS " + String(aFloods[i].iDSNumber)) (&aIgnored) to bIgnored
                If (bIgnored = False) Begin
                    Move (SizeOfArray(aFloods[i].asFiles) - 1) to jMax
                    For j from 0 to jMax
                        Move (Trim(aFloods[i].asFiles[j])) to sFile
                        If (sFile <> "" and SearchArray(sFile, asOut, Desktop, RefFunc(DFSTRICMP)) = -1) ;
                            Move sFile to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                    Loop
                End
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return asOut
    End_Function

    /* THE 4546 CALL-SITE RENAME, part 1 (2026-08-16): which name on this line is called
       function-style? E 4546 "Illegal runtime function in expression" fires when a MESSAGE
       (a procedure - sql.pkg's SQLExecute is the classic) is called inside an expression, and the
       usual cause is a call left pointing at the old name after the workspace's own wrapper was
       renamed (Nils renamed SQLExecute -> SQLExecuteX by hand; the two calls stayed). The compiler
       does not name the offender, so this reads the line: every identifier immediately followed by
       "(" (spaces allowed), on the CODE-ONLY text (literals masked - "SELECT CONVERT(" inside the SQL
       string is not a call), minus DataFlex's own global functions (asBuiltins - the install
       catalogue: Trim, String, ...) and reserved words (If, Not, ...). Usually exactly one survives.
       PURE -> unit-tested (oModAnalyzer-Tests). */
    Function CallNameCandidates String sCodeLine String[] asBuiltins Returns String[]
        String[] asOut
        String sLine sCh sName
        Integer i iLen iStart j
        Boolean bReserved

        Get _CodeOnlyLine of ghoFuncLib sCodeLine to sLine
        Move (Length(sLine)) to iLen
        Move 1 to i
        While (i <= iLen)
            Move (Mid(sLine, 1, i)) to sCh
            If (_IsIdentifierChar(ghoFuncLib, sCh) and not(sCh >= "0" and sCh <= "9")) Begin
                Move i to iStart
                While (i <= iLen and _IsIdentifierChar(ghoFuncLib, Mid(sLine, 1, i)))
                    Increment i
                Loop
                Move (Mid(sLine, (i - iStart), iStart)) to sName
                // skip spaces, then a "(" makes it a call
                Move i to j
                While (j <= iLen and Mid(sLine, 1, j) = " ")
                    Increment j
                Loop
                If (j <= iLen and Mid(sLine, 1, j) = "(") Begin
                    Get _IsReservedWord of ghoFuncLib sName to bReserved
                    If (bReserved = False and SearchArray(sName, asBuiltins, Desktop, RefFunc(DFSTRICMP)) = -1) Begin
                        If (SearchArray(sName, asOut, Desktop, RefFunc(DFSTRICMP)) = -1) ;
                            Move sName to asOut[SizeOfArray(asOut)]
                    End
                End
            End
            Else ;
                Increment i
        Loop
        Function_Return asOut
    End_Function

    /* WHY IS THE CONSTRUCT NOT ON THIS LINE? (2026-08-19)

       ClassifyGuardedRecipe establishes only a NEGATIVE - the reported construct is not on the line the
       compiler pointed at - and then labelled every such row "inside macro expansions". On Nils's
       WinServ that lumped 533 rows under one cause that is wrong for most of them:

           Object SnabbProspekt is a cTrDbView      <- W 4538, and no macro anywhere near it
           Set_Foreign_profile_String sKey sSub ...   <- W 4533, genuinely a macro command

       The line itself says which it is, and the row already carries the file and line. Returns
       eMacroCauseClass (sName = the CLASS being instantiated - the technique lives in that class),
       eMacroCauseMacro (sName = the command/macro invoked on this line), or eMacroCauseUnknown
       (sName = "" - and then the row must SHOW THE LINE rather than name a cause it does not know).

       A statement keyword in first position means the line is ordinary code, not a macro invocation:
       there the honest answer is Unknown. PURE -> unit-tested (oModAnalyzer-Tests). */
    Function MacroExpansionCause String sCodeLine String ByRef sName Returns Integer
        String sLine sLow sW1 sW2 sW3 sW4

        Move "" to sName
        Get _CodeOnlyLine of ghoFuncLib sCodeLine to sLine
        Move (Trim(sLine)) to sLine
        If (sLine = "") ;
            Function_Return eMacroCauseUnknown
        Move (Lowercase(sLine)) to sLow
        Get _NthWord of ghoFuncLib sLow 1 to sW1
        // "Object <name> is a <Class>" - the construct is in the class, not here.
        If (sW1 = "object") Begin
            Get _NthWord of ghoFuncLib sLow 3 to sW3
            If (sW3 = "is") Begin
                Get _NthWord of ghoFuncLib sLine 5 to sW4      // as spelled, for display
                If (Trim(sW4) <> "") Begin
                    Move (Trim(sW4)) to sName
                    Function_Return eMacroCauseClass
                End
            End
        End
        // An ordinary statement is not a macro invocation - say Unknown rather than name its keyword.
        If (sW1 = "move" or sW1 = "send" or sW1 = "get" or sW1 = "set" or sW1 = "if" or sW1 = "else" ;
            or sW1 = "while" or sW1 = "for" or sW1 = "loop" or sW1 = "begin" or sW1 = "end" ;
            or sW1 = "function" or sW1 = "procedure" or sW1 = "class" or sW1 = "use" or sW1 = "case" ;
            or sW1 = "repeat" or sW1 = "until" or sW1 = "break" or sW1 = "indicate" or sW1 = "showln" ;
            or sW1 = "return" or sW1 = "abort" or sW1 = "property" or sW1 = "define" or sW1 = "register_object") ;
            Function_Return eMacroCauseUnknown
        // Whatever else leads the line is the command being invoked.
        Get _NthWord of ghoFuncLib sLine 1 to sW2
        Move (Trim(sW2)) to sName
        If (sName = "") ;
            Function_Return eMacroCauseUnknown
        Function_Return eMacroCauseMacro
    End_Function

    /* WHERE IS THIS MACRO DEFINED, in the file that invokes it? The 1-based line of its
       '#COMMAND <name>' declaration in asLines, or 0 when the file does not carry it.

       Deliberately ONE FILE - the lines the caller has already read for the source guard - and not
       a workspace scan. FindDefinitionSite answers the general question by reading every file in
       the compiled scope PER NAME, which is what made the findings board crawl on a large
       workspace before its index existed (see DashboardFT's pasIdxKey). A macro that warns at its
       use sites is very often declared in the same file, so the free answer is right most of the
       time and the row simply says 'search for it' when it is not. Cost: one pass over lines the
       caller holds anyway.

       The '#' is built with Character(35) for the same reason FindDefinitionSite does it: a literal
       directive token inside a .pkg string is consumed by FMAC during OUR own compile.
       PURE -> unit-tested (oModAnalyzer-Tests). */
    Function MacroDefinitionLine String[] ByRef asLines String sName Returns Integer
        String sLowName sLow sW2
        Integer j jMax

        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sName))) to sLowName
        If (sLowName = "") ;
            Function_Return 0
        Move (SizeOfArray(asLines) - 1) to jMax
        For j from 0 to jMax
            Move (Lowercase(Trim(asLines[j]))) to sLow
            If (Left(sLow, 9) = (Character(35) + "command ")) Begin
                Get _NthWord of ghoFuncLib sLow 2 to sW2
                If (sW2 = sLowName) ;
                    Function_Return (j + 1)
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return 0
    End_Function

    /* THE OBSOLETE COMMAND THIS MESSAGE IS ABOUT, lowercased - "Item_List" out of "Obsolete Command
       Obsolete command: Item_List" - or "" when the message is not of that family.

       It exists so a row that was NEVER auto-fixable can still be recognised as a macro expansion.
       VerifyAutoFix establishes that fact for a row it demotes (Guard D: the command keyword is not
       on the reported line), but it only guards a recipe that was Auto-fix or Review - a message
       matching only a GENERIC Explain recipe returns before any guard runs, and 20 Item_List rows
       from a single command body stayed one row per call site because of it (Nils 2026-09-09, on his
       own workspace, after the first cut of the fold). Reading the command out of the message lets
       the caller run the same test for itself.

       Cut at the first '.', because several messages carry advice after the name ("Obsolete command:
       Return. If a gosub, use Gosub_Return") and only the name is the thing to look for on the line.
       Matched on a LOWERCASED copy - Pos is case-sensitive, and the compiler's own casing varies.
       PURE -> unit-tested (oModAnalyzer-Tests). */
    Function ObsoleteCommandInMessage String sMsgTxt Returns String
        String sLow sRest sName
        Integer iPos iDot

        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sMsgTxt))) to sLow
        Move (Pos("command: ", sLow)) to iPos
        If (iPos = 0) ;
            Function_Return ""
        // Full length as the length argument, clamped by Mid - the idiom _GosubReturnKind and
        // _GotoTarget already use for "the text after this keyword", and one less thing to get
        // off by one.
        Move (Mid(sLow, Length(sLow), (iPos + 9))) to sRest
        Move (Pos(".", sRest)) to iDot
        If (iDot > 0) ;
            Move (Left(sRest, (iDot - 1))) to sRest
        Get _NthWord of ghoFuncLib sRest 1 to sName
        Function_Return (Trim(sName))
    End_Function

    /* THE FOLDED MACRO-ORIGIN ROW'S HINT. One row now stands for every call site of one command in
       one file, so the hint has to say three things the old shared sentence could not: WHICH command,
       WHERE its body is, and that the lines behind the row are uses rather than faults.

       It ENDS with CS_ModMacroExpansionHint so the family stays recognisable to the findings board
       without matching prose - the board tests for that constant inside the hint (Nils 2026-07-28,
       and the constant's own note). Keep the constant last: putting the named part first is what
       makes the row readable in the grid, where the hint column is what the eye lands on.

       No file argument: MacroDefinitionLine only ever searches the INVOKING file's own lines, so a
       definition it found is by construction in the file this row already names in its File column.
       iDefLine is 0 when it was not found there.
       PURE -> unit-tested (oModAnalyzer-Tests). */
    Function MacroOriginHint String sName Integer iDefLine Returns String
        String sHint

        Move (Trim(sName)) to sHint
        If (sHint = "") ;
            Function_Return CS_ModMacroExpansionHint
        If (iDefLine > 0) ;
            Move (sHint + " is defined in this file at line " + String(iDefLine) + ".") to sHint
        Else ;
            Move (sHint + " is defined elsewhere - search the source for '" + Character(35) + "COMMAND " + Trim(sName) + "'.") to sHint
        Move (sHint + " The lines behind this row are its CALL SITES, folded into one: the compiler") to sHint
        Move (sHint + " repeats the warning at every use of a command whose body carries the construct.") to sHint
        Move (sHint + CS_S + CS_ModMacroExpansionHint) to sHint
        Function_Return sHint
    End_Function

    /* The refactor function that migrates objects of sClass, or "" when DFRefactor has none. Read out
       of the RECIPE TABLE (message 4537, "class: <NAME>" - the obsolete-class family) rather than from a
       second hand-kept mapping, so the offer on a findings row can never disagree with what the grid
       and the plan would do for the same class. 2026-08-19. */
    Function RecipeFunctionForClass String sClass Returns String
        tModAnalyzer[] aRecipes aMatched
        Integer i iLast
        String sUp

        Move (Uppercase(Trim(sClass))) to sUp
        If (sUp = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        // The live table when one has been loaded, else the built-in seed (the same source the grid uses).
        Get paRecipes to aRecipes
        If (SizeOfArray(aRecipes) = 0) ;
            Get BuildDefaultRecipes to aRecipes
        Get MatchingRecipesFromList 4537 ("Obsolete class: " + sUp) (&aRecipes) to aMatched
        Move (SizeOfArray(aMatched) - 1) to iLast
        For i from 0 to iLast
            If (Trim(aMatched[i].sRefactorFn) <> "") ;
                Function_Return (Trim(aMatched[i].sRefactorFn))
        Loop
        Function_Return ""
    End_Function

    /* WHERE IS IT DEFINED? Scans asFiles for the definition of a macro command ("#COMMAND <name>") or a
       class ("Class <name> is a ..."), returning "<file>|<line>" for the first hit, or "" when none of
       the files carries it. That answer decides what the row can offer: a definition inside the
       WORKSPACE is a place the user can go and fix; one that is only in the DataFlex install is not
       theirs to change and the row should say so instead of linking it.

       Scans until the first hit and reads each file once - the caller passes the compiled set and asks
       for a handful of names, not one per row. */
    Function FindDefinitionSite String[] ByRef asFiles String sName Boolean bIsClass Returns String
        String[] asLines
        String sLow sLowName sLine sW1 sW2
        Integer i iMax j jMax

        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sName))) to sLowName
        If (sLowName = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move (SizeOfArray(asFiles) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArray(asFiles[i])) to asLines
            Move (SizeOfArray(asLines) - 1) to jMax
            For j from 0 to jMax
                Move (Lowercase(Trim(asLines[j]))) to sLow
                If (bIsClass = True) Begin
                    // Class <name> is a <parent>
                    Get _NthWord of ghoFuncLib sLow 1 to sW1
                    Get _NthWord of ghoFuncLib sLow 2 to sW2
                    If (sW1 = "class" and sW2 = sLowName) ;
                        Function_Return (asFiles[i] + "|" + String(j + 1))
                End
                Else Begin
                    // #COMMAND <name> ... (the "#" is built with Character(35): a literal directive
                    // token in a .pkg string is consumed by FMAC during OUR own compile).
                    If (Left(sLow, 9) = (Character(35) + "command ")) Begin
                        Get _NthWord of ghoFuncLib sLow 2 to sW2
                        If (sW2 = sLowName) ;
                            Function_Return (asFiles[i] + "|" + String(j + 1))
                    End
                End
            Loop
        Loop
        Function_Return ""
    End_Function

    /* THE 4379 OBJECT-EXPRESSION CALL (2026-08-19): which method does this line call in the
       "Name(Self, args)" form? The form itself is legal DataFlex and STAYS legal in DF 26 (probe-proven
       2026-08-19: the same shape compiles for a method whose name DataFlex does not use). E 4379 fires
       on a NAME COLLISION - DF 26's runtime owns Split, declared in no package - so the call resolves
       to that function and the argument count no longer fits. Nine of Nils's
       "Move (Split(Self, ':-:', saData[i])) to saValues" lines broke at once. The Get form is a message
       send, so it binds to HIS method: Get Split ':-:' saData[i] to saValues.

       The compiler names no symbol for a 4379, so the line is read. Deliberately NOT filtered against
       the install's global functions the way CallNameCandidates is: here the name COLLIDING with a
       built-in is the whole point, so that filter would reject exactly the rows worth offering.
       Reserved words are still rejected, and the first argument must be Self - a call whose receiver is
       another object is not this shape. PURE -> unit-tested (oModAnalyzer-Tests). */
    Function ObjExprCallNameInLine String sCodeLine Returns String
        String sLine sLow sCh sName sLowName
        Integer iPos i

        Get _CodeOnlyLine of ghoFuncLib sCodeLine to sLine
        Move (Lowercase(sLine)) to sLow
        Move (Pos("(self", sLow)) to iPos
        If (iPos = 0) ;
            Function_Return ""
        // "(self" must END there: "(selfish" is a different word.
        Move (Mid(sLow, 1, (iPos + 5))) to sCh
        If (_IsIdentifierChar(ghoFuncLib, sCh) = True) ;
            Function_Return ""
        // Walk LEFT from the "(" over the identifier that precedes it.
        Move "" to sName
        Move (iPos - 1) to i
        While (i >= 1)
            Move (Mid(sLine, 1, i)) to sCh
            If (_IsIdentifierChar(ghoFuncLib, sCh) = False) Begin
                Move 0 to i
            End
            Else Begin
                Move (sCh + sName) to sName
                Decrement i
            End
        Loop
        If (sName = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        If (Left(sName, 1) >= "0" and Left(sName, 1) <= "9") ;
            Function_Return ""
        // A keyword followed by "(Self" is not a call of that keyword: If (Self ...), Move (Self ...).
        Move (Lowercase(sName)) to sLowName
        If (sLowName = "if" or sLowName = "while" or sLowName = "until" or sLowName = "and" or sLowName = "or" ;
            or sLowName = "not" or sLowName = "move" or sLowName = "get" or sLowName = "set" or sLowName = "send" ;
            or sLowName = "function_return" or sLowName = "procedure_return" or sLowName = "indicate" or sLowName = "case") ;
            Function_Return ""
        Function_Return sName
    End_Function

    // An identifier that names an object handle by this codebase's conventions: gho* (globals),
    // ho* (locals/parameters), pho* (property accessors read into a local). Deliberately NOT o*:
    // "Move (Value(oGrid, 3)) to sV" is classic item-access of a form, not an object-method call.
    Function _IsHandleReceiverName String sIdent Returns Boolean
        String sLow
        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sIdent))) to sLow
        If (Left(sLow, 3) = "gho" and Length(sLow) > 3) ;
            Function_Return True
        If (Left(sLow, 3) = "pho" and Length(sLow) > 3) ;
            Function_Return True
        If (Left(sLow, 2) = "ho" and Length(sLow) > 2) ;
            Function_Return True
        Function_Return False
    End_Function

    /* THE 4379 HANDLE-RECEIVER CALL (2026-08-29): "Name(ghoDialog, args)" - the same legal
       object-expression form as Name(Self, args), aimed at another object. Found on a real 19.1
       workspace where SelectFolder(ghoDFSFolderSelectorDialog, 4 args) called a 2-parameter method:
       DataFlex 19.1 let the extra arguments pass unchecked, DF26's count check reports the 4379,
       and the arguments fit the near-miss sibling SelectFolder2 - the call was wrong all along.

       Reads the method NAME and the RECEIVER off the line so the findings row can DIAGNOSE:
       nothing automatic is offered for this shape (Nils 2026-08-29) - a blind Get rewrite keeps
       the wrong argument count and produces a differently broken line. Same rejections as the
       Self reader; the receiver must be a handle-conventioned identifier. PURE -> unit-tested. */
    Function ObjExprHandleCallInLine String sCodeLine String ByRef sReceiver Returns String
        String sLine sLow sCh sName sRecv sLowName
        Integer iPos i iLen iAt
        Boolean bIdent bHandle

        Move "" to sReceiver
        Get _CodeOnlyLine of ghoFuncLib sCodeLine to sLine
        Move (Length(sLine)) to iLen
        For iAt from 1 to iLen
            If (sReceiver = "" and Mid(sLine, 1, iAt) = "(") Begin
                // Read the identifier right after the "(" - the would-be receiver.
                Move "" to sRecv
                Move (iAt + 1) to i
                While (i <= iLen and Mid(sLine, 1, i) = " ")
                    Increment i
                Loop
                Move True to bIdent
                While (i <= iLen and bIdent = True)
                    Move (Mid(sLine, 1, i)) to sCh
                    Get _IsIdentifierChar of ghoFuncLib sCh to bIdent
                    If (bIdent = True) Begin
                        Move (sRecv + sCh) to sRecv
                        Increment i
                    End
                Loop
                While (i <= iLen and Mid(sLine, 1, i) = " ")
                    Increment i
                Loop
                // Receiver boundary: the next char must continue the argument list or close the call.
                If (i <= iLen and (Mid(sLine, 1, i) = "," or Mid(sLine, 1, i) = ")")) Begin
                    Get _IsHandleReceiverName sRecv to bHandle
                    If (bHandle = True) Begin
                        // Walk LEFT from the "(" over the identifier that precedes it - the method name.
                        Move "" to sName
                        Move (iAt - 1) to i
                        While (i >= 1)
                            Move (Mid(sLine, 1, i)) to sCh
                            Get _IsIdentifierChar of ghoFuncLib sCh to bIdent
                            If (bIdent = False) ;
                                Move 0 to i
                            Else Begin
                                Move (sCh + sName) to sName
                                Decrement i
                            End
                        Loop
                        If (sName <> "") Begin
                            If (Left(sName, 1) >= "0" and Left(sName, 1) <= "9") ;
                                Move "" to sName
                        End
                        If (sName <> "") Begin
                            Move (Lowercase(sName)) to sLowName
                            If (sLowName <> "if" and sLowName <> "while" and sLowName <> "until" and sLowName <> "and" ;
                                and sLowName <> "or" and sLowName <> "not" and sLowName <> "move" and sLowName <> "get" ;
                                and sLowName <> "set" and sLowName <> "send" and sLowName <> "function_return" ;
                                and sLowName <> "procedure_return" and sLowName <> "indicate" and sLowName <> "case") Begin
                                Move sRecv to sReceiver
                                Function_Return sName
                            End
                        End
                    End
                End
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return ""
    End_Function

    /* One logical line from a physical one: while the code part (comments stripped) ends with the
       ";" continuation, the following lines are appended - trailing ";" becomes a space, each
       continuation's leading indent collapses to one space. Capped at 6 physical lines. Built for
       DIAGNOSIS (argument counting on a call split across lines), not for emission - comments on
       the joined lines are dropped, which is fine for reading and would not be for rewriting. */
    Function JoinContinuedFrom String[] ByRef asLines Integer iStart0 Returns String
        tTokenizer TD
        String sJoined sCode
        Integer i iMax iUsed

        Move (SizeOfArray(asLines) - 1) to iMax
        If (iStart0 < 0 or iStart0 > iMax) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move "" to sJoined
        Move 0 to iUsed
        Move iStart0 to i
        While (i <= iMax and iUsed < 6)
            Get Tokenizer of ghoFuncLib asLines[i] to TD
            Move asLines[i] to sCode
            If (TD.sEndComment <> "") ;
                Move (Replace(TD.sEndComment, sCode, "")) to sCode
            Move (Trim(sCode)) to sCode
            Increment iUsed
            If (Right(sCode, 1) = ";") Begin
                Move (Left(sCode, (Length(sCode) - 1))) to sCode
                If (sJoined = "") ;
                    Move sCode to sJoined
                Else ;
                    Move (sJoined * sCode) to sJoined
                Increment i
            End
            Else Begin
                If (sJoined = "") ;
                    Move sCode to sJoined
                Else ;
                    Move (sJoined * sCode) to sJoined
                Move (iMax + 1) to i
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return sJoined
    End_Function

    /* How many arguments this (logical) line passes to sName AFTER the receiver - the number to
       hold against the method's declared parameter list. -1 when the call cannot be parsed off
       the line (no such call, or unbalanced). Splits at the top level only, so nested calls and
       quoted commas do not inflate the count. PURE -> unit-tested. */
    Function ObjExprCallArgCount String sJoinedLine String sName Returns Integer
        tTokenizer TD
        String sOver sLow sCallArgs sCallArgsOver sBefore
        String[] asArgs
        Integer iPos iOpen iClose iLen
        Boolean bIdentBefore

        If (Trim(sName) = "" or Trim(sJoinedLine) = "") ;
            Function_Return -1
        Get Tokenizer of ghoFuncLib sJoinedLine to TD
        Move TD.sOverstrikeLine to sOver
        Move (Lowercase(sOver)) to sLow
        Move (Pos((Lowercase(sName) + "("), sLow)) to iPos
        If (iPos = 0) ;
            Function_Return -1
        // Boundary on the left: "MySelectFolder(" must not read as "SelectFolder(".
        If (iPos > 1) Begin
            Move (Mid(sOver, 1, (iPos - 1))) to sBefore
            Get _IsIdentifierChar of ghoFuncLib sBefore to bIdentBefore
            If (bIdentBefore = True) ;
                Function_Return -1
        End
        Move (iPos + Length(sName)) to iOpen
        Get _MatchParenInOver of ghoFuncLib sOver iOpen to iClose
        If (iClose <= iOpen) ;
            Function_Return -1
        Move (Mid(sJoinedLine, (iClose - iOpen - 1), (iOpen + 1))) to sCallArgs
        Move (Mid(sOver,       (iClose - iOpen - 1), (iOpen + 1))) to sCallArgsOver
        If (Trim(sCallArgs) = "") ;
            Function_Return -1
        Get _SplitTopLevelArgs of ghoFuncLib sCallArgs sCallArgsOver to asArgs
        Function_Return (SizeOfArray(asArgs) - 1)
    End_Function

    /* HOW MANY PARAMETERS DOES THIS FILE'S OWN <sName> DECLARE, and on which line - the fact that
       separates the two causes of a 4379 (Nils 2026-09-04, LegacyApp PlanView.VW). The board told him
       "DataFlex 26 now owns the name fheadvalue", which was false in every clause: fHeadValue is
       his, declared in the same object above the call, and DataFlex owns no such name. The real
       fault was arithmetic - it declares one parameter and the call passes two, exactly the shape
       19.1 waved through - and the Get rewrite offered as the cure does NOT fix it. Probe-compiled
       under DF26: "Move (f(Self, 1, 2)) to n" is the 4379, "Get f 1 2 to n" against the same
       one-parameter f COMPILES CLEAN, because a message send does not count arguments. So the
       offer would have made the error vanish and dropped the surplus argument in silence.

       Returns -1 whenever no claim can be made, and the caller must then say nothing: the file
       declares no such Function, the declaration carries Global (there the expression form's first
       argument IS a parameter, so the arithmetic differs), the words do not pair up, or two
       declarations of the name disagree. iDeclLine comes back 1-based, 0 when there is nothing to
       point at. Reads asLines ByRef for cost only. PURE -> unit-tested. */
    Function DeclaredParamCountIn String[] ByRef asLines String sName Integer ByRef iDeclLine Returns Integer
        String[] asLocal
        String sLow sW1 sW2 sLowName sJoined
        Integer i iMax iCount iFound iFoundLine
        Boolean bInBlock

        Move 0 to iDeclLine
        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sName))) to sLowName
        If (sLowName = "") ;
            Function_Return -1
        Move -1 to iFound
        Move 0  to iFoundLine
        // A LOCAL copy before anything is handed on by reference - a ByRef parameter passed
        // onward ByRef comes back empty in a deep chain (the ChangeLegacyIndicators lesson).
        Move asLines to asLocal
        Move (SizeOfArray(asLocal) - 1) to iMax
        Move False to bInBlock
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (Lowercase(Trim(asLocal[i]))) to sLow
            /* A COMMENTED-OUT DECLARATION IS NOT A DECLARATION. "//Function fPOrderC ..." already
               fails the word test (the marker is part of word one), but a DF23 block comment does
               not, and a count read out of one would be stated at the user as fact. Tracked with a
               plain depth flag rather than the tokenizer: this walks every line of the file, and
               tokenising all of them is the 2.2-seconds-per-file shape the row loop's own fast gate
               exists to avoid. A line that opens and closes on itself is left to the word test,
               which its leading marker fails anyway. */
            If (bInBlock = True) Begin
                If (Pos("*/", sLow) > 0) ;
                    Move False to bInBlock
                Move "" to sW1
            End
            Else If (Left(sLow, 2) = "/*" and Pos("*/", sLow) = 0) Begin
                Move True to bInBlock
                Move "" to sW1
            End
            Else ;
                Get _NthWord of ghoFuncLib sLow 1 to sW1
            If (sW1 = "function") Begin
                Get _NthWord of ghoFuncLib sLow 2 to sW2
                If (sW2 = sLowName) Begin
                    Get JoinContinuedFrom (&asLocal) i to sJoined
                    Get _DeclParamCount sJoined to iCount
                    If (iCount < 0) ;
                        Function_Return -1
                    If (iFound >= 0 and iCount <> iFound) ;
                        Function_Return -1
                    If (iFound < 0) Begin
                        Move iCount    to iFound
                        Move (i + 1)   to iFoundLine
                    End
                End
            End
        Loop
        Move iFoundLine to iDeclLine
        Function_Return iFound
    End_Function

    /* The parameter count of ONE joined declaration line - the type/name pairs between the method
       name and Returns, with ByRef read as a modifier rather than a word of its own, so
       "String[] ByRef asX" is one parameter. -1 where no claim can be made: a Global function, or
       an odd word count (a malformed or unexpected declaration). PURE. */
    Function _DeclParamCount String sJoinedDecl Returns Integer
        String sWord sLow
        Integer iN iWords iPairs
        Boolean bDone

        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sJoinedDecl))) to sLow
        Move 0 to iWords
        Move 3 to iN
        Move False to bDone
        While (bDone = False and iN < 60)
            Get _NthWord of ghoFuncLib sLow iN to sWord
            If (sWord = "" or sWord = "returns") ;
                Move True to bDone
            Else If (sWord = "global") ;
                Function_Return -1
            Else Begin
                If (sWord <> "byref") ;
                    Increment iWords
                Increment iN
            End
        Loop
        If (Mod(iWords, 2) <> 0) ;
            Function_Return -1
        Move (iWords / 2) to iPairs
        Function_Return iPairs
    End_Function

    /* DOES THIS FUNCTION RETURN TEXT? The fact that separates an ALIGN from a RENAME on a 4544
       whose declarations are all the workspace's own (Nils 2026-09-04, LegacyApp FCOUNT). The align
       offer compared declared types only - "most say Integer, Unit.sl line 28 says String" - and
       retyped a function whose body is "Move 'Fixed amount' to sdescr ... Function_Return sdescr":
       it compiled, and the selection list now shows 0 where the description was. Two unrelated
       functions sharing a name is a rename, and the tool could have read that off the body.

       Walks the body from the Function line at iDeclLine (1-based) to the FIRST End_Function OR
       End_Procedure - either ender closes either method, and a scanner keyed on one runs to the
       next function's body and reports ITS evidence. Text evidence is a Function_Return of a quoted
       literal that is not merely a number, or of a bare identifier this body declares String.
       Returns the evidence phrased for a dialog ("Function_Return "Fixed amount"" /
       "Function_Return sdescr, a String local"), "" when nothing text-like is returned, when the
       line is not a Function, or when the body cannot be bounded. Block comments are skipped as in
       DeclaredParamCountIn. Reads asLines ByRef for cost only. PURE -> unit-tested. */
    Function FunctionReturnsTextIn String[] ByRef asLines Integer iDeclLine Returns String
        String[] asLocal asStrLocals
        String sLow sOrig sW1 sExpr sExprLc sInner sCh
        Integer i iMax iN iLen
        Boolean bInBlock bNumeric bDone

        Move asLines to asLocal          // a ByRef parameter passed onward ByRef arrives empty
        Move (SizeOfArray(asLocal)) to iMax
        If (iDeclLine < 1 or iDeclLine > iMax) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Get _NthWord of ghoFuncLib (Lowercase(Trim(asLocal[iDeclLine - 1]))) 1 to sW1
        If (sW1 <> "function") ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move False to bInBlock
        Move False to bDone
        Move iDeclLine to i              // 0-based index of the line AFTER the declaration
        While (i < iMax and bDone = False)
            Move (Trim(asLocal[i])) to sOrig
            Move (Lowercase(sOrig)) to sLow
            If (bInBlock = True) Begin
                If (Pos("*/", sLow) > 0) ;
                    Move False to bInBlock
                Move "" to sW1
            End
            Else If (Left(sLow, 2) = "/*" and Pos("*/", sLow) = 0) Begin
                Move True to bInBlock
                Move "" to sW1
            End
            Else ;
                Get _NthWord of ghoFuncLib sLow 1 to sW1
            If (sW1 = "end_function" or sW1 = "end_procedure") ;
                Move True to bDone
            Else If (sW1 = "string") Begin
                // "String sdescr sName" - every word after the type is a local of that type.
                Move 2 to iN
                Get _NthWord of ghoFuncLib sLow iN to sCh
                While (sCh <> "" and iN < 40)
                    Move sCh to asStrLocals[SizeOfArray(asStrLocals)]
                    Increment iN
                    Get _NthWord of ghoFuncLib sLow iN to sCh
                Loop
            End
            Else If (sW1 = "function_return") Begin
                Move (Trim(Right(sOrig, (Length(sOrig) - 15)))) to sExpr
                If (Pos("//", sExpr) > 0) ;
                    Move (Trim(Left(sExpr, (Pos("//", sExpr) - 1)))) to sExpr
                Move (Lowercase(sExpr)) to sExprLc
                Move (Length(sExpr)) to iLen
                If (iLen >= 2 and (Left(sExpr, 1) = '"' or Left(sExpr, 1) = "'") and Right(sExpr, 1) = Left(sExpr, 1)) Begin
                    // A quoted literal: text unless it merely spells a number ("12", "-3.5").
                    Move (Mid(sExpr, (iLen - 2), 2)) to sInner
                    Move (Trim(sInner) <> "") to bNumeric
                    For iN from 1 to (Length(sInner))
                        Move (Mid(sInner, 1, iN)) to sCh
                        If (not(sCh >= "0" and sCh <= "9") and sCh <> "." and sCh <> "-" and sCh <> "+") ;
                            Move False to bNumeric
                    Loop
                    If (bNumeric = False and Trim(sInner) <> "") ;
                        Function_Return ("Function_Return " + sExpr)
                End
                Else If (sExprLc <> "" and Pos(" ", sExprLc) = 0 and Pos("(", sExprLc) = 0) Begin
                    If (SearchArray(sExprLc, asStrLocals) <> -1) ;
                        Function_Return ("Function_Return " + sExpr + ", a String local")
                End
            End
            Increment i
        Loop
        Function_Return ""
    End_Function

    /* Part 2: the near-miss wrapper the call was meant for. Scans the lines for a Function
       declaration whose name STARTS WITH sName and continues (SQLExecute -> SQLExecuteX) - the
       shape the 4546 hint already tells the user to look for - and returns that declared name
       as spelled, "" when there is none. First match wins; the caller pre-fills the rename
       prompt with it and the user can still type another. PURE -> unit-tested. */
    Function NearMissDeclaredIn String[] ByRef asLines String sName Returns String
        String sLow sW1 sW2 sLowName
        Integer i iMax

        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sName))) to sLowName
        If (sLowName = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move (SizeOfArray(asLines) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (Lowercase(asLines[i])) to sLow
            Get _NthWord of ghoFuncLib sLow 1 to sW1
            If (sW1 = "function") Begin
                Get _NthWord of ghoFuncLib sLow 2 to sW2
                If (Length(sW2) > Length(sLowName) and Left(sW2, Length(sLowName)) = sLowName) Begin
                    // return it as SPELLED in the source
                    Get _NthWord of ghoFuncLib asLines[i] 2 to sW2
                    Function_Return sW2
                End
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return ""
    End_Function

    /* The name UseLineForPackage matches a Use line on (2026-09-02, lifted out of it so the board's
       Use-site index keys on the very same string): lowercased, the FINAL extension only stripped -
       "RefactorConstants.h.pkg" -> "refactorconstants.h", "MapiDllCalls" stays "mapidllcalls". */
    Function UsePackageBase String sWord Returns String
        Integer iDot

        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sWord))) to sWord
        Move (RightPos(".", sWord)) to iDot
        If (iDot > 1) ;
            Move (Left(sWord, (iDot - 1))) to sWord
        Function_Return sWord
    End_Function

    /* The workspace's own 'Use <package>' line for a 4536 package self-deprecation (2026-08-29).
       The compiler reports "MapiDllCalls.pkg is obsolete" AT THE INSTALL PACKAGE'S OWN LINE 1 -
       a file that is not the user's to change - so the board re-points the finding at the one
       place the user CAN act: the Use statement that pulls the package in. This finds that line.

       1-based line of the FIRST 'Use <sPkgFile>' in asLines, 0 when there is none. The package is
       matched by BASE NAME (final extension stripped, case-insensitive), because 'Use MapiDllCalls'
       and 'Use MapiDllCalls.pkg' both bind the same package. Comment-safe: '// Use X' lines and
       lines inside block comments are skipped - a commented-out Use is exactly the state the
       finding's fix leaves behind, and re-pointing at it would resurrect finished work.
       PURE -> unit-tested (oModAnalyzer-Tests). */
    Function UseLineForPackage String[] ByRef asLines String sPkgFile Returns Integer
        String sWant sTrim sW1 sW2
        Integer i iMax
        Boolean bInBlock

        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sPkgFile))) to sWant
        // Leaf only, should a full path arrive.
        While (sWant contains "\")
            Move (Mid(sWant, Length(sWant), (Pos("\", sWant) + 1))) to sWant
        Loop
        // Base name: the FINAL extension only comes off - UsePackageBase, shared with the board's index.
        Get UsePackageBase sWant to sWant
        If (sWant = "") ;
            Function_Return 0
        Move False to bInBlock
        Move (SizeOfArray(asLines) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (Trim(asLines[i])) to sTrim
            If (bInBlock = True) Begin
                If (sTrim contains "*/") ;
                    Move False to bInBlock
            End
            Else Begin
                If (Left(sTrim, 2) = "/*" and (not(sTrim contains "*/"))) ;
                    Move True to bInBlock
                Else If (Left(sTrim, 2) <> "//") Begin
                    Get _NthWord of ghoFuncLib (Lowercase(sTrim)) 1 to sW1
                    If (sW1 = "use") Begin
                        Get _NthWord of ghoFuncLib (Lowercase(sTrim)) 2 to sW2
                        Get UsePackageBase sW2 to sW2
                        If (sW2 = sWant) ;
                            Function_Return (i + 1)
                    End
                End
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return 0
    End_Function

    /* Every real Use in a file HEAD, in order (2026-09-02): the base names UseLineForPackage would
       match (UsePackageBase) and their 1-based lines, comments skipped by the same rules. The board's
       Use-site index asks this ONCE per file where it used to run UseLineForPackage once per 4536
       package per file; the per-character word walk in _NthWord is the cost that made that sweep
       take 124 s on a 1976-file workspace, so here only a line that CAN be a Use pays it - one that
       starts with "use" after Trim, which takes a tab indent off as well as spaces. Both lists come back
       emptied first, so a file with no Use leaves nothing behind from the previous one. */
    Procedure UseLinesInHead String[] ByRef asLines String[] ByRef asLeafs Integer[] ByRef aiLines
        String sTrim sLow sW1 sW2
        Integer i iMax
        Boolean bInBlock

        Move (ResizeArray(asLeafs, 0)) to asLeafs
        Move (ResizeArray(aiLines, 0)) to aiLines
        Move False to bInBlock
        Move (SizeOfArray(asLines) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (Trim(asLines[i])) to sTrim
            If (bInBlock = True) Begin
                If (sTrim contains "*/") ;
                    Move False to bInBlock
            End
            Else Begin
                If (Left(sTrim, 2) = "/*" and (not(sTrim contains "*/"))) ;
                    Move True to bInBlock
                Else If (Lowercase(Left(sTrim, 3)) = "use") Begin
                    Move (Lowercase(sTrim)) to sLow
                    Get _NthWord of ghoFuncLib sLow 1 to sW1
                    If (sW1 = "use") Begin
                        Get _NthWord of ghoFuncLib sLow 2 to sW2
                        Get UsePackageBase sW2 to sW2
                        If (sW2 <> "") Begin
                            Move sW2 to asLeafs[SizeOfArray(asLeafs)]
                            Move (i + 1) to aiLines[SizeOfArray(aiLines)]
                        End
                    End
                End
            End
        Loop
    End_Procedure

    /* The class a source line declares AND the class it is built on (2026-10-04): "Class <Name> is a
       <Super>" - "an" too, any case, tabs and runs of blanks folded - gives <Name>, with <Super> in
       sSuper; any other line gives "" and "". A comment or ';' glued to <Super> is not part of it.
       ClassDeclaredOnLine's sibling, for PackageKeptBySubclass below. */
    Function ClassAndSuperOnLine String sLine String ByRef sSuper Returns String
        String[] asSplit asWords
        String   sLC
        Integer  i iPos

        Move "" to sSuper
        Move (Trim(Replaces(Character(9), sLine, " "))) to sLC
        If (Length(sLC) <= 6 or Left(Lowercase(sLC), 6) <> "class ") ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move (StrSplitToArray(sLC, " ")) to asSplit
        For i from 0 to (SizeOfArray(asSplit) - 1)
            If (asSplit[i] <> "") ;
                Move asSplit[i] to asWords[-1]
        Loop
        If (SizeOfArray(asWords) < 5) ;
            Function_Return ""
        If (Lowercase(asWords[2]) <> "is" or (Lowercase(asWords[3]) <> "a" and Lowercase(asWords[3]) <> "an")) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Move asWords[4] to sSuper
        Move (Pos("/", sSuper)) to iPos
        If (iPos > 0) ;
            Move (Left(sSuper, (iPos - 1))) to sSuper
        Move (Pos(";", sSuper)) to iPos
        If (iPos > 0) ;
            Move (Left(sSuper, (iPos - 1))) to sSuper
        If (sSuper = "") ;
            Function_Return ""
        Function_Return asWords[1]
    End_Function

    /* IS AN OBSOLETE PACKAGE'S USE THERE FOR A SUBCLASS? (Nils 2026-10-04, on a Review row: "Its kind
       of pointless to list this line: Use Dftable.pkg from the MyDbGrid.pkg, which is a sub-class of
       DbGrid.") True when sUseFile - the workspace file a W 4536 row points at - declares "Class
       <sClass> is a <sSuper>" and sPkgFile, the obsolete install package, declares <sSuper>. There is
       nothing to check then: the class is built on the package, and the Use goes only when the class is
       ported to something else - hand work, not a judgement to dismiss. The workspace file is read
       first: most Use sites declare no class, and then the package is never read. Its comments are
       skipped by UseLinesInHead's rules, so a class left in a block comment does not count. iClassLine is
       the class line's 1-based number, for SavedPlanHasRowAt. sClass and sSuper come back "" (and iClassLine
       0) with False. */
    Function PackageKeptBySubclass String sPkgFile String sUseFile String ByRef sClass String ByRef sSuper Integer ByRef iClassLine Returns Boolean
        String[]  asLines asNames asSupers asDeclared
        String    sTrim sName sSup
        Integer[] aiLines
        Integer   i iMax
        Boolean   bInBlock

        Move "" to sClass
        Move "" to sSuper
        Move 0  to iClassLine
        If (Trim(sPkgFile) = "" or Trim(sUseFile) = "") ;
            Function_Return False
        Move (rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArray(sUseFile)) to asLines
        Move False to bInBlock
        Move (SizeOfArray(asLines) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (Trim(asLines[i])) to sTrim
            If (bInBlock = True) Begin
                If (sTrim contains "*/") ;
                    Move False to bInBlock
            End
            Else Begin
                If (Left(sTrim, 2) = "/*" and (not(sTrim contains "*/"))) ;
                    Move True to bInBlock
                Else If (Lowercase(Left(sTrim, 5)) = "class") Begin
                    Get ClassAndSuperOnLine sTrim (&sSup) to sName
                    If (sName <> "") Begin
                        Move sName   to asNames[-1]
                        Move sSup    to asSupers[-1]
                        Move (i + 1) to aiLines[-1]
                    End
                End
            End
        Loop
        If (SizeOfArray(asNames) = 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        // The package's own classes, every declaration line of it.
        Move (rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArray(sPkgFile)) to asLines
        Move (SizeOfArray(asLines) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Get ClassDeclaredOnLine asLines[i] to sName
            If (sName <> "") ;
                Move (Lowercase(sName)) to asDeclared[-1]
        Loop
        Move (SizeOfArray(asNames) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (SearchArray((Lowercase(asSupers[i])), asDeclared) <> -1) Begin
                Move asNames[i]  to sClass
                Move asSupers[i] to sSuper
                Move aiLines[i]  to iClassLine
                Function_Return True
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return False
    End_Function

    /* The hand band's hint for a PackageKeptBySubclass row (2026-10-04): why the Use is there, then the
       way on. The three grid families the grid subclass converter ports - the same three as
       cGridSubclassPorter._SetFamily - name it, with its parameter; a List subclass and any other class
       are ported by hand. sPkgLeaf and sUseLeaf are file names, sClass and sSuper as the source spells
       them. */
    Function SubclassKeptPackageHint String sPkgLeaf String sUseLeaf String sClass String sSuper Returns String
        String sWhy sTarget sSuperLc

        Move (sUseLeaf + " needs " + sPkgLeaf + " because " + sClass + " is a " + sSuper + ".") to sWhy
        Move (Lowercase(sSuper)) to sSuperLc
        Move "" to sTarget
        If (sSuperLc = "dbgrid") ;
            Move "cDbCJGrid" to sTarget
        Else If (sSuperLc = "dblist") ;
            Move "cDbCJGridPromptList" to sTarget
        Else If (sSuperLc = "grid") ;
            Move "cCJGrid" to sTarget
        If (sTarget <> "") ;
            Function_Return (sWhy + " The Use goes when " + sClass + " is ported to " + sTarget + ". ConvertGridSubclassToCodeJock does that with the parameter '" + sClass + "': it converts the class and every object of it, or says in its run notes why it cannot.")
        If (sSuperLc = "list") ;
            Function_Return (sWhy + " The Use goes when " + sClass + " is ported by hand. No converter ports a List subclass yet.")
        Function_Return (sWhy + " The Use goes when " + sClass + " is ported by hand to a class that is not obsolete.")
    End_Function

    /* Does the board already carry a row on line iLine of sFile? (2026-10-04) The dashboard asks it for a
       W 4536 row PackageKeptBySubclass answered: when the subclass's own class line has a row - the Deep
       Scan's "dbGrid subclass" (DS 9198) and its kin - that row already sends the user to the port, and the
       port takes the Use with it, so the package row folds into it (Nils, the Check after: "the MyDbGrid.pkg
       and MyDbList.pkg are still listed two times"). Files compare case-folded. (aRows ByRef = copy-avoidance
       only, never modified.) */
    Function SavedPlanHasRowAt tSavedPlanRow[] ByRef aRows String sFile Integer iLine Returns Boolean
        String  sFileLc
        Integer i iLast

        If (iLine < 1) ;
            Function_Return False
        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sFile))) to sFileLc
        Move (SizeOfArray(aRows) - 1) to iLast
        For i from 0 to iLast
            If (aRows[i].iLine = iLine) Begin
                If (Lowercase(Trim(aRows[i].sFile)) = sFileLc) ;
                    Function_Return True
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return False
    End_Function

    /* The PACKAGE a pool-checkout file belongs to, as a display name (2026-08-29) - the outside
       band aggregates its read-only findings per package, and "VDFQuery20" is the unit the user
       thinks (and fixes upstream) in, not 37 file paths. Recognises the two df-cli pool shapes
       (the same two rfPackageManagedPathNote knows):
         <workspace>\DfPkg\<Owner>_<Name>-<commit>\...
         ...\DataFlex\Packages\Cache\<Repo>_<Name>-<version>\...
       and returns <Name>: the owner prefix is cut at the FIRST underscore (GitHub owner names
       cannot contain one), and the trailing -<commit>/-<version> is cut only when the tail after
       the LAST dash is all hex (7+ chars) or a dotted number - a dash that is part of the name
       ("Library-VDFQuery20") survives. "" for a path in neither pool - the caller then groups the
       read-only file by itself. Display only: the FOLDER name is df-cli's and is never rewritten.
       PURE -> unit-tested (oModAnalyzer-Tests). The body is the global PoolPackageDisplayNameOf
       (cShadowedPackageFiles.pkg) since 2026-11-12, so the overriding-copies rows name a package
       exactly as this band does. */
    Function PoolPackageDisplayName String sFile Returns String
        Function_Return (PoolPackageDisplayNameOf(sFile))
    End_Function

    /* WHICH FLOOD A DEEP SCAN HIT BELONGS TO (2026-10-03): its DS number - negated when the scan's own
       verdict on THIS hit dropped the rule's converter (an Explain verdict with no function, as for an
       object of the workspace's own grid subclass, which the converters leave as it is). Those hits
       used to fold into the rule's summary row and take its converter with them: one workspace's 1,509
       MyDbGrid objects became "dbGrid class - the fix runs across the whole selection", a plan row that
       could convert none of them. Keyed apart, they make their own summary row, and that one is Explain.
       A message with no verdict (eModUnknown) keeps the plain number, as before. The grid's row loop asks
       the same question, so the tally and the rows it skips cannot disagree. */
    Function DSFloodKey tCompileError Msg Returns Integer
        If (Msg.eScanCategory = eModExplain and Trim(Msg.sScanFn) = "") ;
            Function_Return (0 - Msg.iErrorNo)
        Function_Return Msg.iErrorNo
    End_Function

    /* THE SCAN-RULE FLOOD TALLY (2026-08-16), lifted out of the analyzer grid so it can be tested:
       the grid's flood collapse (one summary row per rule at or above the threshold) had its
       distinct-file bookkeeping inside an FT-only file, and the two defects it shipped with - one
       file per Check, then a 4509 on the jagged slot - were both found by Nils on live runs.

       Walks the messages ONCE. Only Deep Scan rows (bDeepScan) count; the compiler lane never
       floods here. Messages arrive file-grouped, so distinct files are counted on file
       TRANSITIONS and the SearchArray only guards a rule re-appearing in a file it already left
       (revisits do not double-count). Returns one tally per rule that fired at least iThreshold
       times - its count, distinct-file count, the FIRST finding (the summary row's file + line),
       the symbol, and every distinct file in first-seen order. Rules under the threshold are not
       returned at all (they keep every row). PURE -> unit-tested (oModAnalyzer-Tests).
       A rule's hits flood by DSFloodKey, not by the bare number: the hits whose own verdict dropped the
       rule's converter make up a flood of their own, under the NEGATIVE number (2026-10-03). */
    Function TallyScanFloods tCompileError[] ByRef aMsgs Integer iThreshold Returns tDSFloodTally[]
        tDSFloodTally[] aTally aOut
        Integer[] aiNo             // the flood keys seen so far, index-parallel with aTally (native SearchArray per message)
        String[] asLastFile
        String[] asNone
        Integer i iMax iAt iN iKey

        Move (SizeOfArray(aMsgs) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (aMsgs[i].bDeepScan = True) Begin
                Get DSFloodKey aMsgs[i] to iKey
                Move (SearchArray(iKey, aiNo)) to iAt
                If (iAt = -1) Begin
                    Move (SizeOfArray(aiNo)) to iAt
                    Move iKey               to aiNo[iAt]
                    Move iKey               to aTally[iAt].iDSNumber
                    Move 0                  to aTally[iAt].iCount
                    Move 0                  to aTally[iAt].iFiles
                    Move aMsgs[i].sFileName to aTally[iAt].sFirstFile
                    Move aMsgs[i].iLine     to aTally[iAt].iFirstLine
                    Move aMsgs[i].sErrorTxt to aTally[iAt].sSymbol
                    Move aMsgs[i].sScanHint to aTally[iAt].sFirstHint
                    Move asNone             to aTally[iAt].asFiles
                    Move ""                 to asLastFile[iAt]
                End
                Move (aTally[iAt].iCount + 1) to aTally[iAt].iCount
                If (Lowercase(aMsgs[i].sFileName) <> asLastFile[iAt]) Begin
                    Move (Lowercase(aMsgs[i].sFileName)) to asLastFile[iAt]
                    If (SearchArray(aMsgs[i].sFileName, aTally[iAt].asFiles) = -1) Begin
                        Move aMsgs[i].sFileName to aTally[iAt].asFiles[SizeOfArray(aTally[iAt].asFiles)]
                        Move (aTally[iAt].iFiles + 1) to aTally[iAt].iFiles
                    End
                End
            End
        Loop
        Move 0 to iN
        For i from 0 to (SizeOfArray(aTally) - 1)
            If (aTally[i].iCount >= iThreshold) Begin
                Move aTally[i] to aOut[iN]
                Increment iN
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return aOut
    End_Function

    /* THE COLLAPSED SCAN RULES REACH EVERY FILE THEY FIRED IN (2026-08-16).

       A rule at or above CI_DSFloodThreshold is ONE summary row in the grid (ModAnalyzerFT), and the
       plan is built from the rows - so on the first live run its single sFile was all the plan got:
       a rule with 22,000 findings advanced by ONE FILE per Check (Nils, WinServ: "never shows more
       than 12 at a time ... the Refactoring plan should apply them to *all* files involved"). The
       row stays one row; tSavedPlanFlood carries the files it stands for, and this folds them in.

       A flood contributes only when its summary row DID: the row's function is already in the plan
       (PlanFromSavedRows put it there from the row itself, so the row was Auto-fix / Review with a
       function) AND the row is not dismissed - the ignore key is the row's own file + "DS <n>",
       exactly what the grid dismisses it under. Files are de-duped against the plan. Called by both
       plan paths (live rows and the restored saved plan), so a restored plan reaches as far as a
       live one. PURE -> unit-tested (oModAnalyzer-Tests). */
    Procedure ExpandFloodFilesIntoPlan tSavedPlanFlood[] aFloods tModIgnore[] aIgnored tModRefactorPlan ByRef Plan
        Integer i j iMax jMax
        String sFn sFile
        Boolean bIgnored

        Move (SizeOfArray(aFloods) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (Trim(aFloods[i].sFn)) to sFn
            If (sFn <> "" and SearchArray(sFn, Plan.asFunctions) <> -1) Begin
                Get IsRowIgnored aFloods[i].sFirstFile ("DS " + String(aFloods[i].iDSNumber)) (&aIgnored) to bIgnored
                If (bIgnored = False) Begin
                    Move (SizeOfArray(aFloods[i].asFiles) - 1) to jMax
                    For j from 0 to jMax
                        Move (Trim(aFloods[i].asFiles[j])) to sFile
                        If (sFile <> "" and SearchArray(sFile, Plan.asFiles) = -1) ;
                            Move sFile to Plan.asFiles[SizeOfArray(Plan.asFiles)]
                    Loop
                End
            End
        Loop
    End_Procedure

    // (Re)seed the ModAnalyzer table from the canonical recipe set for the CURRENT target version -
    // on EVERY call, NOT only when empty: the table is a per-target CACHE, so it must always mirror
    // the active target (a previous target's rows carry the same recipe-version and can't be told
    // apart). STEP 1 picks the canonical set - the per-version JSON when present + new enough
    // (iVersion >= CI_ModAnalyzerRecipeVersion), else the built-ins + a fresh install-package scan,
    // then writes that JSON. STEP 2 wipes the table + re-seeds from that set. The table is a cache,
    // not a hand-edit surface - edit the JSON instead. Uses GlobalAutoCreateNewID.
    Procedure RefreshRecipeTable
        tModAnalyzer[] aR aJson aScan
        Integer i iSize iID iJsonVer iCanonicalVer iScan
        Boolean bHaveJson bScanStale

        // STEP 1 - decide the canonical recipe set + version. The editable JSON in the Data
        // folder wins when its version is >= the built-in CI_ModAnalyzerRecipeVersion (so a user
        // can hand-edit recipes) AND the install packages haven't changed under it; otherwise the
        // (The JSON holds compiler + install rows only; the Deep Scan rows are re-derived below.)
        // built-in defaults win and we re-walk + (re)write the JSON so it always exists and tracks
        // both a code bump AND an install change.
        Get BuildDefaultRecipes to aR
        Move CI_ModAnalyzerRecipeVersion to iCanonicalVer
        Get ReadRecipesFromJson (&aJson) (&iJsonVer) to bHaveJson
        // Is the raw install scan stale (DfVersion-ObsoleteScan-<ver>.json missing, or a package newer than its
        // watermark - an in-place DataFlex update)? If so we must re-walk + re-merge even when the
        // merged JSON looks current - else the install's NEW obsolete-warning defs would never reach
        // the recipe table, and a deleted DfVersion-ObsoleteScan-<ver>.json would never be rebuilt. The merged
        // JSON carries no watermark (it's tracked, shared seed data - no machine-specific stamps); the
        // gitignored raw scan owns the watermark, so we ask it. Cheap: one probe per RefreshRecipeTable.
        Get InstallScanIsStale of ghoFuncLib to bScanStale
        If (bHaveJson and (iJsonVer >= CI_ModAnalyzerRecipeVersion) and (bScanStale = False)) Begin
            Move aJson    to aR
            Move iJsonVer to iCanonicalVer
            // The recipe set is current and the recipe scan is fresh, so the shared package walk did
            // NOT run here - a missing/stale DfVersion-TypeDefs-<ver>.json would otherwise wait for the next
            // Type->Struct report. Ensure it now too (walks only if IT is stale, piggybacking the recipe
            // scan if that is somehow also stale), so deleting EITHER install cache is healed at Analyze
            // time. Recipes untouched here (bForRecipes = False); silent (no progress bar) like the scan.
            // GlobalFuncNames is NOT proactively forced (bForGlobalFuncs = False) - it is only needed by
            // FunctionReturnMismatchReport, and the piggyback still refreshes it whenever a walk runs here anyway.
            Send EnsureInstallCaches of ghoFuncLib False True False False
        End
        Else Begin
            // Built-in is canonical (no JSON, a code version bump, or the install changed): append the
            // live install's obsolete-warning catalog (Explain) AFTER the built-ins so function-backed
            // recipes keep first-match precedence, then persist the merged set to the JSON. The catalog
            // comes from the SHARED install-Pkg walk (ghoFuncLib.InstallScanRecipes), itself cached in
            // DfVersion-ObsoleteScan-<ver>.json - so a recipe-version bump re-merges here WITHOUT re-walking the
            // packages (the walk runs only when that raw-scan cache is stale, which InstallScanIsStale
            // detected above; InstallScanRecipes then rebuilds it with a fresh watermark).
            Get InstallScanRecipes of ghoFuncLib to aScan
            Move (SizeOfArray(aScan)) to iScan
            If (iScan = 0 and bHaveJson and (iJsonVer >= CI_ModAnalyzerRecipeVersion)) Begin
                // The re-walk came back empty (only the raw cache was stale and the install Pkg folder
                // is momentarily unreachable) but the merged JSON is otherwise current: KEEP it rather
                // than drop its install recipes down to built-ins only. The raw cache stays stale, so
                // this self-heals on the next run once the install reappears. (Scoped to a CURRENT-version
                // JSON, so a real recipe-version bump - iJsonVer < CI - still rebuilds from the built-ins.)
                Move aJson    to aR
                Move iJsonVer to iCanonicalVer
            End
            Else Begin
                Move (iScan - 1) to iScan
                For i from 0 to iScan
                    Move aScan[i] to aR[SizeOfArray(aR)]
                Loop
                Send WriteRecipesToJson aR iCanonicalVer
            End
        End

        // Whichever set won, its Deep Scan rows are a snapshot at best (an older on-disk JSON still
        // carries them; the writer no longer emits them) - replace them with the live catalog, so a
        // DS row's category and converter can only ever come from the rules (v152).
        Get _WithLiveDeepScanRecipes aR to aR

        // STEP 2 - (re)seed the table from the canonical set on EVERY load. The table is a per-target
        // CACHE of the canonical set, which is now version-keyed by the selected compiler, so it must
        // always mirror the CURRENT target. A skip-if-already-seeded check can't tell whether the
        // existing rows belong to a PREVIOUS target (they carry the same recipe-version), so it would
        // serve a stale catalog after a target switch. Re-seeding is cheap (~150 rows) and the
        // expensive scan already ran (or was cached) in STEP 1, so this just rewrites the table.
        // This whole reseed runs INSIDE the (synchronous) plan build / analyze - a fresh cModAnalyzer
        // is created per StartAutoSelect, so it re-seeds EVERY time - and STEP 1 above can be heavy on
        // the first run after a recipe-version bump (rewrites the JSON) or an install/target change.
        // Pump the FT marquee via phoUIYieldTarget so it keeps animating instead of freezing at 0s
        // until the plan dialog appears (Nils 2026-07-09 - the v49->v50 Gosub-recipe bump surfaced
        // this). Guarded so a console/CI run (no yield target) is unaffected; the pump sits at the TOP
        // of each iteration, right before GlobalAutoCreateNewID re-establishes the buffer, so a pumped
        // handler can never see a half-set ModAnalyzer row. One beat before the wipe covers STEP 1.
        If (phoUIYieldTarget(Self) <> 0) ;
            Send DoYieldToUI of (phoUIYieldTarget(Self))
        // Wipe any junk/stale/previous-target rows first, then (re)seed from the canonical set (aR).
        Clear ModAnalyzer
        Repeat
            Find Gt ModAnalyzer by 1
            If (Found) Begin
                Delete ModAnalyzer
                Clear ModAnalyzer
            End
        Until (not(Found))
        Move (SizeOfArray(aR) -1) to iSize
        For i from 0 to iSize
            If (phoUIYieldTarget(Self) <> 0 and (Mod(i, 40) = 0)) ;
                Send DoYieldToUI of (phoUIYieldTarget(Self))
            // GlobalAutoCreateNewID clears the buffer + sets ONLY the ID field, so call it
            // FIRST, then fill the data fields, then save - else the row saves blank.
            Get GlobalAutoCreateNewID File_Field ModAnalyzer.ID False to iID
            Move aR[i].iMsgNumber to ModAnalyzer.MsgNumber
            Move (If(aR[i].bIsWarning, 1, 0)) to ModAnalyzer.IsWarning
            Move aR[i].sMatchText to ModAnalyzer.MatchText
            Move aR[i].sRefactorFn to ModAnalyzer.RefactorFn
            Move aR[i].eCategory to ModAnalyzer.Category
            Move aR[i].sHint to ModAnalyzer.Hint
            Move iCanonicalVer to ModAnalyzer.Version
            SaveRecord ModAnalyzer
        Loop
    End_Procedure

    /* LEARN WARNING-vs-ERROR FROM WHAT THE COMPILER ACTUALLY SAID.

       tModAnalyzer.bIsWarning had two sources and neither was authoritative: BuildDefaultRecipes
       hand-classifies each number, and the install-Pkg harvest hardcodes True for everything it
       finds - so an ERROR-numbered message picked up from Pkg source was labelled a warning
       (cRefactorFuncLib, _ObsoleteRecipesFromLines). The install ships no message-severity catalogue
       to read instead (checked Lib and Bin, 2026-08-02), so the compiler's own output is the only
       authority there is - and the analyzer already has it: rfPrnDiagnosticKind read "WARNING:" or
       "ERROR:" off the line and stored it in tCompileError.bError.

       So the table stops guessing and starts remembering. Every analyze, each message number seen is
       compared with the recipes carrying that number; a disagreement rewrites the recipe - in memory
       AND in the ModAnalyzer table, so it survives the session.

       SEVERITY IS PER NUMBER, not per recipe: several recipes share a message number and are told
       apart by sMatchText, but the compiler classifies the NUMBER. All of them are updated together.

       DEEP SCAN ROWS ARE EXCLUDED. Their iErrorNo is a DS pseudo-number (9001+) and their bError is
       whatever the synthetic row was built with - it is not a compiler verdict, and letting it write
       here would teach the table nonsense about numbers the compiler never issues.

       Writes only on a real disagreement, so this converges: the first compile that shows 4539 as an
       ERROR fixes it, and every later one costs a comparison and no DB write at all. */
    /* The DECISION half, PURE: no table, no properties - hand it messages and recipes, get back the
       corrected recipes and a count. Split out so it is unit-testable, because the fixture for this
       class is deliberately database-free (it classifies against BuildDefaultRecipes, not the
       ModAnalyzer table) and the storing half below cannot run there at all. */
    Function _LearnedSeverities tCompileError[] ByRef aMsgs tModAnalyzer[] ByRef aRIn Integer ByRef iChanged Returns tModAnalyzer[]
        tModAnalyzer[] aR
        Integer[] aiSeen
        Boolean[] abWarn
        Integer i iMax jMax iAt

        Move aRIn to aR
        Move 0 to iChanged
        Move (SizeOfArray(aMsgs) - 1) to iMax
        If (iMax < 0) ;
            Function_Return aR
        // One observed severity per message NUMBER. A number cannot be both, so first sighting wins
        // and the rest are confirmations - which also makes this a single pass over the messages.
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (aMsgs[i].bDeepScan = False and aMsgs[i].iErrorNo > 0) Begin
                Move (SearchArray(aMsgs[i].iErrorNo, aiSeen)) to iAt
                If (iAt = -1) Begin
                    Move aMsgs[i].iErrorNo to aiSeen[SizeOfArray(aiSeen)]
                    Move (aMsgs[i].bError = False) to abWarn[SizeOfArray(abWarn)]
                End
            End
        Loop
        Move (SizeOfArray(aiSeen) - 1) to jMax
        If (jMax < 0) ;
            Function_Return aR

        Move (SizeOfArray(aR) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (SearchArray(aR[i].iMsgNumber, aiSeen)) to iAt
            If (iAt <> -1) Begin
                If (aR[i].bIsWarning <> abWarn[iAt]) Begin
                    Move abWarn[iAt] to aR[i].bIsWarning
                    Increment iChanged
                End
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return aR
    End_Function

    /* The STORING half: ask the pure decision above, then persist it. Every analyze, so it is
       written to cost nothing when there is nothing to learn - no DB touch unless a severity
       actually disagreed. */
    Function LearnSeveritiesFromMessages tCompileError[] ByRef aMsgs Returns Integer
        tModAnalyzer[] aR
        Integer[] aiSeen
        Boolean[] abWarn
        Integer i iMax iAt iChanged
        Boolean bWarn

        Get paRecipes to aR
        Get _LearnedSeverities (&aMsgs) (&aR) (&iChanged) to aR
        If (iChanged = 0) ;
            Function_Return 0
        Set paRecipes to aR
        // Rebuild the observed set for the table pass - cheap, and it keeps the pure half pure.
        Move (SizeOfArray(aMsgs) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (aMsgs[i].bDeepScan = False and aMsgs[i].iErrorNo > 0) Begin
                If ((SearchArray(aMsgs[i].iErrorNo, aiSeen)) = -1) Begin
                    Move aMsgs[i].iErrorNo to aiSeen[SizeOfArray(aiSeen)]
                    Move (aMsgs[i].bError = False) to abWarn[SizeOfArray(abWarn)]
                End
            End
        Loop

        /* Then the stored table, by the SAME whole-table walk LoadRecipes uses (index 1). A keyed
           Find on the message number would be the obvious move and is the wrong one here: several
           recipes share a number and are told apart by MatchText, so the walk compares BOTH - and it
           reuses the one access path this table is already known to support rather than assuming an
           index exists. ~120 rows, and only on the analyze that actually learned something. */
        Move 0 to i
        Clear ModAnalyzer
        Find Gt ModAnalyzer by 1
        While (Found)
            Move (SearchArray(ModAnalyzer.MsgNumber, aiSeen)) to iAt
            If (iAt <> -1) Begin
                Move abWarn[iAt] to bWarn
                If ((ModAnalyzer.IsWarning <> 0) <> bWarn) Begin
                    /* Reread/Unlock, NOT a bare SaveRecord (Nils 2026-08-03, error 4155 "Edit
                       requires reread or find during lock" - it aborted the whole analyze on the
                       first row that disagreed). Updating an EXISTING record needs the record
                       re-read under lock first; the seed pass above gets away with a bare
                       SaveRecord only because GlobalAutoCreateNewID clears the buffer and makes a
                       NEW row, which carries no such requirement.

                       The Move sits INSIDE the Reread on purpose: Reread refetches the record from
                       disk, so a field set before it would be thrown away.

                       Per row rather than one transaction around the whole walk: only the rows that
                       actually disagree are written (usually a handful), and a lock held across
                       ~120 rows would be the worse trade. Safe inside the walk because index 1 is
                       ID alone - IsWarning is not a segment of it, so rewriting it cannot re-order
                       the records under the Find Gt that follows. */
                    Reread ModAnalyzer
                        Move (If(bWarn, 1, 0)) to ModAnalyzer.IsWarning
                        SaveRecord ModAnalyzer
                    Unlock
                End
            End
            Find Gt ModAnalyzer by 1
        Loop
        Function_Return iChanged
    End_Function

End_Class
